1779266	202012	1801-2400	ZZZ.TRACON	US				7000		IMC	Rain; Turbulence		Night				Air Taxi	Medium Transport		2	Part 135	IFR	Passenger		Climb	Vectors	Class A ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Fractional	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Commercial; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 160; Flight Crew Total 1470; Flight Crew Type 500		Time Pressure; Distraction; Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1779266												ATC Issue All Types; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural FAR; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew		Flight Crew Returned To Clearance	Human Factors; ATC Equipment / Nav Facility / Buildings; Environment - Non Weather Related	Human Factors	We were delayed yesterday because of ATC issues. By the time we took off it was dark and we had been up for 12 hours. As we flew east off the coast of State X we encountered moderate turbulence and precipitation. The Captain got distracted and as we leveled off at 7,000 feet his speed began to increase. I called out 'Speed' and he did not acknowledge so again I said 'Watch your speed.' By this time we were doing around 275 knots and I don't why but he thought we should continue the climb so he used the autopilot to begin to climb the plane. I immediately told him to maintain 7,000 and reduce his speed. The initiated climb resulted in an altitude gain of 400 feet and the Controller told us to maintain 7,000 feet. We returned to 7,000 with no other issues and slowed. I was told our delay was a result of staffing issues at ZZZ center because of COVID-19. This is by far the longest ATC delay I have experienced and I definitely know fatigue was a factor hear. Do I think we were unsafe? No, I do not. That being said I don't think we were the tip of the spear that late in the day. ATC was super busy on the radios and we tried calling several times to request higher to get out of the weather we were in to no avail. Under staffing, busy airspace, delays, fatigue, and weather all combined would be the problem in my opinion. As well as pressure to get the job done.				First Officer reported a speed and altitude deviation during departure and cited fatigue from a lengthy ATC COVID-19 related delay as contributing factor.	
1779383	202012	0601-1200	ZZZ.TRACON	US				2500		IMC	Rain; Turbulence					TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch		Class B ZZZ																								VHF		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 86; Flight Crew Total 14000; Flight Crew Type 666		Workload; Troubleshooting; Communication Breakdown; Human-Machine Interface	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1779383												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	After Takeoff on our climb up 2,500 ft. out of ZZZ, we lost the use of both VHF radios com1 and com2. We leveled off at 2,500 ft. worked the problem and the Captain was able to get com1 back by using the ALT switch. All the communications on the First Officer side of the plane had died I could not use the hot mic or communicate with ATC on my side I tired different headsets and nothing worked. We then decided to return to ZZZ since we were going out over the water on our way to ZZZ1. WE had to burn 14,000 lbs. of fuel to get under max landing weight. We ended up holding over ZZZ VOR since the weather in the area was raining and turbulent and ZZZ VOR was in the clear and smooth. We worked the Flight Attendant, Dispatch, Maintenance Control, and ATC, till we had burned off enough fuel to return to land in ZZZ. We kept the passengers informed of what was going on as much as we could during the entire event.				Air carrier First Officer reported that both VHF radios stopped working during departure, resulting in a diversion.	
1779578	202012	0001-0600	CID.Airport	IA				2000		IMC	Icing; Snow; Turbulence; 3		Night	1200		CTAF CID; Center ZAU	Air Taxi	Small Transport		1	Part 135	IFR	Cargo / Freight / Delivery		Landing	Vectors																									Air/Ground Communication		X	Improperly Operated																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Taxi	Single Pilot; Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Commercial; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 100; Flight Crew Total 2100; Flight Crew Type 500		Workload; Communication Breakdown; Situational Awareness	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Ground Personnel		1779578												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Conflict Ground Conflict, Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence	Horizontal 0; Vertical 1000				Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Human Factors; Airport; Aircraft; Weather	Ambiguous	I was on a instrument approach to CID talking with Chicago Center and picking up ice. Chicago asked me to cancel IFR clearance as early as possible because a (Company) plane was landing behind me. I changed to the CTAF frequency and made a 10 miles out announcement. I broke out around 2,000 ft msl/1,200 ft. AGL and change back to Chicago Center and cancelled my IFR clearance with ATC. I switched the comm back to CTAF and continued the approach, however; the button push failed to change to CTAF and stayed on Chicago Center and I missed that. During the approach I was stunned to see a vehicle pull out onto the approach end of the runway and stop at the very end just beyond the runway end lighting. I was still approximately 1-1 1/2 miles out. I had to make a decision to land or go back up into the icing. As the runway was 8,000 ft. long I elected to extend and land in the 2nd half of the runway as opposed to risk picking up excessive ice and endangering my aircraft.				Pilot was flying instrument approach after having ice build up on aircraft. During landing a vehicle entered the  runway. Instead of going around, pilot chose to land long instead of returning to icing conditions.	
1780476	202012	1201-1800	ZZZ.TRACON	US	134	3		3000		VMC	Turbulence; 10		Daylight	6000		TRACON ZZZ	Personal	Small Aircraft, Low Wing, 1 Eng, Fixed Gear		2	Part 91	None	Training		Climb	Direct	Class E ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X		Personal	Pilot Flying; Single Pilot	Flight Crew Commercial; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 24.1; Flight Crew Total 2574; Flight Crew Type 1675		Distraction; Training / Qualification			1780476												Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Returned To Clearance	Environment - Non Weather Related; Weather; Human Factors	Environment - Non Weather Related	I was using a safety pilot and view limiting device to log instrument approaches to regain IFR currency. I was on climbout/missed approach from a low approach to a practice ILS X at ZZZ. I was told to by the Tower to 'proceed on course' after having previously told the Approach Controller I was headed next to ZZZ1 for the next approach. I was switched to ZZZ Approach at about 1,800 feet MSL on climbout and reported 1,900 feet for 3,000 feet direct ZZZ1. I was told 'Radar contact' and nothing else specifically.On climbout I was trying to set up my Garmin 430W for the next approach, and get a new program on the iPad (Foreflight vs. Garmim pilot I had previously used) to post the selected approach for ZZZ1 on the map. I had trouble with my glasses fogging up due to wearing a COVID mask in the plane and was experiencing updrafts/turbulence. The time to 3,000 feet was shorter than expected and due to the above distractions, I did not notice I was passing through 3,000 feet until I hit 3,250 feet at which point I reduced power and pushed the nose down. I was back to 3,000 feet within 15 seconds. I heard nothing from ATC regarding this, probably due to being VFR and no altitude restriction issued by ATC. I was able to verify all audio and events/altitudes due to a GoPro running to capture the flight.While I was VFR and there was no traffic nearby, if this was IFR, I would have had the same challenges and it would most likely been an altitude bust. There needs to be the same discipline applied to 'practice' instrument work as there is during the real thing in IMC, or the bad habits and distractions you allow during practice will seep into operations in general. I should have left the gizmo programming for level flight and popped the glasses off if the mask induced fogging was a distraction. I should have made the instrument scan primary rather than assuming I had more time than I actually had to program while getting to 3,000 feet.				GA pilot with a safety pilot reported an altitude deviation during climbout.  The pilot cited distraction from goggles fogging up and turbulence was a contributing factor.	
1781326	202012	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US	320	2		3000		IMC	Turbulence; Rain; 2.5		Night	600		Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	Caravan Undifferentiated		2	Part 135	IFR	Passenger		Initial Climb		Class E ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Commercial; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 200; Flight Crew Total 3800; Flight Crew Type 2600		Training / Qualification; Human-Machine Interface; Situational Awareness			1781326												Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance	Human Factors	Human Factors	I was pilot monitoring on the initial climbout from ZZZ when I noticed my FO (First Officer) was nose high and banking to the right approximately 40 degrees with airspeed decreasing from 95 KTS to 85 KTS. I asked why he was turning as were supposed to be climbing left to ZZZZZ. He seemed confused by my question and I realized that he had lost situational awareness. I declared 'My Plane' at which point he gave a positive transfer of controls. I leveled the wings and regained a positive rate of climb before turning back onto course. After regaining stable flight I offered the controls back to my FO. He refused controls and I continued to fly. I told him he had the radios and told him to check in with departure. Center asked us why we had turned to the right off of our expected heading and my FO was honest with the Controller, informing him that he had lost control of the aircraft and heading had diverged prior to me regaining control of the aircraft. The Controller acknowledged and told us to climb according to our planned route. After reaching cruising altitude I debriefed my FO and we discussed what went wrong. We ultimately determined that the loss of control was due to deficiencies in his scan as a result of his inexperience with analog instruments.				Air Carrier Captain reported a recovery from unusual attitude and a track heading deviation during initial climb.	
1781501	202101	1201-1800	MRI.Airport	AK			0			VMC	Turbulence; Windshear; 10		Dusk	7000		Tower MRI	FBO	Small Aircraft, Low Wing, 2 Eng, Retractable Gear		2	Part 91	IFR	Ferry / Re-Positioning; Other instructional		Landing	Visual Approach																																																																Aircraft X	Flight Deck	FBO	Single Pilot; Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Commercial	Air Traffic Control Non Radar 2; Air Traffic Control Radar 2; Flight Crew Last 90 Days 90; Flight Crew Total 2400; Flight Crew Type 17.8					1781501	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	FBO	Instructor; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Flight Instructor; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 45; Flight Crew Total 2600; Flight Crew Type 640					1781522	Ground Event / Encounter Ground Strike - Aircraft; Ground Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Ground Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence; Ground Excursion Runway			Person Flight Crew	Taxi	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Weather	Weather	After landing I maintained center line; braking action after landing I noticed was nil. The plane was near stopping and had started to veer to the right, at the time in which I input left rudder to counter the right turn and no avail the aircraft began to slide sideways. After attempts were made to completely stop, the engines were shut down to prevent further damage as it slid into the snowbank to which after coming to a complete stop, ATC was notified once we were allowed to exit the aircraft noticed the runway was icy.		Upon touchdown on runway 25 at Merrill airport it was very apparent that there was NIL braking action available. Tower informed us prior to landing that there was no braking action reports. As we were decelerating through approximately 35-40 knots ground speed, the student in the left seat (pilot flying) commented that we had no brakes. I immediately placed my feet on the rudder pedals with him and applied brakes. There was no braking available. I released pressure from the brakes to aid in steering and asked the student to cease braking. My intentions at this time was to allow the aircraft to decelerate using aerodynamic braking and roll to the end of the runway. Shortly thereafter the aircraft began a slide to the right. I immediately applied full left rudder without any action. I then applied left brake to aid in the directional control, which also did not assist. My last resort was to increase power on the right engine to attempt to correct the aircraft towards center line, but at this point the aircraft was too far gone in the slide. As it was apparent the aircraft was going to exit the runway I immediately pulled both mixtures to the idle cutoff position in an effort to stop the props from rotating and causing any damage. The aircraft ended up approximately 110 degrees rotated to the right of the center of the runway and the aircraft came to a rest approximately 5-10 feet off of the runway edge in approximately 6 inch deep snow. At this point I informed the tower of our position and we would need assistance to get the aircraft back on the runway. I called the owner of [the FBO and he was able to remove and tow the aircraft without further incident. After further inspection it was determined that there was absolutely no damage to the aircraft or any airport property, including the runway edge lights. The aircraft has since been turned over to Angel Aviation maintenance for further inspections.		Small aircraft flight crew reported braking action NIL on landing resulted in loss of control and runway excursion.	
1782545	202101	0601-1200	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				38000		VMC	Turbulence						Air Carrier	B737 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 69; Flight Crew Total 10301; Flight Crew Type 2994		Physiological - Other			1782545												Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; General Physical Injury / Incapacitation	Weather	Weather	During cruise flight attendants were seated by Captain due to continuous light chop with reports of moderate chop ahead. After close to an hour of flight attendants being seated, we experienced a momentary encounter with moderate mountain wave turbulence where the aircraft banked about 20 degrees and airspeed increased 10 to 15 kts. Event lasted about 10 seconds. Pilot spoke with FAs (Flight Attendants) and they all said they were fine as well as all passengers. Everyone was seated during the turbulence encounter. Later in the flight a FA said her neck had some pain and she thought it was due to the turbulence encounter earlier. She said she was ok to perform her duties for the remainder of the flight and for her return flight. Dispatch and the station were notified, and a FA supervisor met the aircraft to discuss the incident/injury with the flight attendant.				Air carrier First Officer reported that an encounter with mountain wave turbulence cause a brief loss of control and resulted in a Flight Attendant injury.	
1782608	202101	1801-2400	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				36000		IMC	Turbulence					Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B757 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR	Cargo / Freight / Delivery		Cruise		Class A ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1782608												Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Weather	Weather	ATC advised of SIGMET R with reports of moderate turbulence. As we approached the forecast area of turbulence we slowed to turbulence penetration speed of .78 MACH at FL360. After a few minutes of mostly moderate turbulence with significant mountain wave turbulence... we abruptly encountered approximately 10 seconds of severe turbulence resulting in simultaneous 5 to 10 degrees roll variations, +/-10 knots airspeed variations, 5 degrees pitch variations and over 1000 fpm down resulting in approximately 100 ft. below altitude. Autopilot recover aircraft to level flight. No injuries or damage noted. Reported to ATC. A few minutes following our PIREP, another aircraft report severe turbulence. Clear Air Turbulence associated with mountain wave.Suggest better forecasting. Uber conservative routing around forecast turbulence.				Air carrier Captain reported they encountered severe turbulence associated with mountain wave which caused roll variations and altitude and airspeed deviations.	
1782929	202101	0001-0600	ZZZ.Airport	US			0			VMC	Turbulence					Tower ZZZ		MD-11		2					Takeoff / Launch																										Turbine Engine		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck		Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1782929												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Rejected Takeoff; Flight Crew Returned To Gate; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	Departing [Runway] XXL, winds 280/12G19. Rolling takeoff. Slight difficulty setting throttles for takeoff, as #2 did not want to go above 95%. At 80 knots, received Engine Fail light on glareshield. RTO (Rejected Takeoff) at approx 90 knots with #2 engine at 95% and decreasing.  Cleared runway. Complied with QRH. Block turn back. Returned to different gate for transload and relaunch.  Debriefed Maintenance.				MD-11 Captain reported an engine failure on takeoff roll.	
1783064	202101	1801-2400	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				39500		VMC	Turbulence; Windshear					Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class A ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 93; Flight Crew Total 3400; Flight Crew Type 3400		Troubleshooting			1783064	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 40; Flight Crew Total 1821; Flight Crew Type 1821		Troubleshooting			1783065	Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overrode Automation; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; General Maintenance Action	Weather	Weather	Enroute from ZZZ to ZZZ1 at approximately ZZZZZ, we encountered chop at FL350 and decided to request FL390, which was granted. During the climb at approximately FL370 we encountered moderate turbulence. The airspeed increased into overspeed and the vertical speed increased significantly due to an updraft. Though I did not note the actual VS, I estimate at least 5,000 FPM based on the rate that the altitude tape was moving, which was where my focus was. I disconnected the autopilot, and as we went through FL380 I knew leveling at FL390 was going to be difficult because I couldn't safely decrease thrust any further, and pitching over would have made the overspeed worse. Using a little of each method, I was able to achieve a normal thrust setting and speed below max at approximately FL395 and returned to FL390, where we reported the event to ATC. We sent an overspeed code to Maintenance via ACARS. The sky conditions at the time were [VFR].		During climbout through FL370 we encountered an increase in both speed and climb while the B autopilot was engaged. After the PF (Pilot Flying) disengaged the autopilot the aircraft continued to increase in speed causing an over speed condition by approximately 10-15 kts. The aircraft continued to climb through our selected altitude, FL390, by approximately 400 ft. while the pilot flying was executing his recovery techniques to return the aircraft by to the correct altitude and speed. Approximately FL350 I had already asked the flight attendants to be seated because we had a small amount of turbulence. During the event, both the passengers and flight attendants were seated. I relayed the turbulence/updraft to air traffic control as severe in nature so that other aircraft wouldn't be routed through the area. After the event both the PF and myself felt that is was probably more of a moderate turbulence and more of a severe updraft. Shortly after, I checked on both the flight attendants and passengers to ensure no one was injured. The event was not jerky or real rough, it was more of a pretty smooth continual pitch up that was very difficult to stop without really over speeding the aircraft more than we did. I felt like the PF did as good of a good as possible in this strange situation.		B737 flight crew reported an encounter with turbulence and an updraft resulted in a temporary loss of control and overspeed condition.	
1784672	201904	0001-0600	LAX.Airport	CA				2500		VMC	Turbulence		Daylight			TRACON SCT	Fractional	Gulfstream IV / G350 / G450		2	Part 135	IFR	Passenger		Final Approach		Class B LAX																												TRACON SCT	Air Carrier	MD-11		2	Part 121	IFR	Cargo / Freight / Delivery		Final Approach		Class B LAX																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Fractional	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1784672	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Fractional	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Situational Awareness			1784671	Conflict Airborne Conflict; Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Air Traffic Control Issued Advisory / Alert; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Procedure; Environment - Non Weather Related	Environment - Non Weather Related	On a flight to LAX, after being cleared for the approach for Runway 25L around 9 DME our aircraft was snapped hard to the right due to wake turbulence from a preceding MD11. Continuous turbulence, crew climbed above glide path & a dot off of localizer to the right primarily due to the direction the wake snapped us. A B-737 was on the approach for [Runway] 25R. They were in sight the entire time. Crew corrected back on course. Crew stayed high on glide slope, at 100 ft. some turbulence was encountered. There was no wind during the entire approach.		On approach (estimate 2500 ft. MSL) above clouds we encountered wake turbulence from a 'heavy' aircraft landing ahead of us. Aircraft was properly configured for landing and on profile. Wings rocked left and then right by 15 degrees or so, but rapidly. I recall that we exited the turbulence in a right bank and held a course perhaps 5 to 10 degrees right of course as we both acknowledged verbally what had happened. I called Tower and advised them we had experienced wake turbulence. ATC responded by asking our intentions and I responded that we were deviating right. ATC responded not to deviate right. Both PF (Pilot Flying) and I saw an aircraft to our right, presumably on parallel approach. We maintained visual separation from it and the PF returned to the LOC centerline, now maintaining a profile slightly higher than GS. We continued the approach and landed without further incident. Our flight up to this point had been characterized by light to moderate turbulence along the majority of our route, resulting in many altitude changes in attempt to find smoother air for our passengers. The flight itself was fatiguing.I have to admit that I was anticipating the end of the flight and was not properly considering contingencies for wake turbulence. Proper SA (Situational Awareness) would have led me to help the PF out by suggesting that he climb, or deviate left rather than right. I also think that we as a crew need to include wake turbulence in any approach briefing at airports where heavies are operating, and where parallel approaches are in progress.		GIV flight crew reported encountering wake turbulence from preceding MD-11 on approach to LAX.	
1784716	202101	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US				15		Marginal	Fog; Snow; Turbulence		Daylight	2000			Corporate	PC-12		1	Part 91	IFR	Passenger		Initial Climb	SID Y	Class C ZZZ																								Elevator Trim System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Corporate	Pilot Flying; Single Pilot; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Flight Instructor; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 15; Flight Crew Total 1856; Flight Crew Type 20					1784716												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Ground Event / Encounter Fuel Issue	Horizontal 10; Vertical 300		Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Procedure; Aircraft	Aircraft	I was given the SID and cleared to FL150. During the initial climb, I had a Trim Runaway and I tried resetting the autopilot but it happened again. In terms of my GPS issues it was not responding to my inputs. So by me dealing with that and I got behind the aircraft and violated my altitude and heading. On the climb on the departure I believe I had asked ATC for Direct to Destination but I was given a heading instead. On the trim runaway it pitches the nose of the aircraft down, so I had to disconnect the autopilot etc. And long with trying to handle the trim runaway and disconnecting the autopilot and I made the deviations. Also, while being vectored to ZZZ, Center notified me that the weather had dropped there and there were airplanes holding so at that point I decided to go to ZZZ1. However, I was being vectored around a bit, so at that point I decided to land at ZZZ2 as it was right of me about 10 miles. So as to obtain fuel, as I had to take off with minimum fuel as possible in order to meet the climb gradients. My apologies. I should have notified ATC of my Trim issue and GPS issues sooner. This was my first time flying into ZZZ3. In terms of learning from this incident - I should have had another Pilot with me instead of Single Pilot operation into a challenging airport such as ZZZ3.				PC12 pilot reported diverting due to a trim runaway and weather falling below minimums at destination airport.	
1784981	202101	0601-1200	ZZZ.ARTCC	US							Turbulence					Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	A319			Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise; Descent; Climb		Class A ZZZ; Class E ZZZ																								Interphone System		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument			Distraction; Confusion; Communication Breakdown; Time Pressure	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Attendant		1784981	Aircraft X		Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Time Pressure; Distraction; Confusion; Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Attendant		1784998	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Other / Unknown; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Attendant	In-flight	General Maintenance Action	Procedure; Weather; Aircraft	Aircraft	We departed on time for a XX minute flight. Climbing through 10,000 feet I chimed the flight attendants and we continued our climb to 21,000 feet. Center was telling me to expect bad rides all the way to our destination. So when we leveled off at 21,000 feet. I made an announcement stating that the ride was going to be rough and that I would keep the seat belt sign of the entire flight. I also asked the flight attendants to remain seated for the duration of the flight as well or so I thought. The ride was not good. Continuous light occasionally moderate turbulence. The arrival is challenging on a good day and today was no exception. We were dealing with a complex arrival, turbulence, icing and multiple changes to speed and altitude. We were cleared to descend via the arrival with no speed restrictions with about a 90 knot tailwind.We would be early. Around the time that we started our descent I noticed that someone was trying to use the door code to gain access to the cockpit. I immediately declined it and made sure door was locked. The Pilot Flying and I both thought that was strange so I tried calling back to a Flight Attendant to see what was going on. That was the first indication something was wrong. When I turned on the speaker for the cabin I could hear an audible cycling noise. When I tried to chime the flight attendants I couldn't hear the chime and I could not hear any voices on the interphone. I tried to make a quick announcement but nothing was coming out back in the cabin. As the Pilot Flying and I were extremely busy managing the descent and approach we heard a knock at the door and could faintly hear a voice. Pilot Flying turned around and saw a note on the ground that said INTERPHONE INOP.I wanted to get up and grab the note to try and write something back but we were too busy. A few minutes passed. The cockpit door lock code was entered again and I denied it a second time. I tried to yell as loud as I could that we got the message. I think they must have heard that time because they stopped knocking on the door. We landed and began our taxi to the gate. After clearing the active runways I had Pilot Flying call maintenance and inform them that our Cabin Interphone and Public Address system was inoperative. We taxied to the gate and shutdown. After the shutdown checklist was complete and before the forward door was opened I got up to check on the A Flight Attendant and see if everything was OK. He was visibly upset. After the passengers got off the other two Flight Attendant's joined us up in first class to discuss what had happened. A Flight Attendant said that it was the scariest flight of his life. He said that after we had hit a rough patch of turbulence the cabin lights in the plane started to cycle on and off. They were still doing it after I went back to talk to them. He then had tried to call me to see if everything was OK but couldn't reach me. He thought that maybe we had lost pressurization and that we might have been incapacitated. He tried the door code to maybe get a peek inside to see if we were OK. He left a note under the door in hopes that we would grab it and write back. Only after the last time that he tried the door lock and I denied it did he hear me yell through the door. The other two flight attendants were also upset that they couldn't communicate with me. I spent at least 15 minutes with them trying to explain why I couldn't respond to them even though I wanted to.Trying to unlock the cockpit door without my authorization put me on high alert. This is not something I expected. If this had been a different flight with more time I would have taken their note and responded to it and sent it back. That would have been the best way. I tried yelling through the door, but it is loud in the cockpit and only on the second time did they hear me. If we had lost pressurization the masks in the back would have dropped and they would have felt it in their ears. The first time I denied his access to the cockpit door he should have known we were OK. I cannot just open the cockpit door without knowing who is behind it. My first priority is to fly the aircraft. I just didn't have enough time to respond to them the way they wanted me to.I tried to address all of their concerns and I did understand that they were upset. I wrote up the Public Address system as well as the Interphone system with their input to give the mechanics all they needed to fix the system. Maintenance came on board and reset the system. I tested the PA and both forward and aft intercoms with the Flight Attendants and in front of maintenance to make sure everything worked. Maintenance signed off the system and we than headed back. After our initial conversation about what had transpired I thought all Flight Attendants were OK just a little rattled but good enough to continue. If I had known they still had reservations about what had happened I would have immediately responded to them. My crew always comes first.		During our descent on the arrival we heard a knock on the cockpit door. We turned around and saw a note that was slid under the door stating that the interphone was inoperative and there were lights flashing in the cabin. We heard the Flight Attendant said that he was trying to get a hold of us. We never heard him prior to that as this was a 30 minutes or so flight and we were busy from wheels up. The Captain told him we were landing soon. When we landed Maintenance was contacted. Upon arrival at the gate the Captain met with the flight attendants to discuss the event. I went to do the walkaround. The mechanic came and fixed the problem. The interphone and Public Address system was tested before we left the gate and they worked.		A319 flight crew reported while experiencing turbulence they were initially unaware their PA System and interphone warnings to the cabin were not heard due to the system malfunctioning resulting in frightened and angry flight attendants.	
1785430	202101	0601-1200	SCT.TRACON	CA				14000		IMC	Rain; Icing; Cloudy; Turbulence		Daylight			TRACON SCT	Air Carrier	MD-11		2	Part 121	IFR	Cargo / Freight / Delivery	FMS Or FMC	Parked; Climb	Vectors																									Autopilot		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1785430	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1785431	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Maintenance; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft; Procedure	Procedure	During moderate turbulence and deviation around weather, airspeed became wildly erratic. Between maximum and minimum, the autopilot disconnected and several Level Two alerts displayed. I called, 'Airspeed unreliable,' disconnected AFS Override and Flight Director Switches, and established pitch for aircraft stabilized flight. The First Officer's airspeed was the closest to the Standby Instruments, and transfer of control was given to the FO (First Officer). Level Two 'SEL FADEC 1,2 3 ALTN'; 'SEL FLAP LIM OVRD' and 'SEL ELV FEEL MAN' were displayed. The QRH procedures were completed and the flight continued without incident. Possible CADC #1 failure or pitot static malfunction. [Responded as] described above. Additionally, inspection of the logbook revealed that related malfunctions had occurred [previously on four separate dates]. This would suggest that the problem is ongoing and requires further action. [I suggest] maintenance troubleshooting of all related issues by reference to all previous write-ups.		Airspeed unreliable event. Possible altitude deviation. I was deadheading on flight sitting in the observer seat. There were thunderstorms in vicinity and we were experiencing light to moderate rain and turbulence. CA (Captain) was PF (Pilot Flying) with autopilot engaged in climb to 14,000 MSL and also communicating to ATC on Com 1. FO (First Officer) was speaking to Operations on Com 2. I'm not sure exactly what altitude event occurred but happened around 14,000 MSL. There was a high speed warning and I noticed a large difference in airspeed between the CA and FO side. The CA-side airspeed was declining rapidly while FO-side was overspeeding slightly. The Captain recognized the issue and performed the memory items correctly and stabilized the aircraft. It was determined the FO-side was correct and the aircraft control was transferred to FO. The QRH was performed and CADC 2 was selected. There was no further incident. After the event we discussed the possibility of an altitude deviation during the recovery. There was a level off and delayed climb. I did not have a headset on and could not hear any altitude clearances or ATC communication. ATC did not inform the crew of any deviation. The crew handled the emergency well.[Responded by having an] in-depth discussion among the crew about the event and how to handle it better. Reports. Captain discussed event with Maintenance and entered it into logbook. In our event debrief it was discovered that this event has happened multiple times in this aircraft. The FO and another Deadheader both had separate airspeed unreliable events in the same aircraft in similar meteorological conditions. We read through the logbook to discover that this has happened multiple times recently and the aircraft has continued to be put back into service with no permanent resolution. The principle recommendation would be to correctly resolve the issue that is causing this. As for my part in this event, I realized I could have been more active in listening if I were using a headset. I could have verified the ATC clearance and assisted in communication.		MD-11 flight crew reported turbulence and an airspeed disparity between the instruments disconnected the autopilot and resulted in a possible altitude deviation.	
1786024	202101	1801-2400	ZZZ1.Airport	US						IMC	Icing; Turbulence		Night			Center ZZZ1	Air Carrier	EMB ERJ 145 ER/LR		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb	Vectors	Class A ZZZ1																								Pneumatic Valve/Bleed Valve		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1786024												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence		N	Automation Aircraft Other Automation; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	On the climb out from ZZZ on the SID we got direct ZZZZZ. On the climb in the clouds the ice protection came on normally. Within a couple minutes, the anti icing fail warning came on. There after the Bleed 2 Valve Fail also came on. We pulled out the QRH and ran the checklist for the events. Sometime above 15,000 ft. and while still running the QRH, the cabin pressure was being lost at around FL200, the oxygen masks deployed in the back. We also put our oxygen masks on in the cockpit. Notified ATC that moment [received priority handling] for the nearest airport due to loss of cabin pressure. ATC directed the flight to ZZZ1 being the closet. We diverted to ZZZ1. Ran all checklist for descent and landing to ZZZ1. Landed safe and taxied to the gate. Completed the after landing and parking checklist upon arrival. Passengers deplaned at the gate. EICAS alerted. Anti-icing, Bleed 2 Valve Fail and loss of cabin pressure.Diverted Due to the nature of the events, ZZZ might have been a better return option.				EMB 145 Pilot reported loss of engine bleed air caused loss of cabin pressure and anti ice protection, resulting in an emergency descent and a diversion.	
1786030	202101	1801-2400	ZZZ.TRACON	US				15000		IMC	Turbulence		Night				Air Carrier	EMB ERJ 145 ER/LR		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class A ZZZ																								Pneumatic Valve/Bleed Valve		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 100; Flight Crew Total 28000; Flight Crew Type 2400					1786030												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence		N	Automation Aircraft Other Automation; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Climbing north on the SID we entered icing and turbulence. The anti icing turned on normally. After a couple minutes an EICAS massage, #2 Bleed Fail followed by a couple more anti ice failures. We started the QRH to deal with the bleed failure. I hoped we would soon climb out of icing conditions. By now we were approaching 10,000 ft. and cleared to FL230. Before finishing the bleed failure QRH, approximately 15,000 ft. we started loosing cabin pressure. We reached FL220 till we leveled off [received priority handling]. We used our crew O2 masks and the cabin oxygen deployed. Also started a decent and with ATCs suggestion and help we diverted to ZZZ1 and landed.Looking back it would have been better to return to ZZZ.				Loss of Engine Bleed Air resulted in an emergency descent caused by loss of cabin pressure and anti ice protection, requiring a diversion.	
1786175	202102	0001-0600	ZDV.ARTCC	CO				14000			Snow; Turbulence; Icing					Center ZDV		Small Transport				IFR			Initial Approach; Descent	Other Instrument Approach	Class E ZDV																																																															Aircraft X; Facility ZDV.ARTCC			Enroute	Air Traffic Control Fully Certified			Communication Breakdown; Situational Awareness	Party1 ATC; Party2 Flight Crew		1786175												ATC Issue All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Automation Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Human Factors; Weather	Human Factors	Aircraft X was originally going to land at ZZZ, however, 2 new NOTAMs entered the system just before starting an instrument approach regarding standing water and ice on Runway 20 and the taxiways. Due to the wind and previous icing encountered, he decided to not land at ZZZ. I put him into the published holding at JADGU at 120. We discussed his options for many other close by airports, but due to forecast and known icing and moderate to severe turbulence throughout the area we were having a hard time coming up with a good airport. Aircraft X was also not fuel critical, but did not have a lot of reserve fuel. During this, I was working departures and arrivals into/out of DEN with moderate turbulence. I advised Aircraft X that PUB was 60 miles northeast, and had VFR weather with no known icing. I advised him that he would have to climb to 160, but only for a few minutes and they could descend to 140. Aircraft X decided to go to PUB airport. I climbed Aircraft X to 160 and he asked if it was about a 050 heading. I concurred, but he needed to climb first due to terrain before I could turn him there. He accepted the climb and then asked again about the heading. I told him to remain in the published hold and climb to 160 and I would have the turn on course above 130. He advised me that he was already turning to a 050 heading but was climbing to 130. At this point the MSAW alert was going off, but he was through130 in the 130 MIA. I reiterated the climb to 160 and told him the terrain climbed rapidly and to expedite as much as he could. At this point, Aircraft X was climbing through 140 heading into the 160 MIA. I advised Aircraft X the minimum IFR altitude is 160 and to report reaching. I should have issued a heading south as soon as he advised me he was already turning to PUB. I know the highest mountains in that area are not above 140, but the terrain climbs so rapidly in that area I should have known he would not have topped the 160 MIA even though he did climb above the 130 MIA. He also reported turbulence and icing at this time.The pilot should not have exited the published holding without a clearance to do so. I should have issued a south heading as soon as he told me he was turning out of hold. It was poor service on my end. I know all of the moderate reports and constant transmitter changes did not help the situation.				ZDV Center Controller reported an aircraft experienced a CFTT event while diverting due to weather.	
1786404	202102		ZZZ.ARTCC	US				40000			Turbulence					Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B787 Dreamliner Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 141.83; Flight Crew Total 11102.03; Flight Crew Type 1016.48					1786404												Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Automation Aircraft Other Automation; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew FLC complied w / Automation / Advisory; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Human Factors; Weather	Weather	During cruse at FL400 mountain wave was encountered.  The airspeed exceeded Vmo/Mmo with accompany EICAS alert, Master Caution Alert and audible alert.  I was the pilot flying and the FO was Pilot monitoring.  The FO extended the speed breaks to full extend and reduced the throttles immediately to idle.  The aircraft descended 500 feet.  The airspeed reduced below Vmo/Mmo, the warnings stopped.  The FO retracted the speed breaks and the autothrottles increased to maintain VNAV programed speed.  This all occurred within 10-20 seconds.  I was attempting to determine what was happening as the FO accomplished the above items.  ATC was notified of the mountain wave and altitude excursion.We briefed the mountain waive and actions which were taken.  I reviewed the Flight Manual regarding high altitude overspeed situations.  It was determined that the actions taken deviated from FM directed guidance.  We reviewed CRM items on what went right, what went wrong and what we would do in the future.  It was a great discussion.It became apparent to me that the 4 hour departure delay and all the associated tasks had taken a toll on my ability to process information in a timely manner.  I was slow to react to the overspeed.  As a result, the FO used CRM skill of leadership to deploy the speed break and reduce the throttles.  We had a conversation about the FO acting too quickly and me not acting quickly enough.  Maintenance Control was contact to determine what items needed to be included in the report regarding the overspeed.  An electronic logbook write up was submitted.				Air carrier Captain reported that an airspeed exceedance and altitude excursion occurred when the aircraft encountered a mountain wave.	
1786540	202101	1801-2400	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				27000		Mixed	Turbulence		Daylight			Center ZZZ	Corporate	Falcon 900			Part 91	IFR	Passenger		Cruise	Direct	Class A ZZZ																								Fuel System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Corporate	Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 15; Flight Crew Total 12200; Flight Crew Type 1000					1786540												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control			Automation Aircraft Other Automation; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew FLC complied w / Automation / Advisory; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance	Aircraft; Weather	Aircraft	Flight originated in ZZZ with ZZZ1 as destination, original planned cruise was FL230.Upon reaching cruise level we were in IMC and light to moderate turbulence, a request was made for higher cruise level.After leveling off at FL270 we were on top of the layer and smoother air; a few minutes later we had an indication that #3 fuel computer had failed and proceeded to follow POH procedures to attempt a reset, that required bringing the affected engine to idle before cycling the fuel computer control switch. While we were in the process to reset the computer there was an advisory message on the FMS regarding an out of trim condition, before attempting to re-trim the aircraft I disengaged the auto pilot to ascertain the condition, while re-trimming we started to encounter that the tops of the clouds below were in fact rising to our current cruising fl and encountered a sudden but brief increase in turbulence from nil to moderate so the aircraft was pushed upwards about 500 feet before I could correct it without a violent maneuver as we had passengers; ATC requested a confirmation of our current flight level and we advised that we were returning to FL270.The flight proceeded to ZZZ1 without further complication or comment from Controller.As the turbulence encounter occurred while the autopilot was disengaged and the sudden change in flying conditions (from smooth to moderate turbulence) Except for the use of more aggressive maneuvering the altitude excursion was in my opinion almost unavoidable.				Falcon 900 pilot reported that a fuel computer failed followed by an out of trim advisory message. They disengaged the autopilot to respond to the problems and encountered turbulence that caused an altitude excursion.	
1786675	202102	0601-1200	MDW.Airport	IL				1700			Turbulence; Snow; Icing; Windshear					Tower MDW	Air Carrier	Commercial Fixed Wing		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; Localizer/Glideslope/ILS Runway 31C	Final Approach; Landing		Class C MDW																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine			Situational Awareness; Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1786675												ATC Issue All Types; Ground Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Ground Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Human Factors; Weather	Weather	We operated Aircraft X from ZZZ to MDW, and the majority of the flight was uneventful. Mr. Y was my Captain and PF, and we had setup well in advance for the RNAV RNP Y approach for Runway 22L at MDW according to their latest ATIS. Later we received an ATIS update showing that the ILS for Runway 31C was active, so we re-configured and briefed that, still with plenty of time. We had discussed the possible need of diverting to ZZZ1 rather than our filed ALT1 of ZZZ2 and ALT2 of ZZZ3, but the visibility at MDW stayed above the minimums we needed for either the RNAV or ILS approaches to 22L/31C respectively.Fast forward to our first approach attempt: we're established on the ILS to 31C, fully configured well before the FAF and I was thinking we've got it made. We broke out at around 1,700 MSL and inflight visibility was not an issue and better than reported. From around 2,000 feet and down we experienced constant light and moderate turbulence, but no icing or precipitation. Captain had turned the autopilot and autothrottles off to help deal with the winds that were gusting from 260 degrees at 21 to 39 knots. We were also landing with stall protection ice speeds, adding 10 knots to our Vref and Vapp speeds.Everything was going great until somewhere between 1/2 to 1 mile from the approach end of 31C we got multiple 'WINDSHEAR' automated call outs from the airplane. Captain elected to go-around which we did, re-configuring as needed and I let MDW Tower know at around 400 feet AGL per the SOP. They gave us a heading and altitude to fly, asked us the reason for the go-around and handed us back off to Chicago Approach. We cleaned up the airplane and got vectored in for another ILS approach to 31C behind an Air Carrier Y.The second ILS approach was almost identical to the first, albeit with slightly less wind and turbulence. This time, even though we were ready for the conditions and knew more of what to expect, we managed to catch a gust of wind right in the flare and didn't touch down on Runway 31C until between the first third and the midpoint of the runway. Captain did a great job of thinking quick and performed a rejected landing, setting and calling 'TOGA' almost immediately after touchdown and off we went. We reconfigured again, got vectored around and handed off to Chicago Approach again and came around for a third attempt at Runway 31C.The third time Captain was able to land normally and well within the touchdown zone, but when he applied the brakes we found out that the '5/5/5' runway condition codes advertised for 31C were optimistic at best with the airplane noticeably sliding and skidding as we rolled out. We also noticed that there was blowing snow obscuring at least 50 percent of the runway surface. Captain advised MDW Tower of the braking action we experienced, calling it 'poor' and, to their credit, they immediately directed the broom and plow teams out to clear Runway 31C again. The rest of the flight was normal as we taxied into our gate.If there's anything I learned tonight, it's that just because you're on short final that doesn't mean you're going to land. Also: just because the Air Carrier Y flight that landed ahead of you reported 'braking action good' doesn't mean you will. I remember being on short final during our first ILS approach to 31C feeling complacent that we 'had it made' being on the ILS rather than RNAV RNP approach with its higher minimums and having broken out early at 1,700 feet. My go-around call outs also could have been crisper and more prompt, but that was mostly due to the 'startle' factor which was my own fault.My one suggestion would be for the airport operations crews at MDW to conduct their own braking action tests more often and with a more pessimistic eye. When the ATIS states RCCs are '5/5/5' the Air Carrier Y flight that landed ahead of you reports 'braking action good' and yet you experience significant wheel slip/skid due to icy runway surface conditions upon landing, thatindicates someone is being either dishonest or overly optimistic on their own braking action reports. If we or our Dispatch had known that the 'real' RCCs for 31C at MDW were actually 3/3/3 or even 2/2/2, we most likely would not have had the required landing performance numbers that we needed to land at MDW's shorter runways and would've gone to ZZZ1.				Air carrier First Officer reported having to go-around/missed approach several times due to weather at MDW airport.  When they finally landed the crew experienced some sliding and skidding due to braking action being 'poor' contrary to the reported 'good braking action.'	
1786726	202102	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US						VMC	Turbulence		Night			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B767-300 and 300 ER		2	Part 121	IFR	Cargo / Freight / Delivery		Cruise	Vectors	Class A ZZZ																								Hydraulic Main System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Check Pilot; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1786726												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Multiple Center Hydraulic EICAS messages resulting in a total loss of quantity and pressure in the in the Center Hydraulic System. [Reported situation] with ZZZ Center, complied with QRH procedures, alternately lowered the flaps and landing gear, landed Flaps 20 on ZZZ [Runway] XXC. Towed to gate.Center Hydraulic System failure. Upon landing, Maintenance found a hydraulic leak on right spoiler.				Pilot reported loss of hydraulic fluid resulted in a precautionary emergency landing.	
1786903	202102	1201-1800	HDO.Airport	TX						VMC	Turbulence; Windshear; 20		Daylight				Personal	Small Aircraft, Low Wing, 1 Eng, Retractable Gear		1	Part 91	None	Personal		Landing	Visual Approach	Class G ZHU																								Navigational Equipment and Processing			Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Personal	Single Pilot	Flight Crew Private; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 22; Flight Crew Total 730; Flight Crew Type 590		Human-Machine Interface; Situational Awareness; Confusion; Distraction			1786903												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Ground Excursion Runway; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Airport; Environment - Non Weather Related; ATC Equipment / Nav Facility / Buildings; Aircraft	Airport	Landing Runway 26 Using Visual approach guidance from GTN 650(Garmin Navigation/Display). No glideslope guidance was provided by the GTN. I crossed what appeared to be the threshold with very faded marking in significant turbulence. I taxied to the ramp without incident. Only after reviewing the track log from foreflight on google earth did I realize I may have actually landed on the displaced threshold. I believe better markings on the displaced threshold would help prevent this in the future. In addition, a more positive identification of runway markings on my part and a go around would have prevented this event. Contributing was a runway change from our planned arrival to Runway 35R due to changing winds.				A pilot reported they landed on the displaced threshold due to insufficient runway markings at HDO airport.	
1786917	202102	1201-1800	ZZZ.TRACON	US						IMC	Turbulence; Icing; Windshear; 8			2000		TRACON ZZZ	Personal	TBM 700/TBM 850		1	Part 91	IFR	Personal		Initial Approach	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Personal	Pilot Flying; Single Pilot	Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 13; Flight Crew Total 891; Flight Crew Type 91					1786917												Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Automation Aircraft RA; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Weather	Weather	On a flight from ZZZ to ZZZ1 in my TBM 700 (Aircraft X), I experienced moderate icing and severe turbulence and wind shear. On the approach to ZZZ1 moderate icing was reported to ATC which allowed me to descend to 2,000 ft. MSL. This got me out of IMC temporarily. I visually flew directly over ZZZ2. Shortly after I reentered IMC and experienced severe turbulence and wind shear. I had difficulty maintaining heading and altitude. It also caused me to be disoriented. I got heading and altitude warnings from ATC. I reported the severe turbulence and requested a vector twice. The second time registered and by then I was in moderate turbulence, icing and intermediate wind shear. ATC suggested an immediate landing at ZZZ2. I wanted no part of going close to whatever I had just passed thru and proceeded to ZZZ1. The airport came into view and I landed hot with one notch of flaps because of the wind shear and the fact that the plane was iced up. ATC was very helpful.				Pilot flying TBM 700 aircraft reported icing and severe turbulence on approach.	
1787396	202101	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US						IMC	Rain; Turbulence; Windshear; Fog; Icing; 6		Daylight	2000		Center ZZZ	Corporate	Light Transport		1	Part 91	IFR	Other Business		Takeoff / Launch	Direct																																																																Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Corporate	Single Pilot; Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Flight Instructor; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Commercial	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 30; Flight Crew Total 2500; Flight Crew Type 350		Distraction; Situational Awareness			1787396												Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural FAR					General None Reported / Taken	Environment - Non Weather Related; Manuals; Human Factors; Procedure	Human Factors	Based on unfamiliarity with the FAA's COVID relief policy regarding medical duration's, I acted as PIC between ZZZ and ZZZ1 on date in a type aircraft with an unknowingly expired 2nd class medical (issued date). The unfamiliarity came from reading headlines in several industry publication emails about the FAA extending medical certificate duration's during COVID.  I failed to follow up on these headlines to determine the extent of the extensions and therefore performed this flight under the assumption that medicals were extended indefinitely until COVID was over.I discovered my mistake after my coworker (in ground school) asked me about medical duration's in preparation for his private pilot written exam. I proceeded to search for his answer, and in the meantime recalled the COVID medical certification extension news.  Upon finding and reading FAA orders describing the extension details, I discovered my certificate was subject to a two-month extension and expired date.  Immediately I visited the FAA's AME directory and called several AME's because my AME has since closed their practice.  I found several AME's (including my previous AME) to be no longer practicing FAA exams (some for several years), however they were still listed in the FAA directory.  I finally found someone still in business and am in the process of scheduling an appointment with them at the earliest convenience.I have a flight today to/from ZZZ2 where I will sit the in the right seat only to provide an extra set of trained eyes and ears for the safety of the flight while not acting as PIC or SIC until my medical is renewed.				Corporate pilot reported flying without a current medical.	
1788414	202102	1201-1800	SCT.TRACON	CA				11000		VMC	Turbulence		Daylight			Center ZZZ	Personal	Cirrus Vision SJ50		1	Part 91	IFR	Training		Descent	Direct; Airway TRM	Class E ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Personal	Pilot Flying; Single Pilot	Flight Crew Commercial	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 95; Flight Crew Total 5100; Flight Crew Type 70		Workload			1788414												Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Weather	Weather	I reviewed the weather at approximately XA00 hours, including visibility, winds, and turbulence.  When I reviewed this weather, the winds forecast for the ZZZ airport was indicated to be 18-25 knots, and mild turbulence was indicated through both visual and graphical reviews using the ForeFlight products.  At 1 1/2 hours later, two passengers and I boarded our SF50 Vision Jet and rechecked the winds at the ZZZ airport.  The winds had held steady at the previous levels.  We decided that we continue with our planned trip, understanding that the winds were mainly straight down the direction of the runway.  We filed for an altitude en-route at 11,000 feet, believing that it would be high enough to prevent any issues that we've encountered on the numerous trips we've flown to ZZZ with respect to turbulence.  We've flown over 100 missions into the ZZZ area over the past six months.  The takeoff from ZZZ1 was routine and without incident.  We climbed up to 11,000 feet without incident and no issues with turbulence.  Our issues began when we transferred to the TRACON frequency. Twice during the turbulence, we attempted to review the weather using the GTC 2 system within the aircraft and were having a difficult time stabilizing our inputs with our fingers, even using the bezel to support our hands and use our thumbs to do so was challenging.  During one of the larger turbulent events, the autopilot disconnect was engaged by the second pilot in the right seat, which we were immediately able to re-engage as the buttons on the aircraft's autopilot is not electronic but tactile.  Once we had the autopilot back engaged, we referred back to the GTC 2 to re-engage the flight plan and selected the approach that was already listed on the display; which was the visual approach to Runway XXL, using the VOR as a descend and entrance point.  We had not noticed that the VNAV aspect of the approach had been selected when we re-engaged the auto-pilot, at which point we were now consistently in moderate to severe turbulence.  During this period, the TRACON controller called us and simply said 'Why are you descending.'   We told them about the visual approach being loaded in and began to describe what our corrective action would be -- however, in that split second, we hit the single most severe turbulence I've ever felt in an aircraft.  The turbulence threw the aircraft up, then aggressively left, which then dropped aggressively, throwing everybody to the header of the cabin.  My headset  (a Bose A20) was broken on the upper hinge, which struck the upper panel and broke loose my supplemental oxygen mask, breaking the clamp.   Objects in the cabin flew forward and up.  At this point, I immediately placed the aircraft in recovery mode, with full power, level wings and attempted to contact ATC to advise that I needed an emergency descent.  In VFR conditions, I noticed that I was over the mountain and switched to [next] frequency, and immediately advised the same and was given the approval to descend to 6,000 feet with a distinct heading.   I was then advised other aircraft were experiencing similar issues with turbulence and had gone missed into ZZZ. On the new heading and altitude, I began a full systems check of the aircraft, landing gear cycle, ailerons, flaps and review the fuel system.   I advised the TRACON controller that we were fine and that the oxygen masks had been deployed.   The controller provided be vectors, then cleared us for the VOR approach into ZZZ.  We flew the approach; we decided about three miles from the airport to abandon our approach and return to ZZZ1, in which we were given vectors and an approach back into the ZZZ1 airport without incident. Many things happened on this trip, almost in serial.  Without a doubt, the level of forecasted and reported turbulence was well above what we had seen using ForeFlight, or based on our prior experience.  When recovering from the several avionics disruptions (auto-pilot switch shut-offs, etc.), we had maintained a fairly vigilant response to restoring our programmed flight activity - until the turbulence began to get violent.   In a similar environment, I would ask for a 180-degree turn to remove myself and the aircraft from the events that led to deteriorating events with even lesser conditions.  Our corrective action after getting clearance for the emergency descent was sound, but it should have never been necessary if we had more carefully judged the degree of the turbulence, which had caused us not immediately to identify the VNAV programming into the GTC 2.  The three of us debriefed the flight in detail, including a review of the ForeFlight products to identify in greater clarity about Airmets (Tango) and how we could set up levels of automation that we could defer in order to maintain more positive control of the aircraft.   In reviewing our training materials, we reviewed the SF50 process on how to accept lower levels of automation during events that require higher pilot concentration and how this aids in the better situational awareness in events that we experienced on this date.				SF-50 Vision Jet Pilot reported experiencing extreme turbulence resulted in loss of aircraft control.	
1788772	202102	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US	090	153		39000		IMC	Turbulence; Snow; Icing; Hail; Fog; 1		Daylight	400		Center ZZZ	Corporate	BAe 125 Series 800		2	Part 135	IFR	Ferry / Re-Positioning		Climb	Direct	Class A ZZZ																								Oil Pressure Indication		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 120; Flight Crew Total 21000; Flight Crew Type 200					1788772												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Inflight Shutdown; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Climbing to FL390, at FL385 the Master Warning light flickered.  It finally showed 'LOW OIL PRESSURE #2'.  Engine was shutdown in accordance with the checklist. We notified ZZZ Center and we were cleared direct ZZZ1. Having departed ZZZ1 20 minutes earlier and with the Midwest in the midst of a snow storm we chose to return to ZZZ1 because we were familiar with the airport and it's current weather and surface conditions. After a slow descent and completion of all checklists we were a mere 60 from ZZZ1. An uneventful landing was accomplished.				First Officer reported an In Flight Shut Down and Air Turn Back, caused by a number two engine low oil pressure warning.	
1788804	202102	1801-2400	ZJX.ARTCC	FL				41000		IMC	Turbulence					Center ZJX	Air Carrier	B737 Undifferentiated or Other Model			Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZJX																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer		Flight Crew Last 90 Days 82; Flight Crew Total 1626; Flight Crew Type 1626		Situational Awareness			1788804												Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Ground Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew		Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Weather	Weather	We were in clear air above our altitude of FL410. we encountered unexpected continuous moderate turbulence and were unable to maintain altitude. We rapidly lost 1,000 feet of altitude.				B737 First Officer reported temporarily losing altitude control due to continuous moderate turbulence.	
1789763	202102	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				2000		VMC	Turbulence		Daylight			Center ZZZ	Corporate	Any Unknown or Unlisted Aircraft Manufacturer		2	Part 135	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch	Vectors	Class A ZZZ																								INS / IRS / IRU		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Corporate	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1789763												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	After a 2 hour delay for other related maintenance issue we took off from ZZZ on our way to ZZZ1. Right after take off as we were climbing an AMBER CAS message showed indicating IRS 1-2 COOL FAIL we were climbing to 2,000 ft. then we were cleared to 10,000 ft. we kept on climbing with CAS message still showing AMBER at around 8,000 ft. I instructed SIC to get the QRH as we were still climbing, we reached 10,000 ft. then we were cleared to FL230 as we passed 10,000 ft. we started working on the problem QRH indicated to SHUT DOWN IRSs as we kept on climbing to FL230 I was hoping perhaps the cool air would cool IRS and I wouldn't have to shut them off because if I did I will lose automatization and navegation as we were going thru the QRH the IRS 1 and 2 shut themselves OFF at that point I lost all automatization, AP AT, Yaw Damper, Navigation and DUs 1 and 6 showed a RED ATT (no attitude indicators). At that point We manually shut off the IRS and I went to the display controller and selected IRS3 the RED ATT indicator went away I had my attitude indicator back but not any automatization still no AP AT Yaw damper and Navigation then we were cleared to climb to FL320 at that point I made my mind of not more climbing did not want to get to RVSM space without automatization and I instructed my SIC to call ATC and request return ASAP and PRIORITY, ZZZ2 ATC was very busy it took some time for my SIC to get the request in so I stayed at FL230. After communicating with ATC they asked for our intentions I instructed my SIC return to airport but DIVERT to ZZZ4 (ZZZ4 has a longer runway than ZZZ they are closed to one each other and the winds when we took off were very strong) ATC acknowledged the request and were giving some turns and step descents I don't really remember what headings or what altitudes however I was trying no hand fly the airplane and land safely. It was very bumpy we had severe to moderate turbulence I instructed the Captain to go the cabin and tell passengers we are returning to airport fasten their seat belts she went back there and instructed passengers to do that then she came up front and asked if bracing position was necessary I said no but I want you seat in the back with them and make them feel comfortable she said two little girls were crying but they were OK we also had 2 Dogs on board. We started our descent and turns as per ATC instructions preparing for the visual approach latest winds were 08018G23 during the descent at some level off altitudes it was hard to keep the airplane stable or on assign altitude as it was very bumpy it fell like like the trim was working at half rate and without yaw damper I had to use rudder input also. During all the flight from the take off on I couldn't hear my SIC when she was transmitting to ATC when pushing the push to talk button on the yoke, I could hear ATC but not my SIC... I also instructed Captain to call Operations on SAT PHONE but was not working either. I hand flew that airplane for about 40 minutes almost from takeoff to landing we landed safely and exited the runway on our own, Fire trucks were waiting and asked if we needed assistance we said no, then on the ground ATC gave my SIC a phone number for me to call. I also spoke to NAME County fire and rescue they wanted a statement from me as well as my name, I called ZZZ3 ATC on NUMBER they also wanted a statement of the failure and I said we had the NAV computers overheat and the shut off we lost all automatization but I was able to control the airplane however it was very bumpy at some times....they asked for my name, my pilot certificate number, address phone number and said they will write an incident report and I should do the same. After landing I spoke to passengers I explained to them in a way that they could understand why we came back and why we choose ZZZ4 they were fine with it and just wanted a recovery flight. Captain fed them on the plane while I went to FBO I talked to chief pilot I spoke to Operations and Maintenance and I called Center as requested. I came back to plane and right at that time a recovery GV landed we helped the passengers to transfer to other plane and transfer their luggage. After they left SIC and I did a radio check on the ground checked all the buttons in the right position and they were still could not hear her were she pushed the push to talk button not in VHF1 or VHF2. We secured the airplane added some oil helped the Captain with the cleaning and got to our hotel almost 3 hours after we landed.				Captain reported diverting after losing cooling to both Inertial Reference Systems, which caused the failure of numerous systems.	
1789909	202102	0001-0600	CZQX.ARTCC	NF				35000		VMC	Turbulence					Center CZQX	Air Carrier	MD-11		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A CZQX																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1789909	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1789910	Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence					Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Weather	Weather	While cruising at 0.82 Mach at FL350 with an FMC calculated optimum altitude of FL370, aircraft experienced unforecasted severe turbulence with the autothrottles immediately becoming disconnected and the airspeed immediately accelerated to approximately 320 kts. 0.85 Mach (5 kts. over VMO Max speed). As Pilot Flying, I manually managed the throttles to avoid any further overspeeds and requested that Pilot Monitoring coordinate for a descent to FL330. This request was made and we received a 'Standby' message. [Two minutes later], the airspeed fluctuations became more severe and the autopilot had trouble maintaining altitude. I then advised the Pilot Monitoring that I was initiating a descent to FL330 and to advise Gander that were doing so prior to receiving ATC clearance. The PM sent this message to Gander that were descending due to severe turbulence and as we were descending through FL33.5, Gander approved our previous request for descent to FL330.		The plane was in cruise at FL350, the aircraft entered abruptly into severe turbulence without warning. The auto-throttles disconnected and the airspeed accelerated into the barber pole (approximately 3-5 kts. overspeed) for approximately 2-3 seconds. Followed by an abrupt deceleration and continuous vibrations of the aircraft. Previous cruising airspeed was .82M. The PF (Pilot Flying) was manually controlling the throttles to the best of his abilities. We then asked for FL330 via CPDLC. The aircraft was becoming extremely hard to control at an appropriate airspeed and altitude. We received a Standby message. [Two minutes later], the aircraft began to enter constant severe turbulence and the decision was made to descend immediately to FL330 for the safety of flight. I advised Gander Radio that the decent to FL330 had been initiated due to the severe turbulence. We received an approval to descend to FL300 at approximately FL350. No other deviations were noted. We called Gander Radio to issue a Pirep and contacted the Dispatcher or subsequent Pireps and reports.		MD-11 Flight Crew reported encountering severe turbulence which resulted in evasive action for safety of flight.	
1790208	202102	1801-2400	SEA.Airport	WA				12000			Turbulence; Windshear					TRACON S46	Air Carrier	Commercial Fixed Wing		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Descent	STAR GLASR1	Class B SEA; Class E ZSE																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Distraction; Other / Unknown; Physiological - Other; Workload			1790208												Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Weather	Weather	During the approach we were cleared direct down to 8000 feet from 12000 feet. During the descent Autopilot was engaged and following LNAV and VS we were encountering continuous light to moderate turbulence throughout the descent. As we passed through approximately 12000 feet and beginning to slow, we experienced severe turbulence mixed with continuous moderate [turbulence]. When we initially encountered the severe turbulence, the Autopilot disengaged, the aircraft was changing altitude and air speeds abruptly with several hundred feet of altitude change and at least plus or minus 20 to 30 knots airspeed changes. The Captain was thrown hard enough to hit the overhead panel with his head and we received a report one of our Flight Attendants had been hurt and several passengers had hit their heads. I as the Pilot Flying took manual control of the aircraft and flew the remainder of the descent and approach. The aircraft was extremely hard to control throughout most of the descent and approach as we continued to encounter continuous turbulence, strong crosswinds and wind shear. During the ensuing descent and approach the aircraft was extremely hard to control as we continued to experience moderate to severe turbulence, strong crosswind and wind shear throughout the approach. Once established inbound and cleared for the approach we were pushed several degrees off course and had some airspeed fluctuations of plus or minus 20 knots. We quickly discussed missed approach but prior to reaching 1000 feet we had regained the course, were on glide path and stabilized (properly configured) to continue the approach. We continued the approach while receiving some lower wind shear of plus or minus 10 knots down to approximately the 500 foot call. At 500 feet we were still stabilized and continued the approach to landing without further issue. Throughout the approach and landing, we had airspeed, altitude variations and heading deviations that were extremely hard to control due to the turbulence and wind shear events we were experiencing. I do not believe at any point we had any exceedances or violations, however, it was a very task saturated environment. There were no reports of the severity of the turbulence ahead of us and it was a very unexpected event.This was an example of good Crew Resource Management as the Captain and I maintained good communication throughout the event and helped to cross monitor the aircraft systems and instruments. As the pilot flying, I was entirely focused with aircraft control and the Captain doing the pilot monitoring was ensuring all the descent and approach tasks were completed. We kept good crew communication with what was happening with aircraft control and situational awareness. A recommendation is for pilots to ensure they continue to practice hand flying skills for situations like this. The autopilot was unusable, and I was thankful I constantly practice hand flying the airplane. It helped me with confidence to control the airplane to the best of my ability in a situation where the aircraft is being tossed around by turbulence.				Pilot reported encountering a loss of control due to severe turbulence and wind shear on descent and approach into SEA.	
1790342	202102	1201-1800	ZSE.ARTCC	WA				14000		VMC	Turbulence					Center ZSE	Air Taxi	Caravan Undifferentiated		1	Part 135	IFR	Cargo / Freight / Delivery	GPS; FMS Or FMC	Cruise	Direct	Class E ZSE																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Taxi	Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Commercial						1790342												Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Weather	Weather	While approaching Mt Jefferson area on course direct ELKES at 14,000 aircraft encountered a mountain wave. First indications were not unusual; a loss of altitude followed by the autopilot pitch correction to maintain and resulting indicated airspeed loss. It is not uncommon for a momentary loss of a couple hundred feet and airspeed to drop to 90 KIAS, however, at the point where it would 'normal' the situation quickly escalated into an uncontrolled altitude loss as the autopilot rapidly pitched above 15* and I noted a 90+ headwind on the G600 wind vector indicator. I immediately contacted ATC that I was unable to maintain altitude, which they issued a lower clearance (13,000) and vector away from terrain. With the autopilot turned off, I maintained about 10* nose up at 75 KIAS, flaps 10, full throttle and noted a 1200FPM altitude loss. As I approached 13,000 I informed ATC I was unable and inquired about the MVA. I was fully prepared to activate emergency power, declare, and turn back, however ATC stated that the heading I was on gave me about 9000 for an MVA. I elected to ride the wave as the uncontrolled altitude loss was trending up and was able to stabilize as I approached 12,000. While altitude was stable, I remained below the MEA and published minimum off route altitude unable to climb or increase indicated airspeed above 80KIAS for several minutes. As the aircraft exited the mountain wave to the south, I was able to recover the airspeed slowly back to normal cruise as the aircraft moved over the ridge lines.This is the second time a mountain wave event has resulted in altitude loss of more than a few hundred feet. During the 'normal' events I have always notified ATC that I was encountering a wave and only one time previous did the incident result in the necessity for a lower clearance at 13,000'. However, in this event, I would characterize the resulting altitude loss as uncontrolled at minimum control airspeed and max continuous power for the altitude. I would consider this to be an escalation of what might be a normal inconvenience, and causes me to inquire about alternate routing. In all cases of my experience, these waves seem to be encountered over the Mt Jefferson area and a southerly re-routing of to the south is the normal avoidance procedure when the waves are encountered. But why wait until one is encountered? Might it be more prudent to take a different airway further south to begin with? Might be worth exploring.				C208 Captain reported encountering mountain wave action which resulted in momentary loss of control and altitude excursion.	
1790344	202102	1801-2400	ZME.ARTCC	TN				38400		VMC	Turbulence		Night			Center ZME	Air Carrier	Commercial Fixed Wing		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Cruise	Direct	Class A ZME																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument			Time Pressure; Situational Awareness			1790344	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Time Pressure; Situational Awareness			1790345	Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence		Y	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Maintenance Action	Weather	Weather	Cruise flight at FL 390. Smooth air while overlying thunderstorms and VFR conditions. ATC authorized deviations left and right. Captain (CA) made PA call to Flight Attendants (FA) and passengers to all be seated and fasten seat belts. As the aircraft approached the far end of storm, severe turbulence rocked the plane and stick shaker and high speed warning activated twice. CA corrected and re-established control each time, but lost approximately 600 feet in so doing. Pilot Monitoring (FO) called FA's and FA1 reported FA2 was injured. They found her on the floor. FA1 asked for help from medical personnel and they responded. Destination was only 25 minutes away so we continued heading there in case medical attention was needed. Reported injury to ATC. Below 10,000 feet personnel in back advised paramedics were needed. Called for them to meet us at gate and proceeded direct to airport with ATC approval. Emergency vehicles were at the gate, but no ramp personal. FA2 was conscious but confused. She was taken to hospital. Dispatch and Maintenance contacted. I (Captain) called hospital from hotel and spoke to FA2. She was coherent but had no memory of any events previous to people talking to her from the passenger seat.		Captain was Pilot Flying. Cruise at FL390 with smooth air flying over a thunderstorm. Deviations left and right approved. PA call to FA's (Flight Attendants) and passengers to all be seated and buckled. Far edge of storm was in sight. Severe turbulence, as published, was encountered. Stick shaker activated twice and Hi Speed aural warning twice. Both times, stick shaker stopped quickly before throttles were advanced to maximum thrust. Loss of altitude approximately 600 feet. Control was re-established. Called FA's and heard FA2 was injured. They found her on the floor. FA1 asked for help from medical personnel; two nurses and one flight attendant responded. Destination was only 25 minutes away so we continued heading there in case medical attention was needed. Reported injury to ATC. Below 10,000 feet, personnel in back advised paramedics were needed. Called for them to meet us at gate and proceeded direct to airport with ATC approval. Emergency vehicles were at the gate. FA2 was conscious but confused and in pain from a hip. She was taken to hospital. Dispatch and Maintenance contacted. I (Captain) called hospital from hotel and spoke to FA2. She was coherent but had no memory of any events previous to people talking to her from the passenger seat.		Air Carrier Pilots reported severe turbulence encountered and a Flight Attendant injured.	
1791302	202102	1801-2400	ZKC.ARTCC	KS				35000		IMC	Turbulence		Daylight			Center ZKC	Air Carrier	Commercial Fixed Wing		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZKC																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1791302												ATC Issue All Types; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence		N	Automation Aircraft TA; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Returned To Clearance; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; Flight Crew FLC complied w / Automation / Advisory; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Weather; Airspace Structure; Procedure	Weather	The flight was cruising at 35,000 ft. (FL350) in Instrument Meteorological Conditions (IMC). The flight attendants had been briefed earlier about turbulence along the route of flight. When the turbulence became constant moderate, the flight attendants were informed via the public address (PA) system to 'take their seats.' The flight was deviating around weather and needed a left deviation and attempted request a deviation from ATC; however, due to frequency congestion this was causing a delay in deviation. The Captain executed the deviation out of safety (emergency authority) and had the First Officer announce the deviation to ATC, not long after the deviation severe turbulence was encountered lasting approximately one minute. The aircraft was out of control with large airspeed, attitude, and altitude deviations. The severe turbulence caused a 500 foot altitude deviation (35,500 ft.) which triggered a TCAS alert. All automation disconnected in the turbulence and the Captain assumed manual flight control. The second aircraft was acquired visually, and the Captain manually maneuvered to regain control, avoid the aircraft, and return to 35,000 ft. Once clear of the turbulence, the auto-throttles and autopilot were re-engaged, and ATC informed about the location and magnitude of the severe turbulence. The First Officer was tasked to check on the status of the flight attendants and passengers. There were no flight attendant or passenger injuries. Both during flight and upon landing, Maintenance Control, Dispatch, and Operations was kept informed of the severe turbulence encounter along with crew and passenger status. A combination of active weather with turbulence across much of the central United States; high workload environment; saturated ATC frequencies.One cannot control the weather; gather as much information as possible about the route and possible threats along the way.				Air carrier pilot reported severe turbulence encounter during cruise and ATC communication problem that resulted in altitude excursion, track deviation and conflict with other aircraft.	
1791689	202103	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US						VMC	Turbulence		Night				Air Carrier	Commercial Fixed Wing		2	Part 121	IFR	Cargo / Freight / Delivery		Climb; Initial Climb		Class B ZZZ	N	Y	Y	Y	Unscheduled Maintenance	Inspection; Testing; Installation																		Main Gear		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument						1791689	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1791292	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Maintenance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural FAR		N	Person Maintenance; Person Flight Crew	In-flight; Aircraft In Service At Gate	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Procedure; Aircraft	Procedure	First leg was a block turn back in ZZZ. Fluid leaking under right main gear. Maintenance performed the following corrective action: REMOVED AND REPLACED RH GEAR TRIM CYLINDER RELIEF VALVE. I asked the maintainer whether he needed to bleed the brakes since he cracked the closed system. He told me to cycle the brakes and if they felt good we were good to go. Legs 2 was the subsequent takeoff. After takeoff, gear handle would not move. QRH procedure offered no resolution. Declared priority handling. Conferenced maintenance and Operations Control in the air. Uneventful landing ZZZ Runway XX. The following was the maintenance corrective action: CONFIRMED FAULT, GEAR HANDLE WILL NOT RETRACT IN ANY CONFIG. T/S SYSTEM AND FOUND MLG HYDRAULIC INTERLOCK CYLINDER NOT EXTENDING WITH SYSTEM 3 PRESSURE, WHICH MECHANICALLY INHIBITED TRAVEL OF GEAR HANDLE. DUE TO TRIM CYLINDER RELIEF VALVE RECENT CHANGE, SUSPECT RESIDUAL AIR IN SYSTEM. BLED TRIM CYL RELIEF VALVE, NO AIR. BLED HYD FUSE, FOUND A SIGNIFICANT AMOUNT OF AIR-ENTRAINED FLUID. BLED INTERLOCK CYLINDER. NO FLUID CAME OUT AT FIRST, FOLLOWED BY A SIGNIFICANT AMOUNT OF AIR ENTRAINED FLUID. AFTER BLEEDING, SYSTEM WORKS NORMALLY, INTERLOCK EXTENDS AND RETRACTS AS NORMAL, REF [type aircraft] AMM 32-31-05-2 AND AMM 32-37-03-2. JACKED ACFT AND SWUNG GEAR TO VERIFY A POSITIVE FIX. GEAR SWINGS NORMALLY WITH CORRECT INDICATION MULTIPLE CYCLES. ACFT OK FOR SERVICE. Is it reasonable to draw a connection between leg 1 and leg 2 as to cause and effect for the gear handle stuck in the down position? After extensive research, I wasn't able to find a definitive answer as to a correlation. However, during my research, I discovered that it is maintenance procedure to bleed the hydraulic system after relief valve replacement. Interestingly enough, I even asked the mechanic about this. He response was to me was to set and release the brakes a few times, and then he asked me if they 'felt okay'. When I replied that 'They seemed normal' he said 'You're good to go'. Obviously, I wasn't good to go. I should also mention that after the job was declared complete, I went down to observe the new valve. There was hydraulic fluid everywhere including a 3 foot diameter puddle under the valve. As the Captain, when I ask specific questions about a maintenance procedure, it is unacceptable to be given incorrect information by maintenance or be lied to by maintenance. Why wasn't the procedure completed correctly? Why isn't there a check and balance system in place to verify mechanics are completing maintenance actions? I demand an answer to these questions as well as why there was so much air in the hydraulic system which inhibited gear handle movement.		Crew contacted me via radio, shortly after takeoff. Captain advised that they were unable to raise the gear and will be returning to ZZZ. Captain advised gear was down and locked but said he will [advise ATC] as a precaution. Earlier, flight did a block turn back due to a hydraulic leak at the gear. Maintenance repaired the leak by replacing a valve. Not sure, at this time, if that played a part with the gear not retracting. Flight landed safely and Manager was notified.		Heavy aircraft flight crew reported a landing gear malfunction that resulted in a return to the departure airport.	
1792233	202103	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US		0	0			VMC	Turbulence; 10		Daylight	6500		Tower ZZZ	Personal	Skyhawk 172/Cutlass 172		2	Part 91	None	Training		Takeoff / Launch		Class D ZZZ																												Tower ZZZ	Personal	Skyhawk 172/Cutlass 172		1	Part 91	None	Training		Landing		Class D ZZZ																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Personal	Pilot Not Flying; Instructor	Flight Crew Flight Instructor; Flight Crew Commercial; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 30; Flight Crew Total 450; Flight Crew Type 401		Time Pressure; Distraction; Confusion			1792233												Conflict Ground Conflict, Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Ground Incursion Runway	Vertical 200	N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued Advisory / Alert; Flight Crew Rejected Takeoff; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Environment - Non Weather Related; Human Factors; Procedure; Aircraft	Human Factors	After completing a run-up, my student and I taxied to the Runway XX hold short line and advised ZZZ Tower, '(Aircraft X), [Taxiway] XX, [Runway] XX, ready for departure'. We received a takeoff clearance from ZZZ Tower and entered Runway XX, after confirming the Runway XX final approach was clear. Upon lining up with the runway center line, my student applied full power, as part of the takeoff procedure. After checking the engine instruments and airspeed indicator conditions, I looked up and observed an airplane flying not more than 300 ft. above our right wing. This airplane (Aircraft Y) then proceeded to overfly us and land not more than 1,000 feet of my airplane on Runway XX, which was occupied by us at that time. I immediately aborted the takeoff and exited the runway at XX1, then advised the tower that we were aborting.ZZZ Tower cancelled our takeoff clearance and questioned Aircraft Y, asking why they executed an unauthorized short approach, which led to loss of separation between our two aircraft and furthermore led to a runway incursion that could have been detrimental.ZZZ Tower did what they could to assist with the situation and made it clear to Aircraft Y that their actions were unacceptable and reckless.				Pilot reported while on takeoff roll another aircraft over flew the departing aircraft and landed ahead.	
1792383	202103	0601-1200	SMF.Airport	CA	045	30		37000		VMC	Turbulence		Daylight			Center ZOA	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Climb	SID SCTWN 3	Class A ZOA																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Other		1792383	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 154; Flight Crew Type 1823		Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Other		1792361	Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Weather; Environment - Non Weather Related	Weather	During climb out of SMF, everything was normal with some occasional light chop. We had asked the Flight Attendants to remain seated due to crossing the Sierra Mountains. We were climbing at approximately .76 Mach. Upon leveling at FL 370, we experienced moderate bordering on severe mountain wave. Our airspeed began decreasing rapidly and the F/O (First Officer) disconnected the autopilot, applied full thrust, and began a shallow descent to regain airspeed. The airspeed decayed to about .70 - .71. It was about a 30 knot decrease. I may be a little off on the Mach numbers. We descended about 500' and the airspeed began increasing.  I reported the encounter to ATC and we began climbing back to FL 370. The wave climbed us to FL 375, and at that time we requested a descent to FL 350. The Controller cleared us to descend to FL 350. We did not encounter any additional mountain wave and nothing more than a little light chop. We checked with the Flight Attendants to make sure everyone was alright, and the remainder of the flight was uneventful. I would like to add that the F/O did an excellent job of recognizing and reacting to the situation.		We had just leveled off at our cruise altitude of FL 370. (Filed for FL 390 but we were too heavy at that time.) During climb we had anticipated and encounter some light turbulence. Nothing more significant was encountered. WSI (Weather Service International) and Jeppesen weather showed areas of turbulence covering about the first 45 minutes of our flight. Our clearance was on the SCTWN 3 RNAV Departure. We were level at FL 370 when suddenly our airspeed had dropped from M.76 (250 KIAS) to 220 KIAS this was approximately 10-15 above the yellow maneuvering bar on the airspeed indicator and the green vector bar was indicating that the slowing was continuing and at a significant rate. I disconnected the autopilot and autothrottle and pitched down to keep airspeed. I also increased thrust to max and I was able to recover airspeed about 500' low at FL 365. In my attempt to climb back to FL 370 we started climbing at a significant rate and stopped the climb at approximately FL 376. The Captain reported to ATC that we were in MVA (mountain wave activity) and that we were unable to hold altitude. I continued to hand fly until I was sure we were passed the MVA and re-engaged the autopilot. The rest of the flight was uneventful.		B737-700 flight crew reported encountering moderate to severe mountain wave for which led to a rapid decay of their airspeed.	
1793296	202103	1201-1800	SLC.Airport	UT				9600		IMC	Snow; Icing; Turbulence; 10		Daylight	10000		TRACON S56	Corporate	Small Transport, Low Wing, 2 Turbojet Eng		1	Part 91	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach	Direct; STAR QUENN 5 RNAV	Class E ZLC																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Corporate	Pilot Flying; Captain; Single Pilot	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Flight Instructor; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 49; Flight Crew Total 4415; Flight Crew Type 1550		Workload; Communication Breakdown; Time Pressure	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1793296												ATC Issue All Types; Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Conflict Airborne Conflict; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation - Altitude Crossing Restriction Not Met; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance	Weather; Aircraft; Human Factors	Human Factors	The Deviation event occurred over or around the HLMET intersection on the LDA Runway 35 approach into Salt Lake City International. ATC instructions were to proceed direct to HLMET, Cross HLMET at 11,000, cleared for the approach. Somewhere just seconds before or after crossing HLMET I descended to an altitude of approximately 9,600 MSL. The altitude on approach after crossing HLMET is 10,000 MSL until crossing the nest fix PEDLE. However after becoming distracted (which I will explain later), I noticed my altitude got down to that approximately 9,600 MSL for which I knew was not right, and immediately began corrective action while simultaneously ATC queried my altitude and I made mention of correcting. I saw I was just passed HLMET and quickly climbed back up to the crossing altitude of 10,000 MSL. Shortly later the controller mentioned the possible pilot deviation may have led to a loss of separation, although no TA/RA's were issued by either aircraft. Here is where I want to bring to attention the possible safety issue as related to the situation and what resulted in me becoming distracted and task saturated. Starting at an ATC controller back before TRACON, which was Salt Lake Center and while on the QWENN 5 RNAV Arrival off the MLF VORTAC transition, I was left extremely high by the center controller, for which I was expecting to receive the 'Expect' crossing restriction at FRNZY intersection at 15,000 MSL on the chart. I could hear multiple aircraft in front of me getting the FRNZY at 15,000 MSL restriction, but also complaining of being left very high and possibly not being able to make it. As I started to see the FMS computer show a required 3,500 FPM decent to make that restriction, I thought I better ask the controller for a descent, however the frequency was congested and it took many more minutes until finally the controller just issued me to now cross FRNZY at 16,000 MSL and 250 KTS. I replied that I would do by best, but it would be tight to make. Mind you that this new altitude required my attention to reprogram the altitude into the FMS as I anticipated and setup for 15,000, and still it showed a required 3,800+FPM decent to make it. I immediately initiated the decent for which I used the VNAV mode in the FMS/autopilot to make the crossing, which also required idle power and the activation of the aircraft's speed brake system to induce drag to not over-speed in the decent. During the decent on the arrival there was also light to bordering on moderate rime icing in IMC and snow showers. When the engines are at idle power they do not produce enough bleed air to keep the Anti-Ice/De-Ice systems operating, for which I got warning that they were not being kept hot enough, so I was trying to introduce small amounts of power to try and get more bleed air flowing to those systems, all the while trying not to over-speed and setup for the instrument approach. Being single-pilot this induced a very high work load, which caused me to become task saturated and behind on preparing for the approach. After finally passing FRNZY at the 16,000 MSL altitude and still in icing conditions and IMC I was simultaneously passed to TRACON approach control which issued me the direct HLMET, cross at 11,000 MSL, cleared approach restriction. I continued the decent down out of 16,000 MSL to 11,000 MSL still using VNAV on the autopilot (more on that later). I noticed I was still nearing around idle power and adding what I could but was still having trouble keeping the speed under control and still behind on setting up the approach. Somewhere around 12,000 MSL I got an indication that the speed brake panels went down, even though I didn't select it. Wondering what happened I looked out the pilot side window at the wing and noticed they were in fact still up, just no longer indicating it in the cockpit. This distracted me, as I knew I needed them extended to maintain my speed and help to try and keep power up and bleed air flowing. It took mybrain a few moments to try and figure out what was going on as there is no abnormal procedure for that in the checklist. I decided to recycle the speed brake switch, for which they then operated normally (retracted and extended), and then gave all normal indications. Throughout that situation is when the altitude deviation occurred, as after I was confident there was not a problem with the speed brakes and they just needed a reset, I accidentally went through the 11,000 MSL attitude down to the approximately 9,600 MSL altitude. Upon speaking with Maintenance later on the ground they feel that the system was activated for so long throughout the decent continuously, they work trapping hydraulic pressure to hold it up, that logically that pressure must have bleed down some, causing them to blow in-trial/down just enough to give an indication in the cockpit that they were sent fully down, and that this was not a malfunction or cause for investigation for repair, especially since they functioned normally after the reset. The indication only looks for full up or full down deflection, so if they blew down a little due to reduction in trapped pressure, it made sense to them. Again they continued to function and indicated correctly the rest of the flight. Back to the VNAV function I used on the autopilot to meet the crossing restriction, as that is the recommended function to meet crossing restrictions, and the function I always use to reduce pilot workload. Somewhere during the sequence of the approximate 3,800-4,000FPM required descent the VNAV system apparently kicked off and defaulted back to just a raw pitch autopilot decent, in which with everything else going on I failed to notice. I can only attribute this to it not liking or taking issue with that rapid of a required decent, and thus defaulted out of it. With 1500+ hours in type, I cannot recall ever descending so rapidly, to were I would have seen or noticed its inability to function through that rapid of a decent. Had VNAV stayed activated, it would have saved the deviation from happening as the approach was loaded with all crossing restriction altitudes and it would have followed them; for which I was also expecting it to do. Although this 'event' happened in Tracon airspace, I feel the safety issue started back in Salt Lake Centers airspace by being left very high on the arrival. ATC needs to be cognizant especially when icing conditions are possible/present some or most aircraft cannot descend that rapidly and keep critical anti-ice/de-ice systems operating properly with idle power descent. Additionally, giving instruction to meet a crossing restriction that requires 3,500+FPM descents is unsafe and not conducive to being able to setup and properly execute a stabilized approach, and leads pilots to get behind. After giving this event thought I am recommending to our flight department to not use the VNAV function of the FMS/autopilot for required descents over 3,000 FPM, but rather use vertical speed mode and monitor carefully. In addition, we will no longer accept any crossing restrictions from ATC that would require over 3,000 FPM, and rather state unable and require a re-sequence back on the arrival via a course reversal to help lose altitude and keep from the pilot getting behind the aircraft and task saturated. Us pilot use the term 'slam dunk' when ATC tries to do this, where they keep you very high and then plop you right on the arrival/approach. This condition does present safety issues due to the above mentioned items.				Light Transport Single Pilot reported failing to cross arrival fix at assigned altitude resulting in a loss of ATC separation. Reporter also reported late descent clearance and inflight icing as contributing causes.	
1794131	202103	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US				6000			Turbulence		Daylight	CLR		TRACON ZZZ	Corporate	Medium Transport		2	Part 91	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Descent		Class E ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Corporate	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Flight Instructor; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 9.7; Flight Crew Total 12900; Flight Crew Type 1124		Situational Awareness; Training / Qualification			1794131												Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural FAR; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Returned To Clearance; Flight Crew Became Reoriented	Human Factors; Environment - Non Weather Related	Human Factors	Descending into ZZZ, I was keeping my speed below 250 knots while descending through 10,000 feet using speedbrakes (power at idle). As I was reaching my level off altitude in the descent, I retracted the speedbrakes and the aircraft speed increased to around 258 knots. Upon seeing this excess speed, I re-extended the speedbrakes and slowed the aircraft to below 250 knots. Cause - Not noticing the increasing airspeed was a rusty move on my part, I believe, due to lack of flying over the past few months. To prevent this from occurring again, I'll be more focused on the task at hand when changing configurations.				Corporate Jet Captain reported exceeding the 250 knot speed restriction below 10,000 feet and cited lack of flying as a contributing factor.	
1794180	202103	0001-0600	ROA.Airport	VA						VMC	Turbulence		Night			Tower ROA	Air Carrier	Commercial Fixed Wing		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach		Class C ROA																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument			Situational Awareness			1794180												Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT			Automation Aircraft Terrain Warning	In-flight	Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach	Airport; Human Factors	Human Factors	Landing 24 at night. Coming in over mountains in VMC. We knew we were fine however we got a terrain alert so we cleaned the aircraft up and went around and came back around landed on 34.Cleaned up and did a go around and decided to land on 34.At night when landing on 24 you need to go out further for the approach on 24 to avoid getting a terrain alert.				Air carrier First Officer reported terrain alert on approach resulting in a go-around.	
1794321	202103	1201-1800	ZLC.ARTCC	UT				32000			Turbulence; 100		Daylight	32000		Center ZLC	Air Taxi	Light Transport, Low Wing, 2 Turbojet Eng		2	Part 135	IFR	Passenger		Cruise	Direct	Class A ZLC																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Taxi	Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 60; Flight Crew Total 3000; Flight Crew Type 500					1794321												Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Weather	Weather	We encountered clear air turbulence at FL320. Turbulence at the time was moderate. We encountered a 1,000 feet climb followed by a rapid downward descent of 4,000 feet plus. I disengaged the autopilot and tried to regain control. By the time we were level, we were at FL230. In the descent we did advise ATC that we were unable to maintain altitude and had experienced severe turbulence. We did not declare any emergency and there were no injuries on board or damage to aircraft. ATC did advise us that there could be a light mountain wave and turbulence before this occurred. The turbulence was not too bad up until that point. I have never encountered that in my life. The best way I can describe it was a rogue wave of air and we hit it just right. The weather was clearing up to almost clear skies when this occurred. Lesson learned is don't judge a book by its cover.				Captain reported encountering clear air turbulence during cruise and as a result were momentarily unable to maintain altitude.	
1794633	202103	1201-1800	ZHU.ARTCC	TX				28000			Thunderstorm; Turbulence					Center ZHU	Air Carrier	Commercial Fixed Wing		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	GPS; FMS Or FMC	Cruise	Direct	Class A ZHU																								Central Computer		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Workload; Situational Awareness			1794633												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Aircraft Aircraft Damaged; Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Aircraft; Weather	Aircraft	There were storms in the area, and we were given a reroute by ATC, to go south to the coast, and then follow the coast west to IAH. The FO (First Officer, Pilot Monitoring) had made a passenger announcement telling them that we were expecting storms along the route of flight, and that we were anticipating a bumpy ride. I, the CP (Captain, Pilot Flying), had advised the FA that it was going to be bumpy. In the climb out, we encountered light turbulence. Upon reaching cruising altitude of FL280, it was smooth, so we briefly let the passengers up. Upon reaching the coast, ATC advised us of multiple storms and cells along our route of flight, and had mentioned that several aircraft ahead of us had gone south of these storms, had stayed within 50 NM of the coast, and had reported nothing worse than light turbulence, and advised we do the same. After discussing with the FO, we agreed that flying south of our route, and staying within 50 NM of the coast, was our best option. At this time, I had my radar up, and the FO had his terrain map up. We advised the passengers and the FA to expect a few bumps, turned the seat belt sign back on, and to remain seated. We also decided to slow to turbulence penetration speed. We were painting multiple red and magenta cells which we were staying south of, and had a lot of green all over the radar. We were dodging and avoiding the cells to the north, while staying within the 50 NM range of the coast. We then must have hit an embedded cell that the radar did not pick up, as it was only reading green. Almost instantly, we went into severe turbulence, and lost approximately 400 feet. The aircraft did not go into any unusual or upset attitudes, and the Auto-Pilot remained on. During this turbulence, we also had a suspected lightning strike - we saw a flash of light all around us, heard a bang, and smelt the 'fireworks' smell that you usually smell associated with lightning. After being struck, we got a Stall Protection System advanced master caution message appear. It disappeared within 5 seconds of it showing. As quick as the severe turbulence hit us, it disappeared. ATC queried us regarding our loss of altitude, at which point the FO reported the severe turbulence and suspected lightning strike. ATC asked if we needed further assistance, to which we replied no. As previously mentioned, this happened very quickly. All in all, I would say it lasted no longer than 30 seconds, and closer to 20 seconds. Once we were back at FL280, we had completely exited all weather (which we had been in for a majority of the time upon reaching the coast), and were in clear blue skies. I spoke to the FA, and asked if she was OK, to which she replied she was. She then asked if it was OK for her to check on the passengers, which she did. There were no injuries, and the only thing she mentioned is that the passengers were staring at her wide eyed. ATC asked if there were any injuries onboard, we replied with none. The flight continued with no further incidents. Upon landing, both pilots thoroughly inspected the outside of the plane on the walk around, noting a black mark on the right side of the nose which may be the entry point to the suspected lightning strike, but with no damage seen. I wrote up the plane with 3 write ups - Severe turbulence, Lightning strike, and Lock out/Tag out installed.CRM was effectively utilized during this event, with the PF using the radar, and the PM using the terrain. With the information available to us at the time, with ATC advising us of going south of the cells, as opposed to north, the decision, at the time, to go south was the correct one. That being said, I think it would have been smarter to go north of the cells, but we did not have that information. Utilizing the radar was key during this event, by staying out of the red and magenta cells. However, the radar has its limitation, especially considering they are older pieces of equipment, and can not 100% accurately paint embedded cells, of which we hit. After the event, ATC began recommending to other aircraft to go north of the cells. Ultimately, I would say the crew were stuck between a rock and a hard place - staying within the 50 NM boundary of the coast, but staying as far south as possible of the mega cells to the north. Additionally, I would like to mention the extraordinary job that the company did after the incident. Upon reaching the gate, multiple members of both [safety teams] reached out to me and the crew, checking that we were OK, and that the passengers were OK. I feel our company truly encompassed themselves with the company values, of which we stand for, and has made me thoroughly proud and joyful to be a part of.				Air Carrier Captain reported encountering severe turbulence, loss of aircraft control and a lightning strike.	
1794646	202103	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				35000		IMC	Windshear; Turbulence		Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	EMB ERJ 170/175 ER/LR		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZZZ																														Any Unknown or Unlisted Aircraft Manufacturer									Class A ZZZ																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1794646	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1794647	Conflict Airborne Conflict; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Speed All Types; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Aircraft Damaged; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Weather	Weather	While in cruise at 35,000 feet and in excess of 90 knots of wind from the tail, the flight had been in constant light and occasionally moderate chop. I requested 37,000 feet after confirming performance capability, and hearing ATC advice that higher altitudes were better than lower and the closer we got to 40,000 feet the better the ride. We were told 'Aircraft X, I have your request' and we were handed off to three subsequent controllers each assuring us that the next would assign our climb. Both PM (Pilot Monitoring) and PF (Pilot Flying) recognized the flight path was taking us directly into a cloud bank, and the turbulence levels were becoming more of a solid moderate chop. As a result, PM (the Captain) advised the Cabin Crew to secure themselves and instructed the passengers to take their seats and securely fasten their seat belts. This event took place less than 10 minutes from entry into extreme turbulence.On the last handover, we had a converging aircraft 1,000 feet above us and traveling in the opposite direction. When ATC called the traffic for each of us, we were just entering the cloud bank into IMC conditions. Almost immediately, we encountered an increase in turbulence to solid moderate (changes in aircraft attitude and altitude occurred, but the aircraft remained in positive control). However, within seconds, we encountered severe turbulence including large abrupt changes in altitude, attitude, airspeed, as well as wind shear and updrafts. The result was initially a traffic alert. Within a few more seconds, we were in extreme turbulence and the aircraft was being tossed about violently, control was practically impossible, and the aircraft was being pushed upwards.The PM called the increase in speed and retarded the thrust levers as the aircraft was being shaken so violently that it took both hands of the PF to maintain positive control. Then a TCAS RA alerted us with a 'LEVEL OFF' command with the converging traffic icon in the PFD illuminated yellow. Almost immediately after, the icon turned red and PF pressed the "FORCE TRIM INTERRUPT" button and shoved the yoke forward into the RA box to comply with the given guidance. The turbulence continued so violently that the autopilot was fully kicked offline, and the aircraft remained in a diving attitude per the TCAS guidance.During this period, and as a result of the wind shear and turbulence, the aircraft's speed fluctuated between a momentary illumination of the green PLI (Pitch Limit Indicator) and the triggering speed for a stick shaker activation. PM continued to apply speed brake and retard thrust levers to maintain the aircraft speed within limit (at no time did we encounter either a yellow or red PLI, or the stick shaker). At 30,500 feet we came out of the most violent turbulence and were able to reestablish a climb back to assigned altitude. Immediately after, the Captain contacted the cabin crew for an evaluation on any injuries or known damage. Beyond all the drinks and unsecured items being thrown around, none were reported, so we continued to the assigned destination. After landing, the post flight walk around revealed what appeared to be abnormal alignment or misalignment on the right outboard aileron.		[Report narrative contained no additional information.]		EMB-175 flight crew reported possible damage to aircraft flight controls after an encounter with extreme turbulence in cruise flight at 35,000 feet.	
1794931	202103	0601-1200	ZZZ.Tower	US						VMC	Turbulence					Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	EMB ERJ 145 ER/LR		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Landing																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 65; Flight Crew Total 2600; Flight Crew Type 170		Situational Awareness; Distraction			1794931												Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Became Reoriented	Human Factors; Weather	Human Factors	Arriving in gusty crosswind conditions. VMC. I was hand flying the ILS. At runway threshold, I inadvertently descended below the glide slope during transition to outside and received a glide slope warning. As we were in the flare over the runway landing was the safest option. [Identified by] GPWS warning; glide slope. Began transition to outside too early preoccupation with winds. Failed to continue cross checking indications. I heard the annunciation in the landing flare and completed a normal landing. The flight director indications guide the airplane to the touchdown zone use this information in combination with outside.				EMB-145 First Officer reported receiving a glide slope warning on an ILS approach, citing weather distractions as contributing.	
1796613	202103	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US				1100			Rain; Thunderstorm; Windshear; Turbulence						Air Carrier	B767-300 and 300 ER		2	Part 121	IFR	Cargo / Freight / Delivery		Landing		Class B ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Training / Qualification			1796613												Ground Event / Encounter Ground Strike - Aircraft; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Maintenance	Aircraft In Service At Gate	Aircraft Aircraft Damaged; Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach	Human Factors; Weather	Human Factors	Normal departure and flight enroute to ZZZ with thunderstorm approaching ZZZ airport on arrival from the west. Radar vectors for ILS XXL for approach, multiple speed adjustments from ATC on approach and no reports of wind gusts on initial arrival from ATC with winds 180/6 from ATIS. Storm had moved over field when on approach. On short final there was an RVR (Runway Visual Range) update from recall of greater than 5,000 RVR and moderate to heavy rain but no winds passed from recall. VMC around 1,100 feet and could see right and center runway but then went into torrential rain downpour around 400-500 feet on short final where Captain made an EFIS lights call and then I shortly after picked up runway lights and landing environment just prior to minimums and was a little high but was adjusting in close. Around 30-40 feet we had an instantaneous airspeed loss of around 10kts and at that time I had decided to go-around but with the airspeed loss and instantaneous loss of lift we touched down as go-around decision was made. We had a semi-hard landing and bounced and go-around selected all simultaneously. We were slow to put the speed brake back in after touching down but did eventually secure it and maintained attitude as we executed go around. Go-around procedures were completed airborne. Vectored back around by ATC for uneventful approach, landing and taxi in. The Captain and I both did a walk around of the aircraft post flight to ensure integrity of aircraft and check tail skid as a precaution but didn't notice the skid was ground down as we later were informed from the Director of Operations and Maintenance. We debriefed the event. Windshear/airspeed loss at low altitude while simultaneously executing go-around and didn't maintain proper go-around pitch once touching down as indicated from tail skid damage. Quicker reaction from crew with go-around with instantaneous airspeed loss and loss of lift and maintain proper attitude of aircraft on touchdown when executing go-around. Follow rules to live by, do not make large control inputs when in flare/go-around close to ground.				B767 flight crew reported windshear on landing resulting in tailstrike.	
1796652	202103	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				35000		IMC	Windshear; Turbulence		Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	Regional Jet 900 (CRJ900)		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Situational Awareness			1796652	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument			Situational Awareness			1796653	Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance	Human Factors; Weather	Weather	We were at FL350 in cruise between ZZZ and ZZZ1. ATC had given us left and right deviations and ZZZ1 when able. The radar was not painting much at the time but we were trying to deviate around what we saw on the radar. At the time everything was obscured and we were in the clouds. We did pop out just a little before the front and could make our way around a couple of the darker areas but it was not possible to get around all of it. As we crossed the front we started to get some shear in the winds that caused the airspeed to start to go up and down quite a bit. The autopilot then kicked off so my FO (First Officer) took manual control and the airspeed and altitude varied a lot as he flew the aircraft. The turbulence only got to a moderate level at most of the time but the downdrafts and updrafts made it difficult to keep any specific altitude or airspeed. Soon we hit a downdraft that was much worse than the rest and we were unable to maintain altitude even in climb power. I took control of the aircraft and told him to tell ATC we were descending because we were unable to maintain altitude at the time. We ended up descending about 1,000 feet and were able to level off at FL340 once we were through it. At the time I was just worried about keeping airspeed and not stalling the aircraft. Once we were clear ATC cleared us down to FL330 and we continued on to ZZZ2. At the time of the initial shear we were talking and did not detect the variations in airspeed that were the telling signs of the shear coming. At the time I did not declare an emergency but looking back at it I would now because we were unable to maintain altitude at the time.		While enroute we were navigating around some weather that was obscured. We were communicating with ATC about the best route to take around the weather. We were not painting much if any precipitation at all angles on the radar. We were on left and right deviations and ZZZ when able. Just after passing the ZZZ1 VOR at FL350. We started to experience some light turbulence which continued to worsen. I turned on continuous ignition and continued to maneuver around the weather the best we could. The VSI (Vertical Speed Indicator) started a small trend of 120 FPM down. Our altitude quickly started to dip 300-400 feet as we encountered an unexpected large downdraft that kicked the autopilot off. The VSI showed 2,000 FPM descent. I continued to hand fly the aircraft but we were unable to maintain airspeed and altitude and began a descent down to FL340. We advised ATC that we were unable to maintain altitude because of the downdraft and that we would be leveling at FL340. ATC then gave us a descent to FL330. We continued down to FL 330 and leveled there. We exited the obscured weather and continued to ZZZ2. Limited visual and pictorial view of the weather as we deviated around it. Access to current weather information through the iPad such as tops reports.		CRJ-900 flight crew reported windshear in cruise resulting in a momentary loss of aircraft control and excursion from assigned altitude.	
1796663	202103	1801-2400	TRI.TRACON	TN				5300		Marginal	Thunderstorm; Turbulence		Night			TRACON TRI	Air Carrier	Medium Transport, Low Wing, 2 Turbojet Eng		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Initial Approach	Vectors	Class D TEB																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Situational Awareness			1796663												ATC Issue All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Automation Aircraft Terrain Warning	In-flight	Flight Crew FLC complied w / Automation / Advisory; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Human Factors; Procedure	Human Factors	On vectors 6 SM northwest of TRI airport at 4500, ATC was vectoring us around weather for the approach. We were level, given a left turn from 270 to 090. On about a 180 heading, we got the GPWS 'caution terrain' followed by 'terrain terrain woop woop pull up' one time. I leveled the wings and executed the terrain escape maneuver. We climbed up to 5,300 feet from 4,500 feet. As soon as I started the escape maneuver, the indications went away. We notified ATC and flew the approach without disruption. This seemed like an erroneous activation as we were above what ATC said was the MVA. But, it was night, so not 100% sure.Either a false warning or a terrain clearance issue. It seemed that we were too high to get this in a level turn. ATC indicated we were 100 feet above their MVA. Responded and leveled the wings and executed the escape maneuver. Suggest to fly at or above MSA rather than the minimum vectoring altitude.				Air carrier Captain reported receiving a GPWS alert while being vectored for the approach.	
1796669	202103	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				13000		IMC	Windshear; Turbulence; Icing		Daylight	4500		Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	EMB ERJ 170/175 ER/LR		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch		Class E ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Distraction; Communication Breakdown; Confusion	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1796669												Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Ground Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control					Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Weather; Human Factors	Weather	I completed a thorough walk around and told the Captain there was a lot of ice on the aircraft and we were going to need to deice. I also recommended we take off with full Anti-ice on and the Captain agreed after a minute. We did a full deice type 1 and 4 everything went as planned. I ran the cold weather cold soaked check list to prepare for deicing. Once Deicing was completed I received all the pertinent information from iceman and wrote it down on a TOLD card. I then completed the deicing check list and we called ground for taxi. I made sure all the deice check list was completed and we taxied out for departure. Myself and the Captain talked about leaving the flaps and slats up or down while taxiing and due to having such a long hold over time we chose to lower flaps to proper flap setting for takeoff knowing that our hold over time would be reduced by 50%. As we pulled onto the runway and did our final configuration check we had a no take off and the Captain adjusted the FMS info for takeoff and the neck check was good to go. We were cleared for takeoff and I did all my flows and the before takeoff check list. Power was set and we began to roll and immediately the Captain was going left and right on the runway due to the winds being so strong and at this point I put my feet on the pedals getting ready to help the Captain. We reached V1 and I called out V1 then rotate. As soon as we rotated next thing I know is we are going left of the runway due to the winds being so strong at this point I don't remember if I called out positive rate and this allowing the Captain to call gear up. The next thing I remember is calling out acceleration and the Captain calling out flight level change and auto pilot on. At this point we are getting bumped all around due to the severe weather.  The Captain then says flaps and I raise them watching the airspeed as we are bumped around.  I call out speed since we are getting close to the speed warning and the Captain does not have time to get the nose up higher to stop this warning from happening then we notice all of a sudden we have a packs off message and the Captain says the packs are off and I state they are on.  He resets them but to no avail.  They continue to show off.  At this time the Captain says the gear is down and raises it while we are being thrown all around. I ask ATC if we can level off at 11,000 and they clear us to level off at 11,000.  We are still getting pounded and unable to maintain 11,000.  I reach for the QRH as the Captain calls for the QRH between 11,000 and 13,000 the packs off warning message goes away and due to already being at 13,000, ATC asks if we would like to continue to climb and I state yes after looking over at the Captain and he nodded his head to say yes. I then ask about the weather and they state the weather will calm down above 20,000. We are then cleared to climb to 23,000 and I say to the Captain when we are above 20,000 I'm sorry I don't recall if I called out positive rate allowing the Captain to call for gear up and the Captain responds that he did call for gear up and he always does. But I don't recall any call out for gear up. I would really like to hear the recording just to see what all was said while we were being battered around. Everything ended up normal from this point on.  Once we landed our flight attendant stated the passengers were not happy with the take off and were screaming.Unfortunately, there is not much we could have done on this take off that we did not already do. We had already talked to ground to see if other aircraft had taken off and they said everything was fine with the first 3 departures. We checked the winds with ACARS including gusts and ACARS showed the take off to all be within tolerances. I wish I remembered if I had called out positive rate and had heard gear up but I did not and don't remember.				Air Carrier flight crew reported severe turbulence on takeoff resulting in a speed deviation.	
1796914	202103	1801-2400	ZKC.ARTCC	KS		6		38000			Turbulence		Daylight			Center ZKC	Air Carrier	Commercial Fixed Wing		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZKC																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1796914	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 150; Flight Crew Type 1700					1796934	Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Weather	Weather	Cruise FL 380 above a high undercast, seatbelt sign on, passengers and flight attendants seated. The ride had been smooth when we suddenly encountered light to moderate turbulence.  I checked the weather radar, which was tilted down 1 degree and there were no returns ahead. Concerned there may be embedded precipitation below, I adjusted the tilt further down. This showed green returns ahead embedded in the undercast. These returns turned yellow as we approached.  Concerned about overflying the embedded returns, the First Officer and I discussed deviation options.  During the discussion, we encountered rapidly intensifying airspeed and altitude fluctuations.  The aircraft began an un-commanded climb and rapid acceleration.  The First Officer immediately notified Air Traffic Control of severe turbulence and requested a higher altitude commensurate with the climb. ATC granted the higher altitude. I disconnected the autopilot and autothrottles but was unable to prevent an overspeed condition. In retrospect, the event may better be described as severe wave action. There was a feel of a slight and smooth aircraft loading, but not the significant jarring action typically associated with turbulence. The airspeed and altitude variations continued and we requested a block altitude which ATC immediately granted. With the rapid increase and loss of airspeed, aircraft control became my first priority.  We were visually above the undercast and any possible embedded weather, so I elected not to increase aircraft loading by turning or banking the aircraft. I continued straight and may have inadvertently flown over convective activity embedded below.  Discussions with the Flight Attendants after the encounter indicated they did not feel the ride was too bad and they had not received any complaints or comments from the passengers. There were no injuries, damage or passenger complaints. When the ride settled down we notified Company via ACARS of the event and continued to our destination. Appropriate logbook writeups were made and an aircraft inspection performed by Maintenance. While operating with manual radar in areas of possible convective activity embedded below, more aggressive use of the radar tilt and occasional look downs may allow more time for deviation planning.		While in cruise at FL 380, we were having a mostly smooth ride.  As we began to encounter some light chop to light turbulence we seated the flight attendants and passengers and asked ATC for more ride reports ahead.   The last report from ATC said they had nothing more than light chop ahead so far.  We had elected to stay at FL 380 due to the ride and being bit too heavy earlier in the flight for FL 400. We were above a continuous cloud deck far below us with multiple cloud layers ahead of us, but all below us.  Approaching SPI VORTAC we discussed the clouds ahead and their height relative to us and whether or not we should proceed along our route or take a turn to the north to avoid possible turbulence associated with them.  We could not see any specific build ups, but turned the radar on and began scanning ahead for possible convective activity or embedded thunderstorms in the clouds. Before we could make a turn and before reaching the clouds ahead, we experienced a rapid increase in airspeed.   The Captain immediately tried to reduce power before we started to overspeed. Once we began to overspeed it was apparent we had encountered a very heavy mountain wave or turbulence, and due to the loss of airspeed control I immediately told Center we had encountered severe turbulence. As the Captain worked to control our speed, our altitude began to fluctuate above our cruise altitude about 300 feet. I asked the Captain if he wanted to climb with the wave and asked Center for FL 390.  Center cleared us to FL 390.   As we climbed to FL 390 it became apparent we were not out of the wave, so I asked Center for a block altitude to give us room to work with speed and altitude.  Center gave us a block from FL 390-FL 400.   We then slowly climbed to FL 400 as the wave activity and airspeed fluctuations seemed less as we climbed.  We leveled at FL 400 and the turbulence was mostly gone. We held the block attitude for while until we were sure we were clear of the turbulence. The entire event took place in VMC conditions. The turbulence was not severe in itself, but the mountain wave and rapid uncontrolled airspeed increase was great enough that I felt we needed to call it severe. By the time the event was over, the wind and weather was behind us.  I also felt this would let ATC know we may not be able to hold RVSM altitude separation. ATC was very helpful and we were not in the vicinity of other aircraft during this event. After the event we checked on the flight attendants and verified the passengers were OK and not appearing alarmed.  The flight attendants assured us they were OK and the passengers were not commenting and did not seem alarmed. This event was much more noticeable to us on the flight deck, due to primarily being airspeed related, more than turbulence. After landing, the Captain and I fully debriefed and also debriefed with the flight attendants so they would have a better understanding of what had transpired. This is a glaring reminder for constant vigilance for turbulence and weather. Early warning is key for the safety of the passengers and aircraft.   When we began to discuss the look of clouds ahead and to the left of our course, we should have made a precautionary turn, even when we felt we had enough room above and felt they did not produce a hazard by the look of them. A small turn north may have made all the difference. Also, we did not have any tops reports, which would have helped us, but is also a reminder to not only keep asking for them, but to give them out whenever possible for the safety of other aircraft and crews as well.		Air Carrier Flight Crew reported mountain wave activity inflight.	
1797231	202103	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US				3000			Rain; Turbulence; Windshear; 3			700	1800	TRACON ZZZ	Corporate	Citationjet (C525/C526) - CJ I / II / III / IV		2	Part 135	IFR	Passenger		Initial Climb	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Corporate	Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Flight Instructor	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 40; Flight Crew Total 13300; Flight Crew Type 1800					1797231												Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Weather	Weather	While taxiing out departing ZZZ, ATC advised that a Lear Jet had just reported moderate turbulence along our route.  We acknowledged and briefly discussed the topic and after looking at radar, decided we were safe to depart since our returns showed only moderate rain. After take off and while climbing through 2,000 ft to level at 3,000 ft., we encountered severe turbulence. The aircraft was at 230 knots and began to shake rapidly and violently.  Items in the cabin that were not secured moved about freely and our passengers voiced concern. I reduced power to idle to attempt to slow to maneuvering speed as the airplane violently became marginally controllable. We were assigned a level off at 3,000 ft., however, this seemed an unlikely event as I had tried to level from our turn and pushed forward on the elevator, but the plane continued to climb at idle power with the elevator forward.  When we recovered, we were at 3,900 ft and immediately began our descent back to 3,000 ft as we advised center. The radar only painted green returns and although yellow returns were also adjacent to us, we were at least 10 miles from any of those yellow returns. We were given climb instructions as we leveled at 3,000 and given a controller change.  As we climbed and began to top some of the clouds 9,000 feet we encountered a similar event in essentially clear air just at the tops of the clouds in level flight. Again, I reduced power to idle and tried to maintain wings level and altitude, but the aircraft was only marginally controllable and violently being pitched and rolled. Both events lasted approximately 10-20 seconds and we then reported both events to ATC center as severe turbulence.  Again, radar showed nothing significant on our second encounter.  Once on the ground, the aircraft was inspected for damage and none was found.				Flight Crew flying CE-525 aircraft report severe turbulence after takeoff.	
1798849	202104	0601-1200	ZAB.ARTCC	NM							Turbulence					Center ZAB		Commercial Fixed Wing				IFR			Climb; Cruise; Descent		Class A ZAB																																																															Facility ZAB.ARTCC		Government	Enroute	Air Traffic Control Fully Certified	Air Traffic Control Time Certified In Pos 1 (yrs) 13					1798849												ATC Issue All Types; Ground Event / Encounter Ground Equipment Issue; Inflight Event / Encounter Other / Unknown			Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance	ATC Equipment / Nav Facility / Buildings; Procedure	ATC Equipment / Nav Facility / Buildings	Busy traffic, bad rides, frequency congestion, then GPS jamming.  I asked the Supervisor to lower the sector traffic numbers to reflect volume and complexity. Supervisor called Traffic Management Unit and they didn't lower the sector traffic numbers. Aircraft are greatly affected by the GPS jamming and its not taken seriously by management, we've been told we can't ask to stop jamming and to just put everyone on headings. Limit the length and what time of the day that facilities can GPS jam and have it taken seriously when we call and ask them to stop.				A Center Controller reported GPS jamming in their airspace during busy periods created an unsafe sector operation.	
1799686	202104	1801-2400	ZZZZ.Airport	FO				15000		IMC	Thunderstorm; Turbulence						Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb																										Pressurization System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 244; Flight Crew Total 4699; Flight Crew Type 3101		Workload			1799686												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Automation Aircraft Other Automation; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	Flight ZZZZ - ZZZ1 (all nighter). Runway XXL departure IMC conditions with TS (thunderstorms) in terminal area. Crew briefed to remain seated due to expected turbulence on departure. Passengers briefed in welcome aboard announcement that the Flight Attendants would remain seated due to rain showers and bumps on departure. Performance for A/C weight of 145k, Flaps [set], Runway XXL bleeds on reduced thrust T/O. Normal T/O executed with a climb to cleared altitude FL230 commenced. Passing approximately 11,000 [ft., high elevation airport]. Cabin Alt Red warning light illuminated and horn sounded. Horn silenced - pressurization panel checked - and confirmed correct positioning of switches as terrain noted and communication coordinated with ATC for 'safe' altitude and significant weather avoidance required. Aircraft climbing at normal rate and leveling at 11,000 [ft.] was not an option. O2 masks donned and immediate action items of QRC executed. Remainder of checklist executed as Flying Pilot coordinated deviation with ATC and Captain with regard to safe altitude and course for primarily terrain avoidance and significant weather avoidance as a secondary threat. 17,000 ft. deemed to be safest altitude based on position of aircraft. Requested and set. Executed checklist items concurrently with NO resolution to regaining aircraft pressurization. All indications were normal, but obvious that aircraft was not pressuring at all. Plan determined by flight crew to coordinate safe corridor passage through the 'valley' - again based on terrain primary and weather secondary. Coordination with FAs indicated that passenger O2 had deployed automatically (and of note was deployed manually too during procedure), and that crew and passengers were 'safe'. ACARS NO COMM was illuminated likely due to A/C altitude and mountains blocking signals. Crew coordinated for lower altitudes as safe MORA (Minimum Off-Route Altitude) conditions permitted to account for passenger O2 eventually being depleted. Initially 15,000 [ft.], then 12,000 [ft.] coordinated to attempt to get cabin altitude below 10,000 [ft.]. At 12,000 ft. with the pack switches selected to high - crew was able to get the cabin altitude to below 10,000 ft. On initial communication with the cabin crew - communication with the forward cabin was nearly impossible due to loud noise by door 1L (our apparent source of no pressurization). Crew communication conducted via aft galley inter phone to determine crew and passenger status. Cockpit crew determined return to ZZZZ not best course of action due to terrain, weather, heavy weight of aircraft and passenger O2 depletion. ACARS comm with Dispatch established as determination for diversion and potential diversionary airports weighed. Both ZZZZ1 and ZZZZ2 considered. ZZZZ1 determined to have more threats due to lack of familiarity, time and rising terrain by the airport. ZZZZ2 considered and subsequently determined (in coordination with Dispatch) due to familiarity, the fact that we have been routinely using it as an alternate for ZZZZ flights, safe passage from our current location to airport at 12,000 ft. and relatively easy weather avoidance to get to airport. Aircraft integrity was addressed and deemed not to be an issue to choose the more distant diversionary airport. Coordination with ATC-1 and ATC-2 Control completed to receive priority handling (even though very few aircraft were in the sector). Coordination with Dispatch continued throughout with updated runway data, weather and ZZZZ2 [company] ground operations taken care of. Clearance given to descend for the ILS [Runway] XXR approach to ZZZZ2. Crew understood and briefed overweight landing procedures for ZZZZ2 having previously agreed that the threat of landing at 133,000 lbs outweighed the threat of remaining airborne for another 45 min to burn down below max landing weight. Of note, on descent through 7,000 ft. a loud sound [was] heard in [the] cockpit - aircraft integrity deemed safewith no abnormal engine indications. Aircraft executed a normal approach and landing to Runway XYR in ZZZZ2. Upon arrival maintenance items entered for O2 deployed, aircraft pressurization, and overweight landing. Upon post flight it was determined the aircraft had at least two bird strikes which were also entered in maintenance log book. Maintenance quickly determined that door 1L had failed to seat properly but had given a closed condition indication (never any door indication illumination in the cockpit). Challenges existed in ZZZZ2 for both crew and passengers due to aircraft flight originating in [foreign country] with current COVID restrictions in place and requiring adherence.				B-737 Captain reported executing a challenging mechanical diversion due to pressurization failure during climb. The diversion was concluded with an uneventful overweight landing.	
1801007	202104	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US	0	2		1100		VMC	Turbulence; Rain; Haze / Smoke; 6		Dusk	3500		CTAF ZZZ	Personal	Beech F90		2	Part 91	VFR	Training		Final Approach	Visual Approach	Class D ZZZ																													Personal	Skyhawk 172/Cutlass 172		1	Part 91	VFR	Training		Other Pattern	Visual Approach	Class D ZZZ																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Personal	Single Pilot; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Flight Instructor; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Commercial; Other Remote	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 30; Flight Crew Total 6200; Flight Crew Type 1300		Situational Awareness			1801007												Conflict NMAC; Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence	Horizontal 0; Vertical 300		Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Airport; Aircraft; Human Factors; Environment - Non Weather Related	Human Factors	Arriving ZZZ on IFR plan.  Initially wanted to perform GPS XX due to rain and some lower clouds scattered around airport area.approximately 10 nm south of field, called TRACON and informed them I had the field in sight and would cancel IFR after acknowledgement contacted ZZZ Traffic via CTAF.Two aircraft were in pattern for [Runway] XX, I called south of field 7 nm and informed traffic that I would remain east of field at 1,600 feet MSL until I had traffic in sight.I flew what amounted to a left downwind for Runway XY and picked up a Cessna departing upwind for crosswind off of XX.  we were in contact with each other when I was alerted to a helicopter below via a radio transmission from that traffic north east bound away from field in a position below me with no alt read out or announced, and a second pattern aircraft a piper on final for XX which i assumed was in the pattern training but never got in sight.While remaining VMC avoiding some lower scud clouds in area and flying through light rain, looking for both the helicopter and piper, I lost visual with the Cessna on downwind for XX, during my maneuvering I lost position awareness believing I was due north of the field and made a base turn calling base to final XX for a full stop landing, on the descent I noticed the Cessna coming towards my position.  he was calling downwind for XX, he was level and I didn't think he could see me due to our closing angle so I descended to remain clear of his path, calling traffic in sight and called final XX continued to the landing...I had inadvertently aligned for XY and was not in the position I announced, I was free from traffic and made a routine otherwise uneventful landing.During my after landing self-critique I realized / reviewed my errors:1)FPL mode on G1000 PFD and MFD displays were not showing real time position due to the cursor mode and Runway XX GPS approach data being left on screen.2) Navigation around some lower clouds and traffic had me looking around and distracted me from dialing in the new runway alignment which is typical practice for our operations.3) Looking for traffic and distractions led to the disconnect between my perceived and actual position.Corrective actions:  will be to enter traffic patterns from more typical locations instead of shorter direct to downwind entry, as well as dialing in correct landing runway orientation or approach guidance to verify against my position when approaching uncontrolled fields.				Beech F90 pilot reported an NMAC during final approach.	
1801174	202104	0601-1200	ZME.ARTCC	TN						VMC	Turbulence					Center ZME	Air Carrier	Boeing Company Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121				Descent		Class A ZME																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument			Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1801174												ATC Issue All Types; Conflict NMAC			Automation Aircraft RA; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Human Factors; Procedure	Human Factors	Direct to LIT on the HOBRKK3 arrival at FL390. ZME Controller started a step down descent from 390 to 370, then 350, then 330. Same controller handed us off to another sector while descending to FL330. I was the PM (Pilot Monitoring) and checked in with the new sector. After 3 attempts the controller responded and asked if we were descending to 330 as if he was not sure why we were descending to 330. He immediately turned us to a SE vector due to traffic. TCAS generated a TCAS ALERT on a target under us. The TCAS alert generated a miss distance of <400 feet. I queried the center controller and informed him that I was concerned about the previous controller descending us into another flight directly below us. He was hesitant to respond, and in my opinion possibly didn't know we were descending to the FL330 altitude. Why, I don't know. I called ZME and had a conversation with a Supervisor and he told me a higher level manager would review the event and call me back later in the day but I never received a call back. I discussed this event with Captain (Name) and we both agreed that a conversation would be appropriate and to follow up with this  report. Obviously I do not have access to radar or audio recordings of the event and this narrative is the best info I can provide based on our experience. My crew followed each command from Memphis Center. I do not have internal ZME communication/coordination practices.				Air Carrier Flight Crew reported TCAS Alert on descent.	
1802945	202104	1201-1800	ZSE.ARTCC	WA						IMC	Turbulence		Daylight			Center ZSE	Other unknown	Airliner 99			Part 91		Ferry / Re-Positioning		Cruise	Direct	Class A ZSE; Class E ZSE																								Attitude Indicator(Gyro/Horizon/ADI)		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Contracted Service	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Commercial			Workload; Troubleshooting; Confusion; Distraction; Human-Machine Interface			1802945												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Became Reoriented; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft; Weather	Aircraft	While moving a plane on the first leg, , after entering a downdraft and pitching up (it seemed like I would have lost approximately 200 to 300 feet without any control input, the attitude indicator wobbled then started rolling. Since it was IMC with turbulence, it took me a minute to fully get it under control using the copilot instrument. In that time my altitude was +/- 200 EEFT, and heading +/- 30 degrees, never more than half a dot or a dot off of course. When I was able to get the aircraft under control, I advised ATC that I had an attitude indicator failure and requested a higher altitude of 15,000 feet where I knew it was VMC conditions. When I initiated the climb, the Captain side attitude indicator stabilized, and showed the correct attitude. When I got to my requested altitude and made sure the aircraft was stable and going to stay in visual conditions, I watched the Captain side attitude indicator much more closely, and noted that it would start indicating a climb after approximately 5 to 10 minutes if I didn't pull the 'quick set' knob. Then advised ATC that it was under control and I didn't need any further assistance. After watching the attitude indicator and setting a timer to remember to pull it every 4 to 5 minutes, and verifying with the co-pilot side instruments, I decided that it was safe to continue to the destination where I could talk to Maintenance. As long as the weather didn't deteriorate, if it did then I would have advised ATC and found a better altitude or somewhere closer to land if necessary. At one point I did descend to a lower altitude in a sky clear area to get below the upcoming cloud layer, and not have to use as much oxygen. I had more than enough but it can make my nose dry and be an extra distraction. The rest of the flight continued with no issues, and I was able to get a new attitude indicator at my destination. We tested the new one  and continued  with no issues from any instruments.   I didn't see the altitude go more than +/- 200 feet, but I'm selecting 'altitude deviation' because it's possible that I missed it altitude while trying to stabilize the aircraft's heading/roll.				A pilot reported after temporarily losing control of the aircraft entering a downdraft the Captain side attitude indicator began malfunctioning.	
1803672	202104	1201-1800	ZZZ.TRACON	US				7000		IMC	Cloudy; Turbulence; 10		Daylight	4600		TRACON ZZZ	Personal	M-20 F Executive 21		2	Part 91	IFR	Personal		Cruise; Descent	Direct	Class E ZZZ																								AC Generator/Alternator			Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Personal	Instructor; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Flight Instructor; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 90; Flight Crew Total 4764; Flight Crew Type 127		Troubleshooting; Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1803672												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	While on an IFR Flight from ZZZ to ZZZ1 in cruise flight in IMC conditions, the aircraft experienced a total loss of electrical power. Aircraft was being piloted by a Private Pilot receiving instrument instruction from me. The STEC 60 autopilot was engaged and managing the both navigation and altitude control. When the electrical failure occurred, the autopilot disengaged and aircraft began a descending turn that was recognized by the avionics powering down and the airspeed starting to increase. As the Instructor, I announced that I had the controls and initiated partial panel IMC operations. Focusing on the principals of Aviate, Navigate and Communicate, I began controlling the airspeed to maintain level flight and shallowed the angle of bank until I achieved what appeared to be level flight after approximately 150 degrees of heading change.The Pilot (Instrument Student) asked if he should get the iPad and the AHRS up from ForeFlight which I agreed was the right next step. We confirmed that we were in level flight and executed a shallow bank to reestablish the desired easterly course using the partial panel information and continued to backup with ForeFlight. Next, I called for the pilot to turn off the Master switch and then turn it back on to reestablish the main power. Then I called for him to activate the Avionics Master switch which reestablished all of the avionics. The alternator was not restored so we shed the electrical load and choose not to change anything else since we had the flight instruments and the communications/transponder operational. We confirmed that all of circuit breakers were in and none were popped.We were able to update ATC on the situation and we entered VMC conditions after about 5 minutes. All systems were operational except the alternator. We worked with ATC to change the clearance to ZZZ2 for support and did not feel that we needed emergency equipment when they asked. I also asked for a phone number to ZZZ3 Center for ATC in case we lost communication or electrical again. The weather at ZZZ2 was:[Date] XA:53 METAR ZZZ2 [Date]XA53Z 20008KT 10SM OVC032 11/07 A2988 [Date] XA:53 METAR ZZZ2 [Date]XA53Z 19006KT 10SM FEW022 SCT036 OVC044 12/07 A2987 We maintained visual flight control for the rest of the flight to ZZZ2 in case we lost electrical again. We had been cleared direct to ZZZZZ for the approach to Runway XX. We did lose electrical/communication again due to the battery discharging just before we arrived at ZZZ1. We called ZZZ3 Center on the cell phone, they helped manage us and gave us a phone number to the ZZZ2 Tower. We advised ZZZ2 we had the field in sight and asked for clearance to land. The rest of the flight was uneventful and ATC gave us progressive instructions to the FBO via the cell phone. At the FBO, the I removed the cowling, visually inspected the major electrical components and verified that there were no loose wires or indicated damage to the components. Maintenance was called but none was readily available and no one seemed to have a 12V Auxiliary Power Unit. I powered up the aircraft to see if all of the systems would come back online - all systems came back online including the alternator. An assessment was made that all systems were operational and the next two legs of the flight would be performed in Visual Flight Conditions. Additional Info: The aircraft was just purchased and was being flown by the new owner and the instructor back from ZZZ4 to ZZZ5. The pre-buy inspection had been completed the week prior at a Mooney Service Center in [name removed] State. There were no known or observed issues with the electrical system. ATC was outstanding in their assistance, handling and communication.				Flight Instructor reported that the loss of all electrical power resulted in a diversion, and a precautionary emergency landing.	
1804019	202104	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US	289	5		1800		IMC	Rain; Turbulence; 2		Daylight	1500		TRACON ZZZ	Personal	Skyhawk 172/Cutlass 172		1	Part 91	IFR	Training	GPS	Initial Approach	Vectors	Class E ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	FBO	Pilot Not Flying; Instructor	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Flight Instructor; Flight Crew Commercial; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 124; Flight Crew Total 1380; Flight Crew Type 435		Distraction; Fatigue; Situational Awareness; Training / Qualification; Workload; Confusion			1804019	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Personal	Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Student; Flight Crew Commercial	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 17.3; Flight Crew Total 277.1; Flight Crew Type 100		Situational Awareness; Time Pressure; Training / Qualification; Workload; Distraction; Confusion			1804065	Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Automation Air Traffic Control; Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Air Traffic Control Issued Advisory / Alert; Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew FLC complied w / Automation / Advisory	Chart Or Publication; Airspace Structure; Environment - Non Weather Related; Human Factors; Procedure; Weather	Human Factors	I was acting as a flight instructor on board Aircraft X, a Cessna 172S on a local IFR flight practicing approaches. My student is a Certified Flight Instructor (CFI) candidate who was gaining instrument currency and proficiency. We began with an ILS approach at ZZZ1 with my student flying the aircraft and me handling ATC communications to reduce his workload in the hard IMC conditions. We crossed a cold front while receiving vectors for the ILS approach where we encountered light to moderate turbulence and precipitation. We completed the ILS approach without incident and proceeded to the VOR for the VOR-A approach into ZZZ, planning to complete a Low Approach Only. We experienced severe turbulence which forced the autopilot (GFC700) to disconnect and I took the controls of the aircraft and requested deviations to the north as necessary to track clear of the progressing cold front. Once we got into smoother air, I gave my student the flight controls and we proceeded direct ZZZ2 [VOR] for a procedure turn. Our plan was to complete the VOR-A approach and then receive radar vectors for the RNAV 01 at ZZZ. My student had proclaimed that he wanted to avoid going back through the cold front while getting vectored for the RNAV 01 approach and suggested we circle to land at ZZZ and cancel the IFR flight plan. When my student suggested this, we had passed the Final Approach Fix inbound and began a descent to the Minimum Descent Altitude and were switched over to advisory frequency. I switched frequencies back to Approach to tell them our intentions to try and circle to land since they were expecting us to go missed on the practice approach. When I told ATC of our intentions, they replied in the affirmative and added 'Aircraft X, low altitude alert, check altitude immediately, altitude indicates 1,800.' I replied the altitude checks and requested an altimeter setting. ATC replied with an altimeter setting of 29.58 and we had a setting of 29.67 set in. I figured this was why they gave us the low altitude alert but then quickly realized that the Minimum Descent Altitude for the segment of the approach we were on was 2,020 feet and we were at 1,720 feet with the new altimeter setting. I informed my student to return to 2,020 feet and we continued on the approach without issue and proceeded with the missed approach with vectors to the RNAV01 at ZZZ. During the missed approach, I was surprised at myself that I did not catch that the student had descended below the Minimum Descent Altitude and I assessed why that had happened during a period of smooth flight and during the post flight discussion with my student. During the lead up to the approach, the encounter with the severe turbulence had shaken both me and my student. We also had a back seat passenger who was an instrument student who had never been in IMC before. I was concerned with the safety of my student and the back seat student and began thinking about the circle to land approach and how we would request vectors for the RNAV Approach if we were unable to circle to land in order to stay away from the front line. I had determined from the weather when we departed ZZZ that circling for the runway at ZZZ would have been nearly impossible with the current ceilings. I told my student this and informed him we would need to execute the RNAV Approach in order to make it back into the airport.The workload at the moment was very high and the effects of the turbulence certainly could have impaired multitasking skills for both my student and I. When we began the descent on the approach, we left 3,000 feet for 2,020 feet which is the Minimum Descent Altitude until a fix which then the Minimum Descent Altitude decreases to 1,760 feet. My student did not reset the altitude select when we ran our Final Approach Fix check and the altitude select remained at 3,000 feet. Additionally, when I recognized we were below the published Minimum Descent Altitude, I should have instructed my student to execute the missed approach as 91.175(E)(1)(i) states, rather than just climbing back up to the Minimum Descent Altitude.This flight was also my third flight in hard IMC this day, and this VOR approach was my 8th approach in IMC in a span of 6 hours. I am an experienced Flight Instructor with over 45 hours in actual instrument conditions and over 150 approach logged, but never before had I completed so many IMC flights in such a time span with the challenging conditions of the frontal passage. My self-assessment prior to this flight determined I was able to complete the flight successfully with good quality of instruction to the student, but the extraordinary conditions we experienced during the flight quickly eroded that ability for me, in addition to the highly divided workload situation we found ourselves in while inbound on the VOR Approach which led to the descent below Minimum Descent Altitude and failure to execute the missed approach immediately after. I think also a more comprehensive briefing of the approach would have aided in my student's short term memory of the initial difference of Minimum Descent Altitude. Even though I briefed the Minimum Descent Altitudes, I do not think they were retained due to the stress and workload of the situation. If this event were to occur again, I would better instruct my student to execute the missed approach rather than working to reclaim the missed Minimum Descent Altitude.		The aircraft had 3 people on board for a local approaches flight in IMC conditions in Aircraft. This flight took place while a stationary front was passing through the area. This caused for almost constant moderate turbulence at times during the flight close to the frontal passage. The flight started with an ILS approach into ZZZ1. After the ILS approach we were vectored to go to the ZZZ2 VOR . Along the way, we had to deviate twice for turbulence. This, as well as IMC conditions, was causing a good amount of sensory overload for myself and the flight instructor with me. At times during the flight the autopilot was being used, but at one point, the amount of turbulence actually caused the autopilot to disengage. Because of this, at the time of the deviation, I was hand flying the aircraft while the other pilot was loading the VOR Approach. When we proceeded inbound on the approach, there is a step down to a minimum of 2,020 feet until an 8 DME fix. Then the minimum goes lower to 1,760 feet. With the sensory overload that both I and the other pilot were experiencing during the IMC flight, we ended up descending below the altitude minimum below the fix. ATC had not updated our altimeter setting in a decent amount of time. We were called by ATC letting us know for a low altitude and to immediately recover. They gave us an updated altimeter and our altitude after the correction showed it being right at the approach total minimum. Instead of executing an immediate missed approach we climbed back up to 2020 feet then re-descended to the 1,760 minimums. By doing this we unfortunately broke 14 CFR 91.175 (c) 1, 3 and (e) 1. The proper action would have been to either, not conduct the flight in the first place or, to immediately execute the missed approach once realizing the descent below the step down. We also could have stopped the flight after the ILS approach into ZZZ1. The rest of the flight utilized great CRM, but during this approach, a better action to even ask as the approach started would have been to double check the approach and its minimums including each step down.		Instructor and student reported descending below Minimum Descent Altitude, which they attributed to an incorrect altimeter setting, and turbulence.	
1804190	202104	0001-0600	ZZZ.ARTCC	US						IMC	Icing; Turbulence					Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	MD-11		2	Part 121	IFR	Cargo / Freight / Delivery	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								Airspeed Indicator		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument						1804190												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Aircraft; Weather	Aircraft	We were flying in IMC and received an IAS comparator on both the Captain and F/O (First Officer) PFD (Primary Flight Display). About 30 seconds later the autopilot disconnected. We knew that we had an Unreliable Airspeed. The F/O continued to fly the plane and we went through [the] unreliable airspeed checklist.  We then spoke with ATC and received a block altitude. We then went through the QRH procedure for unreliable airspeed. We determined that the Captain's airspeed was inoperative. We completed the checklist pulling from the F/O CADC (Central Air Data Computer). We got #2 Autopilot and NAV working. The Captain landed the airplane without any issues.Cause: We believe the Captain's pitot tube could have been frozen when we were flying in IMC.				MD-11 Captain reported his airspeed indicator became unreliable, possibly due to pitot icing.	
1804585	202105	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US		0	5			VMC	Turbulence; Windshear; 15		Daylight	9000		Tower ZZZ	Personal	SR22		2	Part 91	None	Training		Landing	Visual Approach	Class D ZZZ																								Main Gear		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Personal	Pilot Not Flying; Instructor	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Flight Instructor	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 28; Flight Crew Total 4952; Flight Crew Type 468		Human-Machine Interface			1804585	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Personal	Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Private; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 27; Flight Crew Total 1326; Flight Crew Type 219.6		Human-Machine Interface			1804618	Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Ground Event / Encounter Ground Strike - Aircraft; Ground Excursion Runway; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control			Person Flight Crew	Other Landing	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Human Factors; Weather	Human Factors	After an Instrument Proficiency Check and Biennial Flight Review with the aircraft owner the last landing of the day was proceeding normally with the owner/operator pilot-flying. There was a crosswind on final approach but nothing abnormal. As the aircraft was entering the flare and about 5 feet above the runway (with power at idle, on centerline, at 75 knots) the aircraft nose suddenly slewed about 45 degrees left immediately taking the aircraft off the runway and over the grass. The pilot initiated a go-around with full power. The aircraft touched down on the grass and rolled straight for several hundred feet before the right wing settled to the ground and the aircraft rotated 90 degrees right and stopped.As instructor I did call for a go-around as directional control seemed lost, but that call was simultaneous with the pilot initiating a go-around. I cannot explain the initial sudden yawing moment. I speculate that the plane was hit with a gust during the flare and there was inadequate energy, even with full power, to keep the plane from contacting the grass. The attempt to prevent the plane from settling onto the grass caused a nose high attitude, along with full flaps, probably created excessive drag preventing acceleration.The METAR at the time showed wind 30009KT The 3 METARS before the incident showed wind 24011G17KT to 28009G14KT. The landing METAR was well within the Demonstrated Cross Wind for a Cirrus SR-22, but perhaps unexpected gusts were starting as we landed.There were no injuries but the airport was closed for several hours.		Just prior to touch down on Runway 20 a wind gust pushed the airplane extremely hard to the left, I initiated a go around but was unable to climb and regain control. I pulled power back and landed in a grass area. The airplane slid and the right landing gear dug in to the ground and separated from the airplane. The airplane came to a stop in the grass. There were no injuries. I was flying with a CFI at the time and had just completed a Biennial Flight Review and an Instrument Proficiency Check and everything had gone fine, the day was done and were landing at my home airport. I feel the cause was a combination of a strong wind gust and wind shear.		SR-22 pilot and instructor reported a runway excursion during landing.	
1805293	202105	1801-2400	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				29000			Thunderstorm; Turbulence					Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	EMB ERJ 145 ER/LR		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise																										Flight Dynamics Navigation and Safety		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine			Human-Machine Interface			1805293												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Fuel Issue; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence		N	Automation Aircraft Other Automation; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued Advisory / Alert; Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Aircraft; Weather	Weather	During our flight from ZZZ to ZZZ1 severe weather storms had begun to move into our course of route. ATC advised us of the weather and gave us a reroute to the ZZZ2 VOR which was significantly off route from our original flight plan. Unfortunately, after re-calulating our new flight plan and fuel, we noticed that due to our reroute we would be landing at ZZZ1 with minimum fuel. After getting our new performance numbers we slowed down to long range cruise and advised dispatch and ATC of our fuel situation. Letting them know that if we continued to fly to the ZZZ2 VOR that we would be landing with min fuel. We were given 'deviations as necessary direct ZZZ1 when able'. Our radar showed two storm cells about 3-5 miles in diameter. We decided to deviate around these cells and proceed direct ZZZ1 once clear of the storm. While navigating around these storms we began to experience updrafts and downdrafts. During our course of route the autopilot was engaged and suddenly began to deviate from FL290 by +800/-300 ft. The VSI (Vertical Speed Indicator) showed a climb and descent of +/-4,000 feet per min and the cockpit windows became fogged. During this time the airplane did not feel like it was pitching this extreme and did not exceed +5/-2 degrees. Altitude miscompare flags appeared on our PFD'S (Primary Flight Display) and the VSI had a RED X for less than 5 seconds. I began to run the QRH. ATC contacted us about our altitude and asked if we were having a rough ride. We responded and advised of the situation and were cleared to descend to FL240 at pilot's discretion. After the airplane was stable at FL290 we descended to FL240 and landed at ZZZ1 with 2,400 pounds of fuel and no further incidents.				EMB-145 flight crew reported severe weather in cruise.	
1805314	202105	0601-1200	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				41000		IMC	Turbulence		Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Taxi	Learjet 60		2	Part 91	IFR	Ferry / Re-Positioning		Cruise		Class A ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Taxi	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1805314	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Taxi	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1805315	Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Ground Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overrode Automation; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Weather	Weather	We were deviating to the southeast, around weather northwest of ZZZ, with the use of on board radar. We were not painting weather on the course we selected. Shortly after asking to deviate the aircraft began to climb. I turned off the autopilot and maintained wings level. Immediately thereafter we encountered numerous updrafts and downdrafts making it difficult for me to maintain altitude and airspeed. I did the best I could to maintain wings level and 'rode out' the turbulence which resulted in altitude deviations. The aircraft also banked left and right however the bank never exceeded 30 degrees in either direction. The entire encounter lasted approximately two minutes.In the future I will take on extra fuel and alter my flight plan and/or course to avoid areas of large precipitation / weather in their entirety.		We were at FL410.  We knew about the weather before we left.  There were some high top reports but not many.  We got closer to the line and asked for FL430.  We started on top but then went into IMC conditions.  We had our radar on the 100 mile range and pointed down so that the 50 mile line was painting the ground and any storms that were between 50 nm and 100 nm.  If there is a storm between the 50 nm and 100 nm and it is severe the ground behind it will not paint.  It will be black.  Also, if the storm crosses the 50 nm line and starts to disappear then you will top that storm.  We deviated to the left of course to an area that looked clear and was disappearing as we got closer.  So we should have topped it.  We got into the top of a buildup.  First Officer turned off the auto pilot and hand flew.  We got some rain and an updraft followed by a down draft and then numerous up and down drafts. we went up to 43,300 and I saw 41,800 on the down side.  The event lasted about 90 seconds.In the future, look to go-around the line or cross the line where there are lower tops.		Lear 60 flight crew reported a momentary loss of aircraft control while flying through cloud tops and encountering up- and downdrafts.	
1805443	202104	1201-1800	ZZZ.TRACON	US						IMC	Turbulence; 4		Daylight	900		TRACON ZZZ; UNICOM ZZZ	Personal	PA-46 Malibu/Malibu Mirage/Malibu Matrix		1	Part 91	IFR	Personal		Final Approach	Direct	Class E ZZZ; Class G ZZZ																								Flight Director		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Personal	Single Pilot; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 44; Flight Crew Total 1660; Flight Crew Type 527		Situational Awareness; Troubleshooting			1805443												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overrode Automation; Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Aircraft; Human Factors; Weather	Human Factors	After an uneventful instrument cross-country flight, I listened to the destination airport ASOS, Wind 29 knot, Gusting 38, clouds OC 900 feet and Visibility 4 sm. I set up the FD (Flight Director) for the RNAV approach into the un-towered airport. I had done this approach before in marginal VFR conditions.Approach cleared me for the approach. With the autopilot coupled to the FD I passed the IAF in heavy IMC and turned inbound at the IF. Approach cleared me to switch to the UNICOM frequency, which I did.  I noticed that the autopilot was not maneuvering the airplane as I had intended, it was making a climbing left turn off course.  I decoupled the autopilot and hand flew the airplane while attempting to reset the FD. While being distracted trying to reset the FD I inadvertently got off altitude and heading and found myself in an unusual attitude.  I recovered from the unusual attitude and got the airplane stabilized and hand flew the airplane and landed without further incident.I performed well during my recent IPC, but single pilot instrument flying in heavy IMC is not the same as being placed under-the-hood with flight instructor in the right seat. Also, emphasis should be made on (UPRT) Upset Prevention and Recovery Training. I will seek training before doing single pilot low IMC approaches in the future.				PA-46 pilot reported difficulties with the Flight Director resulted in a momentary unusual attitude event in IMC conditions.	
1805802	202104		ZZZ.Airport	US			0			VMC	Turbulence		Daylight			TRACON ZZZ; Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Landing		Class C ZZZ							High																	Emergency Equipment		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	General Seating Area	Air Carrier	Other / Unknown							1805802												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Other / Unknown; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence		N	Person Observer	In-flight	General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft; Environment - Non Weather Related; Weather; Human Factors	Weather	While landing in ZZZ it was the worst turbulence ever experienced on landing. The aircraft had shifted on a sharp tilt aircraft left and sharply right. The door to the Portable Oxygen Bottles (POBs) opened and when the wheels/aircraft touched down with excessive velocity and force it catapulted the POB's forward into the isle of the aircraft. But, the grace of GOD, and physics, no one was injured! The latched locked failed. Passenger X was one of many witnesses, his kids were sitting in aft as well! I don't know how but lord I'm so thankful that no one was hit by the catapulted POB bottles. The landing was furious and as the gusts were up to 35 MPH and flaps and speed were notable not in conjunction of safety. It was clear on a not so perfect hard landing the locks failed easily. A very dangerous situation at the very least. As stated thank the lord no one was injured! The locks on cabinet failed and the brackets also failed to keep the POB's secure. It needs complete checks for the safety of the passengers and crew members alike.				Flight Attendant reported that latches and retaining brackets for the portable oxygen bottles failed on landing, allowing the bottles to come loose and catapult through the cabin.	
1806199	202105		SKBO.ARTCC	FO				40000			Turbulence					Center SKBO	Air Carrier	Commercial Fixed Wing		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1806199												Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General Physical Injury / Incapacitation	Weather	Weather	Shortly after commencing the initial descend from 40,000 on the ISVA3E for SKBO and no weather indicated on the radar, we hit turbulence causing the airplane to rapidly climb and then descend. I observed from the FC seat +/- 800 feet and +/- 10 knots of airspeed. The autopilot was engaged with LNAV/VNAV and VNAV PATH indicated on the FMA. The seatbelt sign was on. After the unexpected turbulence was over, the Captain asked me to call the Flight Attendants. #1 FA stated that it appeared two FAs were injured and that he would call back with the final assessment. A few minutes later #1 FA called back and stated two FAs and a passenger were indeed injured and required medical attention. I briefed the Captain and he directed me to have medics meet us at the gate. Upon arriving at gate, the medics boarded the airplane and only took the FAs to the local hospital.Cause - This was unexpected turbulence. Perhaps now in the summer months with convective weather in the area of SKBO, I will suggest to the Captain ask the FAs to remain seated from descend to landing.Night flying into SKBO could hide weather disturbances not shown in the radar, perhaps have the FAs finish service early and sit from descend to landing.				Air carrier First Officer reported experiencing unexpected turbulence at FL400 during initial descent to SKBO airport.	
1806685	202105	0601-1200	MYR.TRACON	SC	265	10		3000		VMC	Turbulence; 10		Daylight	10000		TRACON MYR	Personal	Small Aircraft, High Wing, 1 Eng, Fixed Gear			Part 91	None	Personal		Cruise	Direct	Class C MYR																								GPS & Other Satellite Navigation		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Personal	Pilot Flying; Single Pilot	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Private	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 20; Flight Crew Total 2490; Flight Crew Type 100		Distraction; Situational Awareness			1806685												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Airspace Violation All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural FAR; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Human Factors; Aircraft; Procedure	Aircraft	I was flying VFR cross country to ZZZ from JZI flying direct with a planned northern deviation towards HYW to avoid MYR airspace. Altitude was 3,000 AGL due to moderate turbulence. As I was nearing MYR airspace the GPS encountered interference and the aircraft indicator on the moving map was blocked (later determined to be ADS-B shadow). When the GPS self corrected, I realized that I was near or touching the outer ring below the 4,000 upper limit and above the 1,200 lower limit. I immediately executed a 180 degree turn and then turned north and skirted the outside ring as planned. I did possibly touch the airspace but did so for only the minute it took to turn away. The certified GPS never gave the warning that it usually does when class airspace is entered, I had to range the GPS out near maximum to even see the touching of the outer ring, so I do not know what the ATC radar would have shown. I do not expect a re-occurrence as I better understand how the ADS-B shadow sometimes disguises the aircraft symbol. I have been flying nearly 40 years and have never had a violation or an airspace incursion.				Pilot reported GPS problems associated with ADS-B issues and a possible airspace violation.	
1806979	202105	0001-0600	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				18000		VMC	Turbulence					Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B767 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb	Vectors	Class A ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 103; Flight Crew Total 130; Flight Crew Type 130		Situational Awareness; Distraction; Workload			1806979	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 25; Flight Crew Total 1829; Flight Crew Type 1829		Distraction; Situational Awareness; Workload			1806991	Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Weather	Weather	Severe turbulence encounter on climb after departing.  We conducted a full and normal briefing in operations prior to the flight. During that briefing we identified a threat of possible turbulence around 20,000 feet based on a PIREP. Upon arriving at the plane, I briefed the lead Flight Attendant. That briefing included a discussion on turbulence. I directed that the Flight Attendants stay seated for the departure until I call. Ground operations through takeoff and climbout were normal and uneventful. Departure cleared us to 17,000 feet. We leveled there awaiting hand off to Center and were in light turbulence. After a short delay we were cleared to climb to approximately 25,000 feet and to contact Center. I initiated the climb via VNAV. Almost immediately we encountered severe turbulence. The airspeed was fluctuating and the plane banked +-25 AOB (Angle of Bank). The autopilot remained engaged and made appropriate corrections. However, I put my hands on the controls in anticipation of needing to take control. The ride was so violent that my First Officer could not get his hands on the radio knob to change frequencies. By 18,000 feet the ride eased to moderate turbulence. When I deemed it safe, I then picked up the PA and asked 'Flight Attendants check in.' They checked the cabin and reported all passenger, crew and plane were safe. The First Officer made the check in with Center, reported the severe turbulence.At an appropriate time, I notified dispatch of the event. He published our PIREP. Later we submitted the log entry for maintenance. Further down line ZZZ1 Station was notified and I discussed with them an appropriate response upon landing. The remainder of the flight was uneventful, not even a ripple of turbulence. Once blocked in and checklists completed, I greeted passengers in case any were still concerned. They all seemed very appreciative, and thankful. Many even commented on the smooth landing and offered thanks for a safe trip. Customer Service, Maintenance and Flight Operations and met us at the gate. I provided full debriefs to each. The flight operations manager said we did a great job but also advised we submit a safety report. I want to offer that I believe the system, Standard Operating Procedures, and tools made available work. While I could not foresee the severity of the turbulence that awaited us, we knew a rough ride would be encountered in that area and took precaution. The result was no injuries to crew or passengers or damage to the plane. I offer this report mostly to help improve the system. And I am happy to answer any questions if necessary.		WSI (Weather Service International) indicated a narrow band of turbulence on our route from roughly 17,000 to 20,000 feet due to shear below a jet stream.  Larger jets reported moderate (PIREP symbols) turbulence on WSI so the Captain had the flight attendants remain seated after takeoff until we called them.  At the time of the event the seat belt sign was ON and everyone on the plane was seated.  As we climbed through 17,000 feet the turbulence worsened from moderate to severe.  Large and rapid airspeed deviations of +/- 25 knots and sharp rolling moments of roughly 25 to 30 degrees occurred for roughly 2 minutes, all estimates.  As Pilot Monitoring it took me awhile to tune the ATC radios for a frequency change as I couldn't grab the tuning knobs in the turbulence.  I believe ATC instructed us to climb from 17,000 to 19,000 feet and that put us into the worst of the turbulence.  Once we were cleared to climb higher (above 19,000 feet) the ride improved quickly.  I believe a hand off from TRACON to Center caused much of the delay but I am not certain.Again, the seat belt sign was on and all flight attendants were seated per the briefing.  No injuries and no damage.  We did not make the 'be seated immediately' announcements as everyone was still seated since takeoff.  We were focused on getting a higher altitude and a better ride. Once we were clear of the turbulence we asked the flight attendants to 'check in' per our turbulence training.  We filed a PIREP with ATC and notified Dispatch.		B767 flight crew reported encountering severe turbulence and temporary loss of control.	
1807459	202105	1201-1800	IAD.Airport	DC						VMC	Turbulence; Windshear		Daylight			Tower IAD	Air Carrier	Regional Jet 200 ER/LR (CRJ200)		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Final Approach		Class B IAD																												Tower IAD	Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR			Final Approach		Class B IAD																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine			Distraction; Situational Awareness			1807459												Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach; Flight Crew Became Reoriented	Aircraft; Environment - Non Weather Related; Procedure; Human Factors	Human Factors	Coming into IAD the errors started when we were intercepting the localizer for Runway 1R. I was the pilot monitoring and the FO (First Officer) was the pilot flying. Approach cleared us to intercept when we were basically already crossing the final approach course so we had to navigate back to the localizer on our own. At the same time we had been getting hit with some minor wake turbulence from the airplane ahead of us which Approach informed us was a Boeing 777. We were then cleared for the visual approach and advised of the caution for wake turbulence and to maintain 170 kts. until 5 miles out. At this time we configured gear down flaps 20. I had just recently had training in the sim for wake turbulence and remembered how dangerous it could be so I advised my FO to use caution and I believe I recommended maybe staying above their path since we had already hit a small portion of their wake prior when we were on downwind. He elected to turn the autopilot off and hand fly slightly above their glide path. We then continued down and around 100 ft. got a terrain warning. Our first reaction was this was because we weren't on glide path and the FO called out, 'visual, landing.' around 50 ft. we got another aural message stating 'too low terrain', at this point I realized something was wrong and quickly scanned and noticed we were still at flaps 20. At this point I immediately called for a balked landing. The FO restated in a question, balked landing? I said yes and he immediately executed the maneuver. We advised ATC of the go around and they asked for the reasoning and I stated we were unstable. We continued the go around and came back to land on [Runway] 1R after some vectoring without further incident. To add to this and to summarize, we did not complete a landing checklist, we were not fully configured and were at gear down flaps 20 degrees, and our speed bug was still at 170 kts. We received a terrain aural warning that we misinterpreted followed by a second aural warning and executed a balked landing at a medium energy state with lower power setting but high airspeed. Our attention started deviating from standard starting with the poor intercept angle of the localizer. I further complicated the situation by over concentrating on the wake turbulence from the 777 and focusing too much on the FO's glide path and what I would call tunnel focusing on his flying. I believe my statements to him about the wake turbulence also led to distracting him from normal flight operations as well. He was talking through his thought process on staying a half dot high on the glide path and I was responding with statements like I agree, not necessarily breaking sterile cockpit but also causing more distraction on both of our parts. This was the first time that I had not completed a landing checklist. It has made me realize that while being aware of threats like wake turbulence is important, it's even more important to maintain a greater situational awareness and take a step back and not tunnel focus on one threat while other threats could arise. While I am glad we did not try to force a landing. This is a mistake I never thought would be possible or would happen to me. I have definitely learned a lot from this experience.				CRJ-200 Captain reported distractions from possible wake turbulence resulted in failure to fully configure for landing.	
1807722	202105	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US			0			VMC	Turbulence; Windshear; 10		Daylight	12000		Tower ZZZ	Personal	PA-18/19 Super Cub		1	Part 91	None	Personal		Landing	Direct; Visual Approach	Class D ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Personal	Pilot Flying; Single Pilot	Flight Crew Private	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 8; Flight Crew Total 225; Flight Crew Type 75		Situational Awareness; Training / Qualification			1807722												Ground Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Ground Excursion Runway; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Human Factors; Weather	Human Factors	I was cleared to land. As I came over the end of the runway, I was getting some weird wind shear that was pushing me around, but my approach felt controlled and so I went through with it. Right before I touched down, I got a strong crosswind gust of wind (from the left) that pushed my super cub sideways (nose pointing to the left) as I touched down. Being a tail dragger, the plane responded accordingly, and I began to skid sideways down the runway. I was already down and too sideways for a go-around, so I pulled the throttle all the way back, stick back and left, and rudder full right. The plane continued to skid to a stop sideways on the runway, but all three wheels remained on the runway at all times, and no damage to the runway or aircraft occurred. After coming to a complete stop, I proceeded to turn the plane around to the left, leaving the asphalt for a brief time on the left side of the runway and taxing to the exit ramp on the other side where the Tower then informed me to stop, and I was later met by a foam truck and a service vehicle. I believe that the main factors in this were the wind gusts from the side, which in turn caused me to land sideways. I could have performed a go-around, though in the moment my approach felt controlled up until the last minute. I believe that I took the correct actions on the ground, and in so doing prevented any further damage to the aircraft or to airport property. My plane did not complete a full ground loop, but rather only turned about 90-120 degrees, and the rest of the 'loop' was me taxiing out of my predicament back on to the runway.				PA-18 pilot reported losing directional control on landing roll in a crosswind situation.	
1808674	202105	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US							Turbulence; 50		Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Corporate	King Air C90 E90		1	Part 91	IFR	Passenger		Landing	Visual Approach	Class D ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Corporate	Pilot Flying; Single Pilot	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Flight Instructor; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 30; Flight Crew Total 6000; Flight Crew Type 150		Situational Awareness			1808674												Ground Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Ground Excursion Runway; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence				Other landing roll	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; General Maintenance Action	Weather; Aircraft; Human Factors	Ambiguous	Upon touchdown I applied both brakes and beta on the propellers. The airplane veered to the right and I could not straighten the landing path of the plane. The plane went off the right side of the runway into the dirt between the runways. The airplane was not damaged and there were no injuries. The wind was gusty and variable on landing and there was significant turbulence on the final approach. I am not sure why the incident occurred and I am having the plane inspected. There may have been a tailwind on landing.				King Air pilot reported loss of directional control after landing resulted in a runway excursion.	
1808930	202105	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US		3		3000		Mixed	Rain; Thunderstorm; Turbulence; 3		Daylight	1000		Tower ZZZ	Personal	TBM 700/TBM 850		1	Part 91	IFR	Personal		Final Approach	Direct; Vectors	Class D ZZZ																								Autopilot		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Personal	Single Pilot; Captain	Flight Crew Commercial; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 88; Flight Crew Total 2041; Flight Crew Type 86		Distraction; Situational Awareness; Confusion			1808930												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft; Human Factors	Aircraft	This was not a good flight because of compounding errors that happened and the negative effect that they had.  The good part is that the flight remained safe, but here below are several things that I learned.  The events started when on decent into ZZZ when the lights on the autopilot mode selector began to flicker erratically, however the autopilot appeared to perform normally.  This was in IMC descent thru layers with occasional moderate turbulence.  (The was second flight after the power supply to the EFIS system failed resulting in loss of the primary attitude indicator and HSI which was repaired and return to service.  I mention this only because it attributed to my increase lack of trust in the flight instruments.) The autopilot was on altitude preselect with a vertical speed selected.  During the decent I was evaluating the autopilot issue and I think, after the fact, I pushed the altitude capture twice, resulting in me disengaging the capture and descending below the desired altitude.  At the time I attributed this to a malfunction autopilot and I decided to disconnect the autopilot and manually flew the plane.  This increased the workload.  Weather was also a significant factor.  There was a line of thunderstorms that were moving over ZZZ (destination) and I was watching the satellite radar utilizing a portable GDL52 communicating to Garmin pilot on my I pad. For this flight I was using ForeFlight for navigation/charts and Garmin Pilot for weather, because the GDL would not communicate satellite radar information to ForeFlight.  I have been using ForeFlight for charts and weather in previous aircraft for many years, but this aircraft has only ADSB-out and is scheduled to have an avionics upgrade to a GDL69a (sat WX) and Garmin 345 (ADSB IN/Out) installation in Month.  I was also utilizing the onboard radar, but am learning to use this system.  Bottom line was too many nav / weather systems in use that resulted in unnecessary and additional workload. Another factor was that I had transitioned from a piston to a turboprop and had about 80 hours in the TBM time at the time of this flight. So I was still low on the TBM experience curve.   Based on the weather I requested GPS XX and activated the approach on the 530s. However, as I proceeded to ZZZ GPS XX, the weather changed and the IAF for GPS XX was near the line of thunderstorms and center offered ILS XY and left turn to heading for the IAF - I made a right turn to that heading.  I should have recognized that I was starting to make small mistakes. During this time I also was varying from assigned altitude due to increase workload.   I entered ILS XX in to the 530S and activated and Center vectored me to the approach.   I began the approach and was able to see the ground occasionally.  I was handed off the Tower and I waited to capture the glideslope. I continued the approach, my position and altitude were not cross checking, and I began try to determine why.  I was high when I was on a 2-3 mile final.  While making this evaluation, I was able to see the runway lights and able to make a visual decent and landing.  I was not confident after this flight, there were too many mistakes in this flight.  When I was on the ground I contacted my flight instructor and went over flight and discussed the avionics and my reaction/ decision I made.  We made a plan of how to determine the effect of the flickering light on the mode selector and insure that it was safe for flight.  I altered my plans so that weather would have a minimal impact and returned back to ZZZ1.  To learn and avoid these errors in the future, I did a full debrief with my instructor when I return home.  This focused on both the avionics and cross reference that he recommended to determine status of the navigation system.  We also spent a considerable time on how I responded to the apparent situation and decision making.  It was at this time, I realized how much some minor errors in my part had a large impact onthe flight.  The avionics have also been evaluated by Company in ZZZ2 and determine that the malfunction was limited to the mode selector display and that the autopilot is functioning normally. It is schedule to be repaired.   Further I have also flow approaches in VFR conditions to verify operations of the navigation and autopilot systems and apply some of the procedures that my instructor and I discussed.				Pilot reported autopilot system failure caused difficulties on approach and landing.	
1808940	202105	1201-1800	BJC.Airport	CO				5600		VMC	Windshear; Turbulence; Rain; 10		Daylight			Tower BJC	Other Flight School	Piper Single Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 91	None	Training		Descent	Visual Approach	Class D BJC																												Tower BJC	Other Flight School	Cessna Single Piston Undifferentiated or Other Model		1	Part 91	VFR	Training		Landing	Visual Approach	Class D BJC																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Other Flight school	Instructor	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Commercial; Flight Crew Flight Instructor	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 110; Flight Crew Total 1200; Flight Crew Type 300		Confusion; Communication Breakdown; Situational Awareness	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1808940												ATC Issue All Types; Conflict NMAC; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance	Vertical 400		Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach	Human Factors; Procedure	Human Factors	While doing pattern work on Runway 30L, we requested a full-stop on Runway 3. Tower instructed us to 'make left downwind.' My student and I turned left to enter the left downwind Runway 3, at this time, Aircraft Y traffic was mid-field downwind Runway 30L. Traffic was cleared touch-and-go Runway 30L. Without further instruction, my student and I proceeded with a standard left pattern Runway 3. On 0.25 nm final, I see the traffic landing the parallel is still rolling on the runway, and I did not anticipate them being clear of my runway by the time my student and I crossed the threshold Runway 3, so I instructed my student to execute a go-around. This resulted in our Aircraft X directly overflying the Aircraft Y right as they rolled across Runway 3 on Runway 30L. They were not yet airborne.In hindsight, I believe Tower's instruction to make left downwind may have been intended as extend downwind, or they would call my base. Since this instruction was not given, we made a normal pattern and did not anticipate a conflict until short final.Earlier on the same flight, we had been doing full-stop-taxi-backs, and we landed Runway 30L, and exited left onto Taxiway B. The same controller gave us taxi instructions 'Runway 30L taxi via Delta' and I asked if I needed 'cross Runway 3.' During this same flight, I had prompted the previous controller a couple of times, and gotten the instruction to cross each time, but only after prompting for it. The next controller volunteered, 'we don't normally do that.' She did give me the crossing, but I am wondering why we do not volunteer that crossing since it is necessary to cross the full-hold bar for Runway 3 in order to turn left onto Taxiway D from Taxiway B, or the other way to turn right onto Taxiway B from Taxiway D.				Flight Instructor reported a NMAC during go-around on a training flight.	
1809136	202105	1801-2400	ZZZZ.ARTCC	FO						IMC	Icing; Thunderstorm; Turbulence		Night			Center ZZZZ	Air Carrier	EMB ERJ 170/175 ER/LR		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent	Vectors																									Pitot/Static Ice System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Communication Breakdown; Situational Awareness	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1809136												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Human Factors; Aircraft; Procedure; Weather	Ambiguous	There was a momentary unreliable airspeed followed by a brief failure of the PF's (Pilot Flying) PFD. Once the PFD was restored, airspeed was still momentarily unreliable.While operating the flight from ZZZ to ZZZZ, I was the pilot flying. After deviating for weather, we were proceeding directly to ZZZZZ to perform the VOR DME 2 [approach to] Runway XX at ZZZZ. Approximately 20nm from ZZZZZ and descending, we encountered IMC and light precipitation and light turbulence which gradually increased to moderate turbulence. We were partially configured at flaps 1 and gear was down at 200 kts and beginning our turn for the arc when my airspeed began having deviations of +/- 15 kts, I attributed this to the increasing turbulence and precipitation we had encountered. While banked and descending, the PLIs (Pitch Limit Indicator) presented and I had a loss of airspeed below the green dot and a gain of airspeed that presented amber digits. At this point my airspeed completely went away, as if I was on the ground, a grey box was present where the digits would be but there was no V-Speed information. Shortly after this occurred, my PFD went black for a moment, but never reverted to a different display. Once this happened, I transitioned to the IESS (Integrated Electronic Standby System), rolled the wings level, advanced the thrust levers, climbed to ensure terrain clearance, and we returned to VMC.Nearly immediately after establishing the climb, the PFD and all information on it returned, although there were errors with the airspeed. Initially, the airspeed showed faster than we were, as if we had been repositioned in the simulator, and we received one aural 'High-speed' until the airspeed settled back to what the IESS was showing. Our altitude at this point was approximately between 17,000 ft. and 16,000 ft. with a high peak around 9827 ft. in our vicinity. From our descent, I knew I could find VMC around 19,000 ft. so I directed the pilot not flying to tell ATC we would be present heading and climbing to 20,000 ft.I did not direct the PNF (Pilot Not Flying) to declare an emergency as we were busy figuring out what just happened and coordinating a plan between each other. In the climb, we cleaned the aircraft up and ran an after takeoff checklist. Once we were in VMC, we worked with each other to determine that all of our instruments matched and were reliable. We also determined that we could remain mostly VMC by performing the approach from ZZZZZ1 and asked to proceed directly to ZZZZZ1. We successfully performed the approach and landing at ZZZZ. Upon exiting the runway, we received both AFCS (Automated Flight Control System) Fault, and ADS Probe 1 Fail messages. We felt that we could have been struck by lightning, but we could not find any strike points on the aircraft, and all static wicks that were visible to us were found intact. We entered both messages above in the AML as well as an AML entry for a possible lightning strike.I feel my actions were appropriate given that we were not in radar contact any longer. I was unsure if I could trust any pitot or static instruments and I knew where I could find visual conditions away from terrain. I should have declared an emergency but at the time I felt that doing so with the language barrier would have broken down my communication with my First Officer when we needed to focus on flying the aircraft. Additionally, he was able to secure a clearance for the same heading up to 20,000 ft. as soon as I asked him to do so.				EMB-175 Captain reported unreliable airspeed and indications in flight that required a missed approach and ATC assistance to land safely.	
1809786	202105	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US				4830		VMC	Turbulence; Windshear; 6		Daylight			CTAF ZZZ	Personal	Small Aircraft, Low Wing, 1 Eng, Retractable Gear		1	Part 91	IFR	Personal		Final Approach	Visual Approach	Class C ZZZ																												CTAF ZZZ		Small Aircraft, Low Wing, 1 Eng, Fixed Gear		1					Takeoff / Launch		Class C ZZZ																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Personal	Pilot Flying; Single Pilot	Flight Crew Private; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 10; Flight Crew Total 2900; Flight Crew Type 700		Confusion; Communication Breakdown; Distraction; Time Pressure; Situational Awareness	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1809786												Conflict Ground Conflict, Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy	Horizontal 300; Vertical 200	N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Human Factors	Human Factors	Aircraft X was on Final approach after calling out turning Final Runway 21, Second call Aircraft X on Short final Runway 21. After first call on Final I saw Aircraft Y pull slowly onto the runway and stop, that's when I cancelled Short Final. Just as I was preparing to go-around he started moving and in my judgement I could land if he kept going. He lifted off as I touched down with full brakes and speedbrakes out. I was gaining on him as he climbed to 100 feet and by the time I made the second to last turn off he was 200 feet above and maybe 300 feet in front of my aircraft.Additional realities information. I never heard Aircraft Y announce anything, entering Runway, or even my call to him after I landed close to him. The heavy cross winds were a very big issue for me on the approach to this downhill runway. I know it well and with 160 at 22 knots on a 21 Runway I had 50 degree left crosswind and yaw angle for lineup. I knew I would get a major downdraft at the end of this runway so I elect to land at the 1000 feet marker. My landing speed is normal 100 knots plus 1/2 the wind makes 110 knots approach speed. That is the reason my ground speed and distance is long and fast. I was gaining on him even at his 100 to 200 feet. I also want to point out that as a NON controlled airport no one should pull on the runway and stop for even 10 seconds with an aircraft on final at 110 knots. Since his information indicates he is a commercial pilot I'm sure he realizes that there is no excuse to be on the wrong Frequency XYZ.00 or to stop as in Position and HOLD like you might get at a Towered airport.				Private pilot reported while on short final another aircraft entered the runway and began to takeoff.	
1811235	202105	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US			0			VMC	Turbulence		Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Personal	Cessna Aircraft Undifferentiated or Other Model		1	Part 91	VFR	Personal		Landing	None	Class D ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Personal	Pilot Flying; Single Pilot	Flight Crew Private; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 34; Flight Crew Total 425; Flight Crew Type 63		Training / Qualification; Confusion			1811235												Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control					Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach	Weather; Human Factors	Human Factors	I was returning to my home base of ZZZ from ZZZ1. I am flying my newish Aircraft X, of which I have about 19 hours in. It should be noted that most of my flight hours are from 25 years ago. I knew ZZZ had some crosswinds but I thought they were manageable and I felt I needed to push myself a bit. If I remember, the winds were about 13 at 260 (260 at 13). Part of my challenge was that they were not reporting winds on METAR only ATIS. Upon approach the winds started gusting to 19. This would give an 8.4 kt. crosswind gusting to 12 knots, something I should be able to handle. So, on the first attempt, I think my approach was OK, but I flared high, bounced and quickly realized a go-around was the best option. Not perfect but decent decision making.The second attempt is where things went very wrong. This time I came in with 20 degrees of flaps. My approach was probably a bit fast, but seemed OK as I got closer to the airport. First of all, I flared too high again, bounced, but then made the mistake to try and salvage the landing. On the second bounce I recognized that the plane was proposing and I'd have a prop strike if I let it drop again. So, I started another go-around, but the plane would not climb, more importantly I wasn't facing down the runway but more towards the left side. At this point the helicopters parked left of the runway are getting bigger in my windshield but I know I've got to gain air speed. The one correct decision I made was to keep the nose down until I gained some airspeed. Eventually I got the speed and was able to climbout. At some point in this I realized why I couldn't climb, I had raised all of the flaps! I then came around and successfully landed the plane on the third attempt. It was bad enough that after I parked, the fire department came over to 'congratulate' me on my recovery, but I really think it was to make sure I was OK.I learned the following on this landing attempt. I need more time with my instructor with landings, especially crosswind landings. I need more practice on Go-Arounds focusing on flap management and right rudder.				C206 pilot reported crosswind landings resulting in two unstabilized approaches and go-arounds resulting in momentary loss of control before successfully landing.	
1811246	202105	1801-2400	ZZZ.TRACON	US				3300		IMC	Turbulence; Rain		Daylight	1000		TRACON ZZZ	Personal	PA-28 Cherokee/Archer/Dakota/Pillan/Warrior		1	Part 91	IFR	Personal		Climb	Vectors	Class E ZZZ																								PFD		X	Design																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Personal	Single Pilot; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Commercial; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 21; Flight Crew Total 1796; Flight Crew Type 741		Situational Awareness; Troubleshooting; Confusion; Distraction			1811246												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance			Automation Air Traffic Control; Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Became Reoriented	Human Factors; Software and Automation; Aircraft	Software and Automation	After flying a practice RNAV (GPS) approach into ZZZ, I was climbing through 2,000 feet on the missed approach when I checked in with Approach. Approach provided an altimeter setting (30.12, which had changed since my last check-in of 30.11) and a few seconds later provided a heading for vectors for the ILS at ZZZ1.  I leveled off at the assigned altitude of 3,000 feet and shortly after, ATC asked me to maintain 3,000 feet.  Considering my altimeter was reading 3,020 feet, I knew this call meant something was not right.  I scanned the instruments and saw the altimeter setting was now set to 29.92. I requested the altimeter setting again and when I entered the correct setting, I was approximately 300 feet above my assigned altitude.  After landing I was able to determine the sequence of events that caused this deviation.My airplane has an Aspen Avionics Evolution 1000 PFD.  I have had this for the last X years and feel I am proficient in operating it.  It has a feature that, in my piston powered general aviation airplane, is not used.  If you press the 'baro' button and then push the right knob, the altimeter setting will change to 29.92.  Since I never climb to FL180, I have never used this feature. In this situation, when I contacted ATC, they gave me the altimeter setting, and since it was different, I pushed the 'baro' button and turned the right knob to the correct setting. When ATC then provided me a vector, I needed to move the heading bug almost 180 degrees (it had been set during the southbound approach and now I was flying northbound).  Instead of spinning the heading knob (the same right knob) 180 degrees, I pushed the knob which automatically sets the heading bug to the current heading.  What I didn't realize was that when I pushed the right knob, the 'baro' function was still active and therefore set the altimeter to 29.92.  The heading bug also aligned to the current heading, however since I was in turbulence, I assume I bumped the knob twice instead of once.  I didn't notice the change in altitude because I was in a climb.  When I reached 3,000 feet, I leveled off like normal without being aware that I was higher than I should be.Moving forward I will need to be more careful and aware of what function is active in the PFD before making changes.  However, my fear is that it may be another X or more years before something like this happens again and as time moves forward, we tend to forget these events.  I do think this is an opportunity for Aspen to make a software revision.  If this baro reset feature is meant for aircraft passing through 18,000 feet, it would be nice if this feature could be disabled for installations in aircraft that cannot reach that altitude.  Alternatively, if this feature is meant for aircraft passing through 18,000 feet and it is utilized at an altitude well below that, a flashing yellow flag (similar to what happens when approaching the selected altitude) would help to visually annotate the change that was just made. I'm not trying to remove responsibility from the pilot, but the pilot has hundreds of things to remember when flying.  Modern avionics should not be adding to the list of quirks that need to be remembered.				PA-28 pilot reported an altitude excursion due to an incorrect operation of the Aspen Evolution 1000 PFD, which reset their altimeter to 29.92.	
1811279	202105	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US	290	10		10000		IMC	Rain; Turbulence; 10			2500		TRACON ZZZ	Personal	SR22		1	Part 91	IFR	Personal	GPS	Climb	Vectors; SID ZZZZZ	Class E ZZZ																												TRACON ZZZ	Other Unknown	Any Unknown or Unlisted Aircraft Manufacturer		1	Part 91	IFR	Other Unkown		Cruise		Class E ZZZ																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Personal	Pilot Flying; Single Pilot	Flight Crew Private; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 60; Flight Crew Total 1100; Flight Crew Type 1100		Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1811279												ATC Issue All Types; Conflict Airborne Conflict; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence	Horizontal 11000; Vertical 1000		Automation Aircraft TA; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Weather; Aircraft	Weather	I departed ZZZ, climbed normally, received vector for a right turn to a heading of 290 and a climb to 10,000. I complied with all of the uneventfully. West of the airport was cell of precipitation that I couldn't see through. It was depicted as yellow on my weather display. The precipitation was west of the airport and oriented along the axis of about 290 to 300 west of ZZZ.I was doing the ZZZ4 departure. The tower controller advised in the initial climb that in substance that I'd be boxed around on vectors for the climb before being going to the route.A previous departed reported the ceiling at about 6,500 which was basically true. I changed frequency to ZZZ TRACON and got a heading of 290. When I entered the IMC the rain was very intense and it was turbulent. I noticed changes on the vertical speed indicator up and then later down. The temperature was near freezing, but I did not see any actual ice. While climbing in IMC, the radio frequency began to screech such that I couldn't hear anything. That ultimately occurred on both radios and both frequencies. The marker beacon lights were flashing. I received a traffic alert for traffic on my left. It was very turbulent, but I stayed at 10,000 feet as assigned. With no communications (coms), I squawked 7600.I decided to turn 90 degrees right still in IMC because that resolved the traffic, would get me out of the precipitation, was a heading basically on course, was consistent with the expectations from the departure briefing and ZZZ4, and would restore coms. The precipitation was aligned with the vector so remaining on that heading was not viable. There was traffic there. I didn't change altitude (I was at 10,000 ft. the whole time).After the right turn, I exited the side of the precipitation, I was able to hear ground responding to one of my calls (on COM2 on the bottom antenna), so I went back to the departure frequency on COM1 (on the top), re-established communications and departed.The ATC TRACON controller was upset seemed to think that I was in VMC and just turned randomly or something. I've flown through 'yellow' precipitation many times uneventfully. The lost coms procedure when you're on a vector is to either stay on the vector or turn on course. I turned on course because it solved all of the problems, but seemed to surprise ATC. I think that the confusing thing for ATC was that it was fine weather straight over the airport, which is probably what he has on his computer. But west of the airport was unexpectedly intense rain.I'm not sure that this incident could have been avoided given what everyone knew at the time, although once I squawked 7600, the turn on course shouldn't have been a surprise.				SR22 pilot reported a loss of communication due to moderate weather during departure.	
1811286	202105	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US		10		5000		Mixed	Turbulence; 10		Daylight	3000		TRACON ZZZ	Personal	Cessna Citation Mustang (C510)		2	Part 91	IFR	Personal	GPS; FMS Or FMC	Initial Approach	Vectors; Visual Approach	Class C ZZZ																								Engine Control		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Personal	Pilot Flying; Single Pilot	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Commercial; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 48; Flight Crew Total 866; Flight Crew Type 168					1811286												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	While being vectored for the visual approach at ZZZ, approximately while descending through 5,000 feet, the Amber CAS message (ENG CTRL SYS (L-R)) appeared as well as the Master Caution System light, I referenced the Manufacturers Emergency/Abnormal Checklist which dictated the first step is to reset the FADEC to the affected side, which in this case was both sides, the checklist goes on to say if the CAS message clears then no further action is required, or if the CAS message remains; to land as soon as practical. The checklist also goes on to advise of the possible effects that may be encountered, they are:- Degraded or lack of response to throttle movements including possible surging or flameout.- Possible inability to restart.- Possible inability to achieve ground idle.- Loss of automatic Interstage Turbine Temperature (ITT) limiting during ground idle.- Loss of automatic ITT limiting during ground or windmill starts.- Loss of ITT indication.The message did not clear after the reset, and given the possibility of a flameout, I chose to land ASAP, I notified ATC and requested to proceed directly to the final approach course for landing. ATC complied with my request and gave me priority. The landing was uneventful, but on the landing roll out, the L Engine Fail Warning CAS message came up, it distinguished about 15 seconds later, then appeared once again about a minute later during taxi and stayed on for 10 seconds then went away.Once I arrived to the ramp at the FBO I shut the engines down and powered down the aircraft with no further incident.				C510 pilot reported the left engine control failed while on vectors for a visual approach.	
1812040	202106	0601-1200	ZZZZ.ARTCC	FO						IMC	Turbulence					Center ZZZZ	Air Carrier	EMB ERJ 170/175 ER/LR		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise; Descent; Initial Approach	Vectors																									Turbine Assembly		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument			Situational Awareness; Troubleshooting; Distraction			1812040	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine			Situational Awareness; Distraction; Troubleshooting			1812345	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural FAR; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence		N	Automation Aircraft Terrain Warning; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Became Reoriented; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft; Procedure; Weather	Aircraft	While enroute to ZZZ we experienced unusually high LP vibrations, still within limitations. No amber indication was observed. However, the vibrations were strong enough to be felt in the aircraft. Descending into ZZZ ATC cleared us to descend to 16,000 and cleared us for the approach. We began to descend when we noticed the vibrations were getting worse. While running the QRH we encountered sudden turbulence, shortly after we received a terrain alert and immediately executed the escape maneuver. We continued to ZZZ without further difficulty and to a safe landing. Crew became task saturated by increasing engine vibrations, sudden unexpected turbulence and QRH checklist.Suggestions - Terrain awareness chart or company jepps. Company awareness video regarding descent and approach into ZZZ.		ATC cleared us for the approach into ZZZ and notifies us of no radar coverage under 16,000 feet. The Captain set the initial approach fix and we began a descent on a VNAV path toward the airport. During descent we were handling an engine vibration issue with the QRH along with some unexpected turbulence, when we received a terrain notification. The Captain executed an escape maneuver and we continued to a safe landing on Runway XX at ZZZ. Crew became task saturated with the engine vibrations, QRH, and unexpected turbulence in descent.		EMB-175 flight crew reported distractions from troubleshooting LP engine vibrations and turbulence caused a descent and a terrain alert requiring an escape maneuver.	
1812538	202106	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US				5000			Turbulence					TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	Embraer Jet Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Climb		Class B ZZZ																								Autothrottle/Speed Control		X	Improperly Operated																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine			Training / Qualification; Time Pressure			1812538	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Confusion; Time Pressure; Training / Qualification; Human-Machine Interface; Workload			1813001	Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence					Flight Crew Became Reoriented	Human Factors; Weather	Human Factors	On the departure, the First Officer (FO) had requested Heading Mode (HDG), Flight Level Change (FLCH) during the climb out as we were given a heading.  It was bumpy and he was trying to comply with the SID instructions to speed up to 250 KIAS while hand flying in bumpy air.  As we approached the 5,000 foot altitude restriction, he started to slow his climb for the level off, but didn't pull back on the thrust levers. The autothrottles were on, but we were not quite close enough to the altitude for the flight director to switch to 'ASEL' on the FMA and begin capturing. In a climb, the auto throttles remain at full thrust in green FLCH until ASEL is annunciated, and since the FO was hand flying and beginning the level off before the Altitude Select (ASEL) annunciation, the speed began to pick up.  As pilot monitoring, I noticed the airspeed at 255 KIAS with an increasing trend and I called out 'airspeed' to alert him to the airspeed.  He seemed confused and did not make any corrections. I then called for him to 'pitch up' which he did slightly, but he mentioned the level off altitude that was approaching.  I said to 'pull the thrust back' and he seemed confused again and replied that the auto throttles should do that.  I then pulled the thrust levers back myself to slow the aircraft down as the speed was starting to go past 260 KIAS.  ATC gave us a 'climb and maintain 7000' and a direct to one of the fixes.  The FO pitched up for the climb and I was able to let go of the thrust levers as the speed was again under control.  As we were climbing, ATC instructed us to climb to 16,000 and the FO called for 'Autopilot On' prior to the next level off so there were no further issues.  I reminded him that there is no speed protection in the level off for the FLCH mode if he is hand flying it and levels off early.   ATC didn't seem to notice the increased airspeed,  and the flight continued without further incident.The FO said that he finished IOE and for the next month was unable to get any flying from crew scheduling.  He had less than 30 hours on the aircraft.   The following month, he had to be in Country to check on family due to the COVID pandemic.  He said that he had flown earlier that day and seemed to be comfortable in the plane.  It became clear that he was rusty on the details concerning the FLCH mode.  After I spoke with him about what happened, he said he understood and he did not make the same mistake on his flight the following day. Any time a pilot has less than 100 hours (consolidation) on an aircraft, and doesn't fly for more than 30 days, they should be given a trip with an IOE instructor both to give them recency of experience and to refresh any forgotten material.		During take off climbing to the assigned altitude of 5,000 in flight level change plane accelerated to 250K, during last 1,000 ft I shallowed the pitch as climb was getting a bit bumpy due to builds up around [the] area and I was trying to avoid flying through a cloud straight ahead. Captain advised me of speed increasing and passing 250 and I assumed the auto-throttle would have compensated for that as it was engaged. Leveling off to 5,000 speed past 260 and the Captain reached out the thrust lever decreasing power avoiding further deviation. Soon after cleared to 7,000 climb and flight continued with no further deviation. After IOE I was put on reserve and didn't fly for about a month, I then was able to pick up a line and fly just one sequence as a tragic event forced me to drop the rest of the month and travel overseas, as a result in 2 months I only flew 19 hours and that was my first day flying after a long stop. As newly typed on a new airplane I didn't immediately recalled the different ways the autothrottle responds, however soon after the event my Captain explained [to] me what happened and refreshed my memory on it. After IOE it would be good to assign some extra flight to help consolidate, maybe assigning more frequent flights and not letting more than 2 weeks without flying would avoid skills deterioration until the first 100 hours are reached.		Air carrier flight crew reported turbulence and weak aircraft familiarity, resulted in airspeed mismanagement and confusion.	
1812551	202104	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US				1200			Turbulence; Windshear						Air Carrier	EMB ERJ 145 ER/LR		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Final Approach; Landing	Vectors	Class D ZZZ	N	N	Y	N	Unscheduled Maintenance																			Autopilot		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Workload; Time Pressure; Communication Breakdown; Troubleshooting	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Maintenance		1812551												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural FAR; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Maintenance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence		N	Person Flight Crew	Pre-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	MEL; Company Policy; Aircraft; Procedure	Aircraft	The following narrative is due to a complete lack of threat and error management and operation performance.   This was the first leg of a 4 day trip and I would be flying with a First Officer for the first time.   We were heavily delayed to start our day and weather was terrible.   High winds and windshear were reported throughout the whole Company system with several crews diverting.   Yet my release for this flight indicated nothing other than I should evaluate the use of Runway XX for ZZZ departure.   When the aircraft arrived I met the arriving Captain and he explained to me the auto pilot master warning EICAS message occurred at 1,000 feet on the coupled ILS approach into ZZZ.  The Captain stated maintenance refused to take the write ups as it was going to ZZZ1 and they would casually look at it there.   I immediately called maintenance and asked why they wouldn't take write ups and said from what it sounded like a write up was not warranted.   The maintenance controller asked if I wished to MEL the autopilot and I stated how can I?  At this point I should have immediately refused the plane, but at Company, refusing to fly gets us an immediate phone call from the Chief Pilot office.   We Were now operating in the red of the threat and error management philosophy.   On the way to ZZZ1 we experienced moderate turbulence as expected and when we turned base for Runway XY the field was surrounded by virga.  The FO and I briefed a go around and the windshear escape maneuver once more.   On final at about 1,200 feet the Autopilot Fail master warning EICAS  message appeared and the Autopilot disconnected.  I continued to hand fly the aircraft and remained stable to about 300 feet where I encountered increasing performance and the approach became unstable.   A normal go-around was executed and we called positive rate gear up, we received the windshear caution.   Immediately after we hit severe turbulence.  We diverted to ZZZ without further incident.  The remainder of the flight was performed without the autopilot.Company maintenance did not take a write up from a Captain. I wrote up the AP failure and the encounter with severe turbulence. All write-ups must be taken by maintenance.				EMB-145 Captain reported maintenance refusing to accept a write up on the autopilot and departed to destination airport where the autopilot failed, causing an air turn back.	
1812704	202105	1201-1800									Turbulence; 10		Daylight	10000			Air Taxi	Cessna 402/402C/B379 Businessliner/Utiliner		1	Part 91	VFR	Ferry / Re-Positioning		Final Approach	Direct; Visual Approach																									Engine		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Commercial; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 100; Flight Crew Total 2800; Flight Crew Type 300					1812704												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Ground Event / Encounter Ground Strike - Aircraft; Ground Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Aircraft Damaged; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition	Aircraft	Aircraft	I acted as the pilot-in-command and sole occupant of a Cessna 402 aircraft on a flight in visual meteorological conditions. Prior to departure, I conducted a preflight inspection in accordance with the manufacturer's recommended procedures, and I followed the preflight checklist. All systems appeared to operate normally during takeoff, climb, and cruise flight.During the approach to landing, I monitored the UNICOM frequency when another pilot approximately 4-5 miles ahead of me, approaching the same runway, advised that there was turbulence on final approach. While on short final approach, I experienced turbulence and added power to maintain a proper glide path.  At that point, I noticed that the left (i.e., critical) engine was no longer producing power. As a result, the aircraft nose yawed left (i.e., toward the dead engine) and I applied full right rudder to maintain directional control.Since I was on a short final approach to the runway, I reduced power in order to land while maintaining my airspeed above Vmc (i.e., minimum controllable airspeed with critical engine failure). However, the gusty wind drifted the airplane to the left of center line. Since I had applied full right rudder to maintain runway centerline while in flight, upon touchdown the airplane aggressively spun to the right and departed the runway, which caused the landing gear to collapse.When the aircraft came to a complete stop, I shut down the right engine, secured the aircraft, and exited through the cabin door without incident.				Cessna 402 pilot reported experiencing an engine failure on final approach, followed by loss of directional control after landing that resulted in damage to the aircraft.	
1813200	202106	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US	255	21		4500		VMC	Turbulence; 10		Daylight				Personal	Skyhawk 172/Cutlass 172		1	Part 91	None	Personal		Climb	Direct	Class B ZZZ; Class E ZZZ																								Engine		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Personal	Single Pilot; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Flight Instructor	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 76; Flight Crew Total 7900; Flight Crew Type 2400		Situational Awareness; Troubleshooting; Communication Breakdown; Distraction	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1813200												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Airspace Violation All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural FAR					Flight Crew Exited Penetrated Airspace; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Human Factors; Aircraft	Aircraft	On Date at ZZZ airport, I fueled the airplane, conducted a preflight (includes checking oil quantity and taking a sample of all three fuel sumps), reviewed my direct course back home and updated my weather (VMC) for the short flight to ZZZ1. During the pre-takeoff checklist (engine run up), I notice a slight roughness in the engine. I leaned out the mixture control which cleared out the roughness. I set the mixture control for my takeoff, but not to full rich. I left it leaned out a little for the departure and climb. During my climbout, I always look for off airport landing spots (should I need one). I did not see any good locations and I told myself that I want 4,500 feet once clear of the Class B airspace altitudes. It was shortly after my desire to get to 4,500 feet MSL ASAP that the engine was not performing as it should in cruise climb. I applied full power and checked the engine instruments (all green, but RPM was towards the low end). I was getting a shallow climb at an airspeed of about 90. I then immediately performed the initial emergency procedure; carb heat on, mixture full (then adjust if needed) and check fuel selector on both. That seemed to bring the performance back, but not immediately. Shortly after that the engine then started to run rough. My thought was spark plug fouling or magneto (I know it was not a cylinder failure, because I have experienced that in a twin engine airplane). I adjusted the mixture control first. This is logical, because if you check the mags before the mixture control you could increase spark plug fowling. At some point through all this my mind went into altitude, altitude, altitude. Unfortunately, I had that 4,500 feet MSL in my mind. While dealing with the engine situation, I continued my climb to that altitude. I got the rough running engine cleared out.  Once I was done keeping the airplane in the air (dealing with low performance and then the rough running engine during the cruise climb phase), I checked my position and noticed that I was in ZZZ3 Class B airspace (west end of and close to the 6,000 feet MSL corridor, but I was in the 4,000 feet MSL corridor). I immediately descended below 4,000 feet MSL (3,800 feet MSL). Once clear of the Class B airspace/Mode C veil, I climbed back to 4,500 feet MSL. No airplane conflicts were noted. Once on the ground at the ZZZ1, I performed an engine runup and checked the mags full and leaned mixture settings. Both checked out okay. My plan is to check the oil, fuel and talk with a mechanic. I am confident that the fuel is okay since I checked it after fueling at the ZZZ (but I will check it again). I have cancelled my planned flying for the next week and no flying the Skyhawk until the items are checked out. Once I got home, I chair flew the scenario that I just experienced and realized that all my training and personal home chair flying has been geared to multi-engine, on an IFR flight plan and in radio communications. My scenario was single engine, VFR and not in communication. Do not get me wrong, I always review the emergency procedures before a flight for the airplane that I am flying. Also, the poor engine performance that I experienced did not seem to be carb icing. Since the air filter and bracket were replaced at the annual inspection, the carb air intake system will be looked at. What I learned and what I will do going forward so that this does not happen again:I learned that in my continued personal chair flying training that I need to go over single engine emergency, VFR and not in communications scenarios. This situation reinforced with me that single engine performance (VFR, non-communications) incidents come with a high workload especially when dealing with keeping the airplane in the air. It showed me that I became focused on keeping the plane in the air (altitude and rectifying the engine situation) and that navigating and communicating was lacking with this incident.   Going forward, in addition to reading theemergency procedures that I currently do for every plane that I fly and chair flying multi-engine scenarios, I will chair fly single engine (SE) scenarios. I need to emphasize aviate, navigate, and communicate in any SE situation. When flying under Class B airspace and within a Mode C veil do the following:1.	Obtain flight following when flying VFR, especially when flying in a Mode C veil. I would already be in communications should I need to report a problem.  2.	Mark along the course line a line with an arrow from the departure airport to the point when the plane will be cleared of the Mode C veil. Also, put a B next to this manually drawn line.3.	Apply a post-it-note on the instrument panel that I am in a Mode C veil airspace.I will talk to my Flight Instructor and review this flight to obtain feedback and discuss my ideas and additional ways to prevent this from happening again.				C172 pilot reported an airspace incursion occurred when the engine began running rough and the pilot was troubleshooting. The roughness eventually dissipated and pilot exited the airspace.	
1813254	202106	1201-1800	ZJX.ARTCC	FL				37000		Marginal	Thunderstorm; Turbulence					Center ZJX	Air Carrier	Commercial Fixed Wing		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZJX																																																															Aircraft X; Facility ZJX.ARTCC		Government	Enroute	Air Traffic Control Fully Certified	Air Traffic Control Time Certified In Pos 1 (yrs) 8		Workload; Communication Breakdown; Confusion; Distraction; Situational Awareness; Time Pressure	Party1 ATC; Party2 ATC		1813254												ATC Issue All Types; Airspace Violation All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Physical Injury / Incapacitation	Company Policy; Procedure; Weather; Airspace Structure	Weather	I was working a sector with weather building. The warning areas were hot with numerous aircraft deviating into active military airspace. I was unaware at the time that the only plan was to depend on the military to take point-outs to work aircraft through that area. With no notice the adjacent sector said I can't take anymore northbound aircraft because the controlling facility will not take point-outs. At the time I had several aircraft within minutes of their airspace that required 90 degree turns to miss the sectors airspace. I had to reroute almost every aircraft on my frequency. This reroute put aircraft into weather and overloaded the next receiving sector. This last-minute major increase in workload resulting in Aircraft X not being issued weather. The aircraft flew through moderate to heavy precipitation and encountered severe turbulence which resulted in two passenger injuries.I have NO recommendations this will absolutely happen again. For the years I have been at ZJX, aircraft deviating into active military airspace is normal operations. Look at my previous reports and my co-workers in other areas. Numerous conditions happen in ZJX south area to overload the sectors that cause weather not to be called. After this incident I returned to the south area to see this exact scenario unfolding again. An air carrier flight was in active military airspace [requesting priority]. The only reason this is even being discussed is because a passenger was injured on Aircraft X. If an injury would not have occurred nobody would have investigated what was going on in the ZJX south area. This has been going on for years in the south area with numerous reports. Nothing will change and this will happen again.				ZJX Controller reported working numerous aircraft deviating for weather into military airspace when the controlling facility unexpectedly would not approve anymore deviations. Due to the workload the reporter was not able to issue weather advisories and an aircraft encountered severe turbulence injuring passengers while another Air Carrier advised and deviated into the closed military airspace.	
1813504	202105	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US		0	0				Turbulence; 10			6000	10000	Tower ZZZ	Personal	Skylane 182/RG Turbo Skylane/RG		1	Part 91	IFR	Personal		Landing	Direct	Class D ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Personal	Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 14.1; Flight Crew Total 934.4; Flight Crew Type 826.8					1813504												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Ground Event / Encounter Ground Strike - Aircraft; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Aircraft Damaged; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Weather	Weather	Landing at ZZZ on Runway XX in Cessna Skylane, a strong gust of wind in excess of 20 knots crosswind component almost flipped the airplane. Full left aileron and right rudder kept the airplane from running off the runway or flipping. But there was a propeller strike as the plane came down on the right main then hard on the nose gear. The nose tire developed a leak and went flat over 15 minutes. The plane taxied back to the FBO without much vibration from the propeller and no abnormal engine sounds. [Current weather] information reported 11 knots from 060 and the Tower reported winds had started gusting and were erratic. I had 20 degrees of flaps in and manifold pressure of 12 inches on touchdown. On approach, the plane tracked the centerline of the runway with left aileron and rudder. The crab component was corrected at touch down before crossing Runway XY. With only a light touch the plane went airborne again a few feet only as it crossed [Runway] XY. Then bounced, started to flip with a large gust of wind. There was just enough control authority to prevent the wing from touching the runway but not enough to prevent the propeller strike. No damage was done to the air-frame and the engine continued to run smoothly. No injuries occurred.				C182 pilot reported that a strong gust of wind during touchdown nearly flipped the airplane. The pilot regained aircraft control and the aircraft sustained minor damage. No injuries occurred.	
1814188	202106	0601-1200	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				7000			Turbulence; Thunderstorm		Daylight	3700		Center ZZZ	Personal	PA-32 Cherokee Six/Lance/Saratoga/6X		1	Part 91	IFR	Personal		Cruise	Direct	Class E ZZZ																								Vacuum Pump		X	Failed																																				Facility ZZZ.ARTCC		Government	Enroute	Air Traffic Control Fully Certified	Air Traffic Control Time Certified In Pos 1 (yrs) 6		Communication Breakdown; Distraction; Confusion	Party1 ATC; Party2 Flight Crew		1814188	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Personal	Single Pilot; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Private; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 10; Flight Crew Total 945; Flight Crew Type 861		Troubleshooting; Workload; Distraction; Confusion; Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1815475	ATC Issue All Types; Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT			Automation Air Traffic Control; Person Air Traffic Control; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued Advisory / Alert; Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Aircraft; Airspace Structure; Weather	Aircraft	Aircraft X was on route to ZZZ going northbound at 7,000 feet. I took the hand off. I called out to him and the aircraft was sitting on my frequency. I called the Moderate to Extreme Precipitation between him and his destination and he acknowledged. After a talking with a few other aircraft I went back to Aircraft X to call the weather again and told him it looked as though he was going to go through an area of moderate precipitation but here was extreme precipitation to the east and west. Again, the pilot just simply acknowledged me. It looked as though the aircraft climbed about 300 feet but leveled back off, I asked how the ride was, the pilot responded bumpy. Shortly after, the aircraft began losing altitude, I called the aircraft a few times, gave a low altitude alert, no response. It also appeared the aircraft was in a spiral. A VFR aircraft could hear Aircraft X but attempts to communicate failed. I took a handoff on an air carrier that I asked to help. At that moment I could finally hear Aircraft X and he informed me he lost his vacuum. I asked if he was in control of the aircraft and if he could see the ground, he said yes and that he was below the cloud layer in VFR conditions. Afterwards, I lost communication again. I tried all my transmitters (main/standby/Backups) but was unable to reestablish communications. We watched him proceed very low towards ZZZ and I learned afterwards he did land safely.		I was flying direct to ZZZ. I was IFR in clouds, rain, and turbulence at 7,000 feet approximately 35 miles from ZZZ. The plane started making turns to the right , which I corrected a few times. I was trying to figure out why it was happening. Suddenly the plane did a hard right. I noticed the artificial horizon had 'tumbled' and the 'vacuum' warning light came on. I turned off the auto-pilot and attempted to level the wings with multiple left and right corrections. Also noticed I was now in a dive. I saw the ground through an opening in the clouds and aimed for that. I broke out of the clouds at approximately 3,000-3,500 feet. Once I regained control below the clouds I tried contacting ZZZ Center. They were very difficult to hear/with broken reception. I notified them I had a vacuum pump failure, was below the clouds in VFR conditions and would call when I landed in ZZZ. Not sure if they heard me. I maneuvered the plane as necessary to stay out of the clouds and rain showers to ZZZ. Once on the ground I called Approach and Center to notify them of what had happened. I guess you can never know when a vacuum pump may fail, but it sure does affect many crucial navigation equipment. My assumption would be to have a secondary vacuum pump or artificial horizon as a safety back-up. I will be checking into that.		A Center Controller, and a PA32 pilot flying through rain and experiencing turbulence reported the aircraft had a vacuum pump failure causing a temporary loss of control of the aircraft. The aircraft descended below the Minimum Vectoring Altitude but recovered and landed safely.	
1814256	202106	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				22000		IMC	Thunderstorm; Turbulence		Daylight			Center ZZZ; TRACON ZZZ; Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	EMB ERJ 145 ER/LR		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach		Class A ZZZ; Class B ZZZ; Class E ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Total 2600; Flight Crew Type 1150		Workload			1814256	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument			Workload			1814260	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Flight crew smelled significant burning smell in the flight deck descending out of FL220 on descent into ZZZ.  Flight crew detected burning via smell. Smell was extremely strong and smelled electrical. Flight crew [was unsure of the cause], immediately followed smoke fumes memory items. Donned masks and established communications. Crew then followed QRH and notified ATC of condition and [requested priority handling]. Flight crew descended to make landing safely in ZZZ without further incident. Through 10k feet crew assessed that masks were no longer needed as fumes had dissipated. Flight crew added difficult and different communicating via 02 masks in flight than in sims. Suggest more realistic training methods.		[Report narrative contained no additional information]		EMB-145LR flight crew reported a fume event occurred during initial approach.	
1814266	202106	1201-1800	ZZZ.Tower	US						IMC	Thunderstorm; Turbulence; Windshear		Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	EMB ERJ 145 ER/LR		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																								Central Computer		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Communication Breakdown; Workload; Time Pressure; Situational Awareness; Distraction	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Dispatch		1814266	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Communication Breakdown; Distraction; Situational Awareness; Workload	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Dispatch		1814267	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Fuel Issue; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence		N	Person Flight Crew	Aircraft In Service At Gate	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Maintenance Action	Company Policy; Human Factors; MEL; Weather; Aircraft	Aircraft	Upon taking the aircraft, the number 3 MFD was inoperative which, per the MEL, required the Captain to complete all flying duties. While at ZZZ the Captain noticed that the GPWS Inop, Windshear Inop, Terrain Inop, and Terrain fail message were indicated in the EICAS. The Captain called maintenance who immediately deferred the issue and the Captain completed the logbook entries. Prior to push I entered the appropriate information into ACARS which did not match the PERF INIT which the Captain entered. We verified the numbers multiple times and determined that the ACARS had to be inaccurate. We also verified our numbers with the updated AOM. After calling Dispatch it appeared that their was a fleet wide issue with ACARS. It appeared to me that some information that is generally manually input may have already been calculated in the zero fuel weight of ACARS (85lbs for crew bags). Prior to the ACARS issue we notified Ground Control to receive an off time into ZZZ1 which we were given a departure time. At that point the Captain decided to complete the paper form X. We notified the ground crew of the delay who informed us they had to leave for another aircraft and would not be back for about 30-40 minutes. The Captain decided to have them push us from the gate in the event our issue was solved prior to their return. We completed the appropriate checklists and pushed but did not start any engines. Then notified ATC of the delay to void Off Time. The Captain then called the company for approval to open the cabin window to hand the form paper copy to the ground crew. Once the form was completed we called ops, handed the W&B out the window, and re completed the before start checklist all the way through. We also discussed not utilizing ACARS W&B throughout the flight (added workload). We then notified Ground that we were ready to verify off time prior to starting engines (fuel management). They gave us an immediate wheels up time so we started the engines and taxied to [Runway] XX. I used my IPAD to verify weather at ZZZ1 prior to push which was reporting calm winds , few 050 TCU and 10 miles vis and again I checked prior to taxi which they were reporting calm winds, 046 CB, 10 miles. On departure from [Runway] XX we were given a turn to the west and eventually a heading to the north with a climb to 8,000 [feet]. When given direct to ZZZZZ for the ZZZZZ4 Arrival we had to vector around a few storms. We also had to vector around storm clouds on the arrival and were given vectors off the arrival by approach due to flow into ZZZ1. Within this period we both discussed the weather, verified the TAF, and discussed needing an alternate and potential fuel issues. After updating the D-ATIS we verified that the field visibility had dropped below 3 miles so we messaged Dispatch asking for/if we needed an alternate due to visibility. Dispatch responded that the TAF was good and we didn't need an alternate. The Captain started to become concerned because he was showing extreme areas of precipitation along the arrival and approach. Other aircraft ahead reported heavy rain. There were multiple discussion with ATC about getting in to ZZZ1 but I don't remember if they advised aircraft were going missed. We received normal vectors into the approach to Runway XYL and my first awareness of wind shear is when the aircraft in front of us called wind shear and missed. With our Windshear capabilities inoperative we were both paying very close attention to the aircraft's instruments. After intercepting the localizer and passing the Runway XYL FAF the precipitation was worsening. Somewhere between 1,700 and 1,200 feet the aircraft instruments were showing increased gains and losses and began to vibrate significantly. The Captain called wind shear and we completed the wind shear procedure up to the missed approach altitude of 3,000ft. Tower gave us runway heading and eventually turned us to the west with a hand off to approach. Prior to the handoff we began discussing fuel and alternates. The Captain suggested ZZZ2 which we messaged to Dispatch. Dispatch recommended ZZZ3. I recommend that we not go to ZZZ3 because of all the weather that was building between ZZZ1 and ZZZ3 and it also appeared weather was building at ZZZ3 per my IPAD. We asked ATC about ZZZ3 and they indicated that ZZZ3 had similar weather to ZZZ1 so we determined based on fuel and ATCs information that ZZZ3 was not an option. It seemed to me that Dispatch was adamant about diverting to ZZZ3. We also discussed other airport near ZZZ1 (off field) that could be potential alternates. After the check in with Approach they asked us our intentions which we replied stand by and began rough fuel calculations to ZZZ2. The Captain quickly determined that fuel to ZZZ2 was inadequate due to a 15-20 minute flight and approximately 2200lbs of fuel on board. (Increased fuel burn at lower altitude, storms in the area, and potential unknown issues on arrival at ZZZ2). We then asked ATC about aircraft success in to ZZZ1 which they replied that some aircraft were making it in and some were not. We then requested Runway XYR because it was the closer runway and informed ATC of our minimum fuel situation. The Captain notified the Flight Attendant, we ran the appropriate checklist (ATIS, build it, bug, brief it) and continued vectors to Runway XYR. There were two aircraft in front of us which one was able to land and another missed (aircraft in front of us) due to wind shear. After recalculating fuel requirements the Captain determined that a missed would put us in an emergency fuel situation therefore we [advised ATC]. I believe that upon intercepting the localizer we were somewhere around 1700-1800lbs. During the approach the gains and losses of altitude did not seem significant but it appeared to be difficult to maintain the localizer although I did not see anything greater than one dot. There was also added pressure to continue the approach due to the fuel situation. During the approach tower asked the aircraft that made it in where they broke out and they replied 'right at mins'. At around 800 feet I called out that I could see the ground which was not visible as we got to 500 ft. At 300 feet I called approach lights in sight 12 o'clock and continued to call out the approach lights. The aircraft began to drift right and I called the runway environment insight at 11 o'clock. Throughout this period it was apparent that the Captain was having difficulty seeing the runway. When he finally looked up and determined that he did not have adequate references, and the aircraft was not in a safe position to land, he called the missed. Immediately on the missed it was apparent the at least 90 percent of the airfield was VFR. We could see all of Runway XZ/XA, including approach paths, so we made a decision request XZ and due a VFR pattern into XZ. Tower provided we could use whatever runway we needed so we continued with XZ, entered the left crosswind, base, and landed. During the miss we did not climb to the missed approach altitude, left the gear down, and kept the flaps at nine because of the immediate landing after the missed to Runway XYR. We updated the FMS with Runway XZ and extended a line for reference because there are no approaches in our pubs for XZ. We did not rerun the performance information again because the field length was 10,500 [feet] which was longer than Runway XYR and it was an immediate landing. We also received fuel EICAS alerts during the missed and a fuel critical prior to landing XZ. All approaches were conducted with flaps 22. Emergency vehicles were on scene which we did not require. Upon taxiing into the gate the field went in to a ground stop due to lightning. In all the entire flight segment from door closed to door open was 3.4 hours. I would say that this flight provided the crew with several threats. To name a few, my PFD was inop so only the CPT could fly, windshear, GPWS, and TCAS were inop, pop-up weather wasoccurring throughout the flight route, fuel considerations, ACARS not accurate due to new AOM changes, delays prior to departure, delays in route, other aircraft encountering wind shear, and potentially an inexperienced dispatcher. There was also a significant pressure to land the second approach. I felt that we handled the situation very well as a crew and discussed and analyzed any and all potential alternatives but were cornered due to the fuel situation. I do wish I had analyzed the winds better throughout the duration and been more aware of the weather at the field during the Runway XYL wind shear event. With most of the airfield being VFR it was absolutely possible to successfully complete approaches to the XB runways and XZ/XA [runways]. The only area impacted by the weather was the XY runways approach segments. Therefore, if winds were within landing limits we could have immediately requested an alternate runway from ATC.		While conducting the setup check in ZZZ, the GPWS Inop, Windshear Inop, Terr Inop, and Terr Fail messages appeared on the EICAS. Maintenance was called and the affected system was deferred per MEL. The aircraft also had an MEL for the First Officer MFD, and an NEF item in the cockpit. ACARS would not produce accurate weight and performance numbers, and Dispatch informed us that this was a known issue. We elected to perform a manual weight and balance with the Form X. The TAF for ZZZ1 was forecasting VFR and radar looked fairly benign. The First Officer obtained the D-ATIS for ZZZ1 which showed Winds Calm, visibility 10SM, Few at 5000, Scattered at 8000, with no other weather (information M). Due to this, extra fuel for weather was not considered by us or Dispatch. While in our initial descent the First Officer and myself noticed the weather in ZZZ1 was deteriorating and sent an ACARS to Dispatch asking if we should get an alternate. Dispatch replied that no alternate was needed, and we assumed that they had more up to date information than available to us in the air. Our radar was depicting areas of extreme return (red and purple) blanketing the approach and airport with other aircraft saying it was a smooth ride, but heavy precipitation. While outside of the final approach fix on the ILS Runway XYL, other aircraft were heard going missed due to windshear. ATC informed us that some aircraft were landing and some were going around. We elected to continue the approach as we were not experiencing any abnormal flight conditions. Inside of the final approach fix, I was unable to maintain positive aircraft control on the approach path due to airspeed, altitude, and course fluctuations. I made the determination to execute a windshear escape/go around. The Flight Attendant was notified of our situation. We contacted Dispatch to request a diversion and suggested ZZZ2. Dispatch replied that ZZZ3 was a better option, but ATC informed us that ZZZ3 was having similar weather to ZZZ1. I used the FMS to determine a rough fuel burn to ZZZ2 as 1400 lbs not including any deviations or approaches. We were also low on fuel, approximately 2,200 lbs after the missed approach. We discussed and agreed that we did not want to end up in ZZZ3 with such low fuel and similar weather issues. We decided to attempt a second approach at ZZZ1 after ATC stated that some aircraft were landing successfully. It was apparent that we would be below divert and possibly final divert on the second approach so we declared Minimum Fuel. We requested ILS Runway XYR due to perceived improved conditions and the closer approach for that runway. During the approach, the Flight Attendant called and said that a passenger was in the bathroom and asked if we could delay the landing. I advised her that due to our fuel situation, it would not be possible. She was able to get the passenger back to his seat before final approach. While on the approach, we overheard the aircraft in front of us go around for windshear. Our fuel level was low and a go around would put us in a fuel critical situation. We [advised ATC] and continued the approach. We experienced difficulty in maintaining the approach course and I used my PIC authority to continue the approach since a missed approach would leave us with inadequate fuel for much option. The First Officer called approach lights in sight, and we continued to 100 feet above the field. The First Officer called Runway in sight, and when I looked up, I did not see a runway and was disoriented due to the extreme precipitation and reduced visibility and immediately executed a go around. We were now showing a very low fuel level, and it was unlikely we would have enough fuel to get to another airport or reattempt an approach. I used PIC emergency authority to request a circle to land Runway XZ. We were able to maintain visual contact with Runway XZ during the maneuver. We had no time to get any type of performance numbers, and reasoned that since itwas longer than Runway XYR we could land. The landing was conducted without incident. As we engaged E/TO Reserve on the windshear escape, I entered this in the logbook and contacted maintenance. See attachments for reference if needed.Many threats existed in this incident including: pop up weather, weight restrictions, the need for paper performance calculations, an inoperative MFD, an inoperative Windshear system, and lack of an alternate. The weather and TAF looked ok during the planning portion of the flight, it was not until enroute, that everything deteriorated. I believe we acted in the best manner possible given the stressful situation and lack of backup options (fuel and/or alternate). It was a unique scenario that required a unique response. I believe we made safe decisions based on the current parameters. In hindsight, I would have been more assertive about requesting an alternate enroute. I do not believe we could have forecasted such bad weather, which is why we did not ask to remove passengers to add fuel in ZZZ. As we could not find a suitable alternate, we had little choice once we were low on fuel than to attempt to land on any runway. A third go around would have likely had us considering an off field landing.		Air carrier flight crew reported numerous overlapping problems caused them to depart for destination airport with reduced IMC capability and minimum fuel, resulting in minimum fuel before landing.	
1816701	202106	1201-1800	C81.Airport	IL	120	2		3750			Turbulence; 10		Daylight				Corporate	Small Aircraft, High Wing, 1 Eng, Fixed Gear		2	Part 91	None	Training		Cruise		Class B ORD																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck		Pilot Flying; Single Pilot	Flight Crew Private	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 3; Flight Crew Total 319; Flight Crew Type 68		Situational Awareness; Training / Qualification; Distraction			1816701	Aircraft X	Flight Deck		Pilot Not Flying; Instructor	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Flight Instructor; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 32; Flight Crew Total 1753; Flight Crew Type 280					1816711	Airspace Violation All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural FAR; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Human Factors	Human Factors	While flying out to the practice area outside of the Bravo, I was attempting to maintain an altitude 100 feet below the shelf of the ORD Bravo airspace.  There were some up and downdrafts during the first portion of our flight. Based on the altimeter reading in the aircraft I had thought I had caught it in time, but while reviewing radar/ADS-B data on an aviation website, I concluded I did enter the airspace. It was only for a few seconds, but I do take this quite seriously. I believe an additional factor is a lack of flying currency related to the pandemic. In the future, when I feel unstable air, I will select an altitude even lower to provide more of a buffer.		Flying with a private pilot to help him get recurrent. Departed ZZZ and climbed to intermediate altitudes to avoid the Bravo airspace. Pilot selected 3,500 [feet] and acknowledged he would stay clear of the Bravo. He leveled at 3,500 [feet] but then caught an updraft and allowed his altitude to surpass the 3,600 foot floor of the ORD Class B momentarily. He called out that he had allowed the aircraft to climb 100 feet high and I immediately commanded a descent back to the selected altitude. No other aircraft were in the vicinity. Lack of currency, having been out of the cockpit for months, turbulent conditions, and the distraction of instruction as we were reviewing G1000 operation led to the momentary altitude excursion.		Small aircraft instructor and student reported momentary incursion of ORD Class B airspace, citing lack of recent flying experience as contributing factor.	
1816732	202106	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US				1165		Mixed	Rain; Turbulence; 5			500		CTAF ZZZ	Air Taxi	Super King Air 300		2	Part 135	IFR	Passenger		Landing	Vectors	Class G ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Taxi	Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Flight Instructor	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 100; Flight Crew Total 8600; Flight Crew Type 500		Time Pressure; Workload; Situational Awareness; Fatigue; Physiological - Other			1816732	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Taxi	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Flight Instructor; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 119; Flight Crew Total 2293; Flight Crew Type 315		Situational Awareness; Workload; Time Pressure; Physiological - Other; Fatigue			1816728	Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Ground Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Ground Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence; Ground Excursion Runway			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Environment - Non Weather Related; Human Factors; Weather	Environment - Non Weather Related	Shortly after touchdown on Runway XX the plane began drifting to the left, controllability was deteriorating rapidly. The SIC was attempting to correct the drifting, I (the PIC/PM) stated we're drifting left and applied Full, Authoritative, Firm pressure to the right rudder.  The plane began to straighten out and correct right. The left tires were on the edge of the runway next to the grass and water runoff, causing hydroplaning. As the plane decelerated I was able to regain control and corrected back to the center of the runway. The SIC further assisted in maintaining control of the aircraft as we could feel the tires slipping and hydroplaning. With team work and crew coordination, we regained full control of the Aircraft again and we're able to stop and clear the runway onto a taxiway.Contributing factors include, but not limited to: Weather, rain, winds/crosswinds, darkness/night, unfamiliar airport, wet runway, uncontrolled airport, fresh grass clippings and water pooling along edge of runway, long duty day greater than 12 hours,  consecutive long duty days, multiple flights in a duty day (4th),  human factors, pilot/crew exhaustion, crew dehydration due to extreme heat and humidity throughout the day, high stress loads throughout the day, get there itis, external pressure from passengers,  crews desire to succeed and make everyone happy by completing the mission.		After touchdown the aircraft began to drift to the left. Control correction was applied as the aircraft was drifting but with very little effect. As we neared the left edge of the runway the Pilot Monitoring (PM) assisted the Pilot Flying (PF) and applied full right rudder.  Due to the water collecting on the runway the aircraft did not respond very well to the rudder input until we decelerated.  At that point we had drifted slightly off the runway edge onto the grass. With crew coordination we were able to recover the aircraft back to the runway center line. We excited the runway without further incident.Contributing factors:Weather, fatigue, hot temperatures and humidity throughout the day, dehydration, lack of nutrition, end of a long duty day (arrived at ZZZ on hour 13) preceded by multiple long duty days.This event has opened my eyes a little wider to the complexity of risk assessment. In the future more emphasis will be placed on assessing external pressure, fatigue, and physiological needs.  Also more assertiveness towards these areas of risk assessment. ADM is a very complex system of checks and balances and awareness to even the slightest deviation can prevent future contributing factors to this and other events. A lot could be benefited from a personal assessment matrix to pair with a flight risk assessment matrix to help with mitigating a go/no go decision.		The crew of a King Air 300 reported loss of control during landing but were able to regain control. The crew cited weather, including wet runway and cross winds; also sighting fatigue and a long duty day as contributing factors.	
1817997	202106	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US		16		12500		VMC	Windshear; Turbulence; 10	Excessive Wind (UAS)	Daylight	20000			Government	Medium UAS (At or above 55 lbs and less than 1320 lbs)		3	Public Aircraft Operations (UAS)	None	Surveying / Mapping (UAS)		Cruise		TFR ZZZ											Authorized Third Party	Y		Special	Medium	Hybrid	BVLOS	Y	Autonomous / Fully Automated	Natural Disaster; Aircraft / UAS	N	Purchased	Number of UAS 1	Communication Systems		X	Failed; Malfunctioning																																				Indoor / Ground Control Station (UAS)		Government	Person Manipulating Controls (UAS)	Flight Crew Private; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days (UAS) 10; Flight Crew Total 300; Flight Crew Total (UAS) 2600; Flight Crew Type (UAS) 100					1817997	Indoor / Ground Control Station (UAS)		Government		Flight Crew Private; Flight Crew Flight Instructor; Flight Crew Commercial; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Remote Pilot (UAS); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days (UAS) 10; Flight Crew Total 300; Flight Crew Total (UAS) 2600; Flight Crew Type (UAS) 10		Human-Machine Interface; Troubleshooting; Confusion			1817998	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Weather; Aircraft; Environment - Non Weather Related	Ambiguous	While level at altitude we experienced wind shear the aircraft dropped 2,300 ft.We tried to communicate the radio did not work. We got a back up radio. Received a call and made the launch recovery zone back hot.Recovered altitude and continued the mission. We also record a link hit at the same time.		Due to what we believe to be strong wind shear we were unable to maintain our altitude above 10,000 feet. We began troubleshooting and attempted to communicate, but were unable to get through on our radios due to malfunction and had to grab a backup handheld. By this point we had descended roughly 2,500 feet when we established communication, at which point we made the mission hot again. We were then able to establish a climb and continue our mission.		UAS encountered wind shear and was unable to maintain altitude. Flight crew was able to regain control of the UAS and return to previous altitude.	
1818245	202106	0001-0600	IAD.Airport	DC				27000			Turbulence					Center ZDC	Air Carrier	Embraer Jet Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent		Class A ZDC																								Autopilot		X	Failed	Center ZDC	Air Carrier	Heavy Transport		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZDC																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1818245	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1818244	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Maintenance Action	Environment - Non Weather Related; Procedure; Weather	Ambiguous	We had begun a descent from our cruising altitude of FL280. Passing FL270 the aircraft violently rolled to the left and then to the right. The pilot flying used TCS (Touch Control Steering) to override the autopilot and maintain control of the aircraft. We where thrown around in our seats from the turbulence. "AUTOPILOT FAIL" message was displayed on the EICAS. The forces of the turbulence had disengaged the autopilot. We ran the QRH checklist for "AUTOPILOT FAIL" -which did not mention to re-engage the autopilot so we took the conservative approach and the rest of the flight was hand flown. We reported the severe turbulence to ATC and asked if there was any traffic nearby that may have caused the turbulence -they advised that there was a heavy Airbus 15 miles ahead. We could not be sure as to whether the turbulence was caused by clear air turbulence or wake turbulence. There was no SIGMETS for the area and the weather was clear.  I checked in with the Flight Attendant and he said that both he and the passengers where okay. I advised that he remain seated for the rest of the flight. The fasten seat belt sign was on prior to the event and was turned on at top of descent. I sent an ACARS message to Maintenance about the "AUTOPILOT FAIL" message and they responded with that it would require a maintenance write up. The rest of the flight continued uneventful. Upon arrival I wrote up 'Encountered Severe' turbulence and also a second write up for 'AUTOPILOT FAIL'. As we where waiting for the flight data recorder download to be completed by Maintenance a message populated on the EICAS. This was an additional write up 'SPOILER FAIL'. The spoilers where indicating "OPEN" on the EICAS. Speed brakes where set to "CLOSED". We inspected the spoilers outside and they where both in the deployed position. The right hand side spoiler seemed to be deployed a little less than he left hand spoiler. Even though we did not see any indications suggesting this in flight. It's a possibility that the spoilers briefly deployed in flight and possibly caused the turbulence however I feel that this is unlikely to cause the severe turbulence that we encountered. We swapped into a new tail after this 3rd write up. Check the flight data recorder to see if there was a momentary uncommanded spoiler deployment.		We started our descent from FL280 at ATC's direction.  We were descending at 290 KIAS as per ATC instruction.  Passing FL270, the aircraft uncontrollably rolled left and then right.  I used the TCS (Touch Control Steering) button to override the autopilot.  At that time, autopilot also kicked off and gave an 'Autopilot Fail' master warning message on the EICAS.  The PIC, pilot not flying, ran the QRH.  We did not consider the hardover protection to be an issue.  We flew the remainder of the flight without use of autopilot out of caution, conservative considerations. We reported the severe turbulence to ATC.  We checked EICAS and MFD system indications.  All appeared normal.  We checked with the Flight Attendant, who stated that the turbulence was unexpected, but all was normal after the turbulence.  ATC advised that there was a heavy Airbus 15 miles ahead.  Weather conditions were very clear at the time with no visible moisture and winds were relatively calm for that altitude.  We sent an ACARs message to the company to relay the situation.  Upon landing, the First Officer did a walk around and did not notice any significant details to report.  After landing and while sitting in the aircraft while waiting for it to be released for the follow-on leg, the aircraft gave a 'SPOILER FAIL' advisory on the EICAS.  Upon exterior inspection after the message, the spoilers were raised/partially extended.  The right side was lower than the left side.  Pictures were taken of the spoilers, spoiler control handle in the cockpit, and EICAS. Suggest checking aircraft system data after download to verify severe turbulence or other system anomalies.		EMB flight crew reported encountering severe turbulence in descent, unknown whether turbulence was related to wake of preceding aircraft or clear air turbulence.	
1818590	202106	0601-1200	SDF.TRACON	KY		15		2500			Turbulence; Windshear; 10					TRACON SDF	Air Taxi	Small Transport		1	Part 135	IFR	Passenger	Localizer/Glideslope/ILS ILS RWY 17L	Initial Approach	Vectors	Class C SDF																														Any Unknown or Unlisted Aircraft Manufacturer				IFR			Final Approach		Class C SDF																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Taxi	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 70; Flight Crew Total 4500; Flight Crew Type 1800					1818590												Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued Advisory / Alert; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance	Environment - Non Weather Related; Procedure; Weather	Ambiguous	We were cleared to 3,000 ft. on a wide base and a tight vector to ILS [Runway] 17L behind a heavy jet. It was very turbulent and our path put us downwind to the heavy jet. During the turbulence I lost control of the aircraft and we shot down about 500 ft. below our assigned altitude. I was in the process of correcting when the Approach Controller called out the fact we were off altitude. I told her we concurred and were correcting. I believe the issue was caused by the moderate to severe turbulence we were experiencing along with the wake from the heavy jet.				BE20 pilot reported loss of control resulting in uncommmanded 500' descent on arrival into SDF due to either severe turbulence or a wake encounter.	
1819860	202107	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US	330	10		5500		VMC	Turbulence; 10		Daylight	25000		CTAF ZZZ	FBO; Other University flight program	Small Aircraft, Low Wing, 1 Eng, Fixed Gear		2	Part 91	VFR	Training		Initial Approach	None	Class E ZZZ																														Any Unknown or Unlisted Aircraft Manufacturer				VFR					Class E ZZZ																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Personal	Single Pilot; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Private; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 35; Flight Crew Total 310; Flight Crew Type 285		Training / Qualification; Distraction; Confusion; Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1819860												Conflict NMAC	Horizontal 150; Vertical 0		Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Procedure; Human Factors	Human Factors	While maneuvering near ZZZ airport, another aircraft was on a similar flight in the same general vicinity. My instructor and I made at least six radio calls to the other aircraft by its tail number to attempt to determine its intentions, but received no response to any of them. Said aircraft did make occasional radio calls, but they did not reflect its actual flight path or maneuvering intentions. On two occasions, the aircraft maneuvered directly into our immediate vicinity, although we were able to remain clear of it in the course of our maneuvering. On the third instance, we had announced our intention to follow the other aircraft as we were both returning to ZZZ airport. We had maneuvered well outside of the traffic pattern because of other traffic in the area, continuing to follow the other aircraft. Said aircraft then announced a right turn to increase its clearance from us and other traffic, but instead initiated a descending left turn directly into our path of flight. At this point we were approximately 1,000 feet, and were forced to initiate an evasive descent of about 350 feet. We estimate the lateral distance between our aircraft and the other to be approximately 150 feet, as its registration was clearly legible. Prior to these events, our aircraft was on a short final approach as part of a practice landing, at which point the other aircraft in question cut in front of us to take off. We had announced our position and the other aircraft had responded that it was holding short of the runway, but did not do so. Throughout the duration of our flight, the aircraft in question appeared to operate without regard for us or other aircraft in its vicinity, and had we not taken the evasive action that we did, a collision would have been highly likely.				A student pilot training at an uncontrolled airport reported a NMAC with another aircraft that turned left after announcing it would turn right. The student reported the same aircraft had repeatedly maneuvered near them in different directions then announced including taking the runway to takeoff after stating they would hold short.	
1820413	202107	0001-0600	ZZZ.Airport	US				18000			Turbulence					TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	EMB ERJ 145 ER/LR		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class A ZZZ																								Air Conditioning Compressor		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument			Workload; Troubleshooting; Time Pressure			1820413	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Time Pressure; Workload; Troubleshooting			1820412	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Captain (CA) was Pilot Flying. During climb going through FL180, windshield 1 heat fail message showed in the EICAS, message went away. A few seconds later PACK 1 valve closed (cyan message) showed on the EICAS, CA recycle the pack. Both CA and First Officer (FO) observed a boringness smell after pack was cycled. Crew was notified by the Flight Attendant (FA) that there was smoke in the cabin. CA and FO donned the oxygen mask and gaggles and started an emergency descent and turning back towards airport of departure. FO notified ATC. Crew was cleared by ATC direct ZZZ. [The briefing] was done between crew and FA. FO was able to get landing data and set up the FMS for the RNAV RWY XX in ZZZ. CA landed and exited the runway safely, parked at a ramp shutdown engine #1 and opened the main cabin door. CA used engine #2 for power and chose to not start the APU since the crew wasn't sure about the integrity of the system.		On climb through 21,000 ft crew received a windshield heat fail message, CA (Captain) pulled out the QRH but the message cleared. Moments later the crew received a cyan PACK 1 valve close message. CA selected pack one off and then reset. Shortly after the crew both smelled smoke in the flight deck. Seconds after the smell of smoke the FA (Flight Attendant) called notifying crew of smoke filling up the cabin. The crew put on oxygen and smoke goggles and the CA initiated an emergency descent, the FO notified ATC and requested direct ZZZ. ATC assigned 15,000 ft direct ZZZ. Crew established that the CA will fly and the FO will work the radios and FMS. The FO informed ATC of the souls on board and fuel on board and requested visual for Runway XX from the final approach fix (ZZZZZ). FO got landing numbers for Runway XX, and verified the aircraft would not be overweight. The crew checked in with the FA 3 times before landing back at ZZZ, each check in the FA stated the smoke is slowly dissipating. Upon landing the crew exited on [taxiway] and had trucks follow them on A to an empty ramp area. The crew shut down engine 1 and opened the main cabin door, engine 2 was kept running to maintain radio contact. The APU was not started because the crew was not sure of the systems integrity. After aircraft was inspected and safe, the aircraft was brought back to the gate and deplaned.		EMB-XRJ flight crew reported a fume event during departure climb which resulted in an air return.	
1820448	202107	1801-2400	ZZZZ.Airport	FO				2600		IMC	Rain; Turbulence		Night			Center ZZZZ	Air Carrier	MD-11		2	Part 121	IFR	Cargo / Freight / Delivery		Descent																										Stall Protection System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1820448												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Requested priority for flight control malfunction on descent into ZZZZ. For approximately 5 minutes I was unable to comply with assigned altitude and course while regaining control of the aircraft. We received 3-4 "STAB OUT OF TRIM Level 2" alerts. Alert would be presented for 15-20 seconds, disappear, then return after a minute or two. Reviewed stab out of trim level 2 in QRH. Late in the descent, roughly simultaneously, AP1 (Autopilot 1) failed to capture final approach altitude of 2,600 feet, descending and FO (First Officer) reported LSAS (Longitudinal Stability Augmentation System) "ALL FAIL" alert, which I was unable to confirm as I was simultaneously manually arresting the uncommanded descent, close to ground, IFR, night in moderate turbulence and rain, slow, configure, determine what if any stab trim was available, and navigate the aircraft to final. Disconnected AP, slowed and configured the aircraft with FCP speed commands and the pedestal long trim handles. Flew the rest of the ILS XXL manually in approach/ land. Stopped on taxiway for inspection by fire personnel, nothing noted, taxi to gate.				Captain reported an LSAS system failure caused control issues on descent and approach to landing resulting in a precautionary landing.	
1820677	202107	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			50			VMC	Turbulence; Windshear; 10		Daylight	25000		CTAF ZZZ	FBO	Skyhawk 172/Cutlass 172		1	Part 91	VFR	Personal		Takeoff / Launch	Direct	Class G ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	FBO	Pilot Flying; Single Pilot	Flight Crew Private	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 2; Flight Crew Total 163; Flight Crew Type 94		Situational Awareness			1820677												Ground Event / Encounter Ground Strike - Aircraft; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Aircraft Damaged	Human Factors; Weather	Weather	Preflight and runup was normal. Engine operation was good. Began taxiing on the runway. Full power was applied and engine instruments were all in the green (RPM, pressure, and temperature). Airspeed was increasing slowly initially at first, but RPM increased as airspeed increased and I reached rotation speed of 60 to 65. I reached my normal Vx speed of 65 to 70 MPH. I had seen the climb-out of another aircraft and it appeared to be windy so I decided to add a little more speed to that as a buffer for safety. I had briefed this prior to takeoff and was aiming for 70 to 75 for the climb out, for obstacle avoidance and wind buffer. I also had briefed and prepared for a wind shift as I got above the trees, as this is a standard precaution that I have regularly made at this airport (because of the location of the trees) and was prepared to expect it (as is regularly briefed for an eventuality such as this). This is a part of my normal before takeoff emergency brief. As soon as I got nearing the tops of the trees I felt the lift reduce and the stall horn began. The controls were sluggish and I knew instantly that I needed to lower the nose to prevent a stall. I lowered the nose and tried to nurse it back up but very quickly realized something needed to be done as the runway was reducing and I didn't feel comfortable in the lift being generated being able to take me over the trees. I made a quick decision to reduce the power and attempt for an aborted landing. I pitched over, touched down, and hit the brakes. I realized that the runway was coming to an end and the hill was going to slope down. So I attempted to turn the aircraft using the rudders and was successful in slowing the aircraft and not going down into the sloped hill. Then, managed to stop the aircraft at the end of the runway. Once the aircraft was stopped, the engine was running, I pulled the mixture, switched the mags to off, and turned off the master. Post-incident inspection showed apparently minor prop damage (a ding in the prop) and nothing else appeared to be damaged. Postflight discussion with the pilot of the initial observed aircraft and he stated that he also felt the wind shift and was struggling to climb. The climbout seemed a little low to me, but it was not readily apparent to me that it was solely because of the winds. I surmise that a windshear event occurred from the changing wind direction because of the trees. Luckily, I had briefed for this eventuality and knew what to do. Things that went right:- Proper brief prior to takeoff which covered a change in wind due to the trees- Proper speed-wind buffer: this resulted in the lift that WAS available at that time and contributed to a safe landing without exceeding the critical Angle of Attack.  - Managed to stop the aircraft with minimal damage, and no occupant damageThings that went wrong:- Initial observation of the struggling climb of the aircraft on departure should have resulted in more hesitancy to depart to begin with				C172 pilot reported flying the aircraft back to the runway after noticing poor climb performance shortly after takeoff.	
1820785	202107	0601-1200				1.5	30			VMC	Turbulence; 10	Excessive Wind (UAS)	Daylight	CLR			Recreational / Hobbyist (UAS)	Micro UAS, Multirotor		1	Recreational Operations / Section 44809 (UAS)		Recreational / Hobbyist (UAS)		Cruise														N			Micro	Fixed Wing	VLOS	N	Manual Control	People / Populated Areas; Private Property; Open Space / Field	N	Purchased	Number of UAS 1																																								Outdoor / Field Station (UAS)		Recreational / Hobbyist (UAS)	Remote PIC (UAS)	Flight Crew Private; Flight Crew Remote Pilot (UAS)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days (UAS) 5; Flight Crew Total 145; Flight Crew Total (UAS) 150; Flight Crew Type (UAS) 1		Distraction			1820785												Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Ground Event / Encounter Loss Of VLOS (UAS); Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person UAS Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Lost / Unrecoverable (UAS); Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Weather; Environment - Non Weather Related	Ambiguous	Flying a 0.055 lbs fixed wing RC in an open park space in early morning. An unleashed dog came running up and created a distraction for a few seconds, at which time the aircraft drifted about 200 yards downwind over nearby residential area. Steered the aircraft back, but at one point the wind and turbulence became too much and the aircraft went down over the residential area. It has so far not been relocated. The winds were much too close to the limits for such a light aircraft.				Hobbyist UAS pilot was distracted by an animal while flying. The UAS flew into an area with high winds and the UAS pilot was unable to maintain control of the UAS which crashed a short time later.	
1821187	202107	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				5000		Marginal	Turbulence; 10		Daylight	3000		Center ZZZ	Personal	PA-23-250 Aztec		1	Part 91	IFR	Personal		Cruise	Direct	Class E ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Personal	Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Commercial; Flight Crew Flight Instructor; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 100; Flight Crew Total 2000; Flight Crew Type 19		Distraction; Situational Awareness			1821187												Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued Advisory / Alert; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance	Weather; Human Factors	Human Factors	While enroute operating IFR, deviating around buildups, we received an altitude alert from ATC. We immediately initiated corrective action and returned to our assigned altitude. At the time of the altitude alert we were off altitude by roughly 400 ft. While deviating around the buildups, we were experience moderate turbulence as well as continuous changes in updrafts and downdraft. I believe this was the cause of the deviation.				Pilot reported receiving an altitude alert from ATC while deviating around weather buildups.	
1821771	202107	1801-2400	S56.TRACON	UT				7100			Thunderstorm; Turbulence					TRACON S56	Air Carrier	Medium Transport, Low Wing, 2 Turbojet Eng		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent	Vectors	Class B SLC																												TRACON S56	Air Taxi	Small Transport, Low Wing, 2 Turbojet Eng		2	Part 135	IFR	Passenger		Descent	Vectors	Class B SLC																								Aircraft X; Facility S56.TRACON		Government	Approach; Supervisor / CIC	Air Traffic Control Fully Certified	Air Traffic Control Time Certified In Pos 1 (yrs) 11		Confusion; Situational Awareness; Time Pressure; Workload; Communication Breakdown	Party1 ATC; Party2 ATC		1821771												ATC Issue All Types; Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued Advisory / Alert; Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach	Human Factors; Airspace Structure; Procedure; Weather	Procedure	Working 4 sectors combined, and Controller in Charge. SLC was in a South Operation, Visual Airspace, Visual Approaches. Weather was VMC but visibility was diminishing and was marginal. There are thunderstorms throughout the sectors in my airspace. Lightning reported on the finals to all Runways, airspeed fluctuations reported on final, audible Wind Shear Alerts continuous all runways, 15 knot wind shift approaching the SLC Airport. Light to occasional moderate chop reported in the area. Tailwinds on final.Aircraft Y vectored to a 15 mile right base, cleared for Visual Approach to Runway 17 at SLC, switched to Tower. Aircraft X at 210 knots given descent to 8,000 feet abeam the SLC Airport, expecting Runway 16R, on the RNAV arrival being sequenced with other traffic within my sector(s) and on final. Traffic Management Unit calls down to TRACON through speaker in the Supervisor's console that Aircraft Y had a Lightning Strike and coordinated a heading 270 at 9,000 feet. I responded Radar Contact. Tower initiated the Heading 270 on a 9 mile final at 9,000 feet, which you never do, however, Aircraft Y was coordinated at 9,000 feet.  I kept Aircraft X expediting to 6,000 feet, which would not be an issue as long as Aircraft Y leveled at 9,000 feet. This was not the case as I observed Aircraft Y continuing descent through 8900 feet and continuing.  Recognizing this immediately, Aircraft Y appeared to be an Emergency aircraft not knowing what altitude he would be stopping at, I issued traffic to Aircraft X followed by a Traffic Alert with a heading 270 immediately to avoid a mid-air collision, all the while I was still not talking to Aircraft Y performing evasive maneuvers in my airspace.As soon as Aircraft X was past Aircraft Y I would then have divergence. I turned Aircraft X to heading 340, and issued a Low Altitude Alert and climbed him immediately to 7800 feet, while doing so I see Aircraft Y turning to the same north heading 340 at 8000 feet, with Aircraft X just West of Aircraft Y on a parallel course, and not talking to Aircraft Y in my airspace, not having lateral separation, I immediately turned Aircraft X to heading 310, all the while trying my best to achieve some sort of radar separation, and avoid a collision, until I find out who is talking to Aircraft Y. Aircraft Y at 8,000 feet is not what coordinated nor approved by me in my airspace, and a Heading 270 off the finals is never performed, for this exact reason. This is an unsafe operation with traffic in the downwind. Aircraft Y at 8,000 feet leaves no options at all.After determining the adjacent sector controller, who is only certified on those positions, was working Aircraft Y, in which Tower switched him to my airspace, when I had conflicting traffic. The adjacent sector controller provided zero communications to me, even though I repeatedly asked who was working Aircraft Y and what is he doing?  After I had Aircraft X squared away, they just happened to say 'Aircraft Y had your Aircraft X in sight and was maintaining visual separation.'This would have been nice to know.When an aircraft is coordinated to be 9,000 feet then have the aircraft be at 9,000 feet, not at 8,000 feet, which leaves the final sector Controller absolutely no way out. No attempt was made to by Tower who assigned 8,000 feet, or by the adjacent sector who received the aircraft at 8,000 feet to advise the sector who owns the airspace with an aircraft in direct conflict. When a heading of 270 and 9,000 feet is coordinated with another sector, a Departure Only Sector, then make sure he enters the airspace for which that specific coordination was originally made, and not another sector that has conflicting traffic with that type of coordination.No coordination or even a point out was attempted to be made to the appropriate sector in which the vital coordination directly impacts. When Visual Separation is applied between two aircraft that are in direct conflict with each other, it is absolutely a top priority to ensure that all parties involved receive this valuable bit of information.When a Controller takes it upon themselves to accept the responsibility of an aircraft in someone else's airspace at an altitude that is in direct conflict with an aircraft that you are not in communication with and at a different altitude than what was previously coordinated, then it might be a top priority to advise Controller working the aircraft in his own airspace that he is in direct communications with that visual separation is being applied. When a controller is taking evasive actions to prevent a mid-air collision with an aircraft that you are talking to, that is not in your airspace, and you are providing control instructions to, don't sit quietly when you hear 'Who is talking to Aircraft Y'?				A TRACON Controller reported an aircraft was issued incorrect go-around instructions which placed it into conflict with another aircraft and below the Minimum Vectoring Altitude. Unbeknownst to the reporter the go-around aircraft which was on another controller's frequency was maintaining Visual Separation.	
1822799	202107	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US	270	30		40000		IMC	Thunderstorm; Turbulence; 2		Daylight	42000		Center ZZZ	Corporate	Gulfstream G280			Part 91	IFR	Passenger		Climb	Direct	Class A ZZZ																								Cockpit Window		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Corporate	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Flight Instructor; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 50; Flight Crew Total 10600; Flight Crew Type 70					1822799	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Corporate	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Flight Instructor; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 50; Flight Crew Total 3500; Flight Crew Type 60					1822811	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Aircraft Damaged; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	During climbout we deviated multiple times to avoid convective buildups, which is normal and air traffic control helped us greatly. We experienced intermittent light turbulence in the climb as we deviated around heavy precipitation and convective cloud build ups that we were observing on weather radar.Our filed altitude was 41,000 feet, we were cleared to climb to 40,000 feet and were informed we would be cleared to climb once we were clear of some conflicting traffic.We leveled off at 40,000 feet and were clear of the convective thunderstorm buildups but were still in Instrument Meteorological Conditions (IMC). FO and I were discussing potentially climbing to 45,000 to get on top of the IMC. Approximately 30 minutes into the flight as we were level at 40,000 feet I heard a pop, looked up and saw the right forward windscreen was completely shattered. FO noticed it at the same time and I said get our oxygen masks on immediately. We donned our masks, advised ATC and asked for a descent and requested a diversion to the nearest airport with emergency services. Air traffic informed us it was ZZZ and we elected to divert there. We informed our passengers of the situation, to please be seated with the seat belts securely fastened and requested that if the oxygen masks deploy to please don them. I continued flying the aircraft as FO ran the QRH Emergency Checklist for a shattered windshield. FO then set up our flight management system for approach and landing into ZZZ as I guided us in.The rest of the descent, approach and landing went without incident.		During climbout we deviated multiple times to avoid convective buildups, which is normal and air traffic control helped us greatly.  We experienced intermittent light turbulence in the climb as we deviated around heavy precipitation and convective cloud build ups that we were observing on weather radar.Our filed altitude was 41,000 feet, we were cleared to climb to 40,000 feet and were informed we would be cleared to climb once we were clear of some conflicting traffic.We leveled off at 40,000 feet and were clear of the convective thunderstorm buildups but were still in Instrument Meteorological Conditions (IMC). The Captain and I were discussing potentially climbing to 45,000 to get on top of the IMC.  Approximately 30 minutes into the flight as we were level at 40,000 feet I heard a pop, looked up and saw the right forward windscreen was completely shattered. We noticed it at the same time and I said get our oxygen masks on immediately.  We donned our masks, advised ATC and asked for a descent and requested a diversion to the nearest airport with emergency services.		Gulfstream G280 flight crew reported that the First Officer's windshield shattered at top of climb, causing a diversion and precautionary landing.	
1825984	202107	0001-0600	ZZZ.ARTCC	US						VMC	Turbulence		Night			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	MD-11		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise	Vectors	Class A ZZZ																								Hydraulic Main System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Communication Breakdown; Troubleshooting	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Other		1825984												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft; Procedure; MEL; Human Factors	Aircraft	After passing ETP (Equal Time Point) on RZZZ1 eastbound past ZZZZZ at flight level 380. level 1 hydraulicaulic pump 3R off during cruise with associated amber 'T' displayed above pump on hydraulicraulic synoptic page. This is the standby pump for Engine 3. It was indicating an overheated condition. Operations notified via ACARS. Satcom was not working properly. Could not connect. Referenced QRH (Quick Reference Handbook). Next, level 1 hydraulic 3 off, system pressure zero, temp 35C, quantity decreasing rapidly from 10.4 gal to 1.8 gal. Pressure zero, temp 35C, both hydraulic 3 pumps off, system in auto. Referenced QRH, stabilized situation. Over the next 2 hours, contacted Maintenance, as a crew using CRM (Crew Resource Management) reviewed QRH and other sources for next possible scenarios. Attention to level 2, hydraulic 3 fail checklist. Observed during that time period, hydraulically quantity 3 increased to approximately 8.9 gal with a temperature of 3C prior to descending to ZZZ. With more than one plan/upcoming scenario ready to go, as a crew we started the descent into ZZZ. Direct ZZZZZ1 6000 ft. ILS XXR. We started to configure for the ILS at 16 miles due to a request from ATC to slow further to 180 kts. 'Slats extend'...about 4 seconds later level 2 alert hydraulic 3 fail. Complied with QRH, requested priority handling with ZZZ Tower, manually lowered the landing gear and proceeded to land uneventfully on runway XXR. AP2, flaps 35, no auto brakes, 400K landing weight. Restrictions for PW (Pratt Whitney) engine thrust reverser inop and limitations for turning right. Once clear of the runway we worked with ZZZ airport operations to confirm there was no excessive fluid/fire coming from the airplane. As expeditiously and safely as possible we taxied clear of the north runway complex so other aircraft could land. In contact with ZZZ Ramp/ZZZ Tower we devised a safe taxi plan to exit the North complex and meet a tug. Once at the designated point ZZZ Maintenance showed up promptly and towed us back.				MD-11 First Officer reported loss of the #3 hydraulic system in flight which resulted in a precautionary landing.	
1826245	202107		ZZZ.TRACON	US	090	25		8000		IMC	Turbulence; Thunderstorm; Rain; 3			1400		TRACON ZZZ	Corporate	Citation V/Ultra/Encore (C560)			Part 91	IFR	Passenger		Descent	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Corporate	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Flight Instructor; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 250; Flight Crew Total 38000; Flight Crew Type 1800		Situational Awareness			1826245												Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Weather	Weather	Signed on with ZZZ Approach and they cleared me to descend to 8000 ft. and asked to turn to 170 heading. Shortly after I turned I realized they had vectored me right into a cell and we were simultaneously leveling at 8,000 ft. I was on the autopilot and the turbulence was severe enough that it disengaged and we caught a huge downdraft. The airplane was still trimmed nose down in a descent when the autopilot turned off. The aircraft descended several hundred feet below the assigned 8,000 ft. MSL and lost 50 kts. in airspeed in a matter of a few seconds. So as not to impose heavy load factors on the aircraft and passengers I recovered slowly and climbed back to the assigned 8,000 ft.				C560 Pilot reported being vectored by ATC directly into a cloud cell and lost several hundred feet of altitude and 50 knots of airspeed resulting in a subsequent slow climb back to assigned altitude.	
1826656	202107	1201-1800						34000		IMC	Thunderstorm; Turbulence		Daylight				Air Carrier	EMB ERJ 170/175 ER/LR		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class A ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Total 3240		Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Maintenance		1826656	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Total 4285		Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Maintenance		1826659	Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Maintenance; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Human Factors; Procedure; Weather	Weather	On a step climb around FL300, between ZZZ and ZZZZZ, we noticed a weather cell we wanted to deviate around. The Captain had already briefed the FAs (Flight Attendants) about the en route weather, that we would most likely have some bumps, that we were keeping the seat belt sign on, and to use caution. We were in full IMC and our radar return showed that a turn to the left would be best. We requested an initial left turn of 20 degrees but almost immediately we encountered heavy and extreme precipitation and ultimately had to turn 80 degrees. We were given the necessary deviations but then told to go direct ZZZZZ when able as well as climb to FL340. We also encountered moderate and possibly severe turbulence. We experienced both positive and negative G's. The speed tape never went into the red but the trend vector showed both rapid climbing and simultaneous descending trends that took up the entire top to bottom range of the speed tape. The plane was moving quite a bit as we were both in updrafts and then subsequent downdrafts getting bumped around. After about 5 minutes when we could actually get everything sorted out, we called back to check on the status of both the Flight Attendants and Passengers. FA1 answered and she proceeded to tell us there was an accident in the aisle and she would call us back. FA2 was about mid aircraft doing her beverage service when we encountered the worst of the turbulence. She thought she had hit her head and beverages had been thrown from the cart and some passengers tray tables into the ceiling. We consulted our FOM and made the decision we needed to contact Dispatch and make them aware to coordinate with [Maintenance Control]. We continued to keep checking on the FAs for the rest of the flight and as we got closer, FA1 called and said FA2 had requested In-Flight to meet us at the gate. Upon block in, [flight attendant representative] and member of [Company] met the aircraft. CA (Captain) called Dispatch/[Maintenance Control] to explain event and to assess if an aircraft inspection for severe turbulence was required. Based on our reading and interpretation of FOM on the definition of severe turbulence, we determined we encountered severe turbulence. However, [Maintenance Control] said because the autopilot did not disengage, that there was no 'momentary loss of aircraft control' and it was only moderate turbulence which does not require an aircraft inspection. The aircraft was not removed from service, boarding for the next flight started immediately, and [Company] took FA2 for a medical evaluation.Pilot interpretation of FOM differed from [Maintenance Control]. [Maintenance Control] did not accept our interpretation of severe turbulence. It kept them from having to inspect the aircraft and delaying the flight schedule. Any disconnection of autopilot is apparently the determining factor in deciding if aircraft control is lost. There needs to be a more clear definition of what severe versus moderate turbulence is and pilot experience should be held in account. Safety should always be the priority over schedule integrity.		On final climb out from FL300 to FL340 between ZZZ and ZZZZZ intersection, we had to deviate for weather. Seat belt sign was on and FA (Flight Attendant) briefing and PA to passengers included weather/ thunderstorms in the area and to expect a bumpy ride on the climb out. We were in and out of IMC conditions as the weather radar depicted a buildup. We discussed a plan for deviations prior to the radio frequency change that we received at the same time. Originally asked for 20 degree left turn, ATC approved it and said no other traffic in the area do what we need to and cleared back direct ZZZZZ when able.Simultaneously, we encountered heavy to extreme precipitation which FA1 later said she could hear In the galley. FA1 started to clean up galley and observed FA2 in the middle of aircraft conducting beverage service. We continued the left turn to approximately a 270 degree heading due to the intensity of precipitation and increasing turbulence, while still trying to climb to FL340. In the turn and climb, we encountered moderate to potentially severe turbulence: VSI showed +/- 2000 fpm climb, ASI showed a white positive and negative trend vector for the entire range of speed tape, and we experienced zero G and positive G due to the turbulence.Once clear of the weather, we immediately called the FAs to get a status of the cabin. FA1 answered and she said there was an accident in the aisle. A few minutes later, she called back and reported that FA2 believes she hit her head on the ceiling and the surrounding passengers in the area agreed. Beverages had spilled on to passengers and were also dripping from the ceiling. Throughout the rest of the flight, we were in constant communication with the cabin on the status of FA2 and the passengers, as well as Dispatch/[Maintenance Control] via ACARS. FA1 requested that inflight meet us at the gate for FA2.Upon block in, a member from inflight and from [Company] met the aircraft. CA (Captain) called Dispatch/[Maintenance Control] to explain the event and to assess if an aircraft inspection for severe turbulence was required. Based on our reading and interpretation of FOM on the definition of severe turbulence, we determined we encountered severe turbulence. However, [Maintenance Control] said because the autopilot did not disengage, that there was no 'momentary loss of aircraft control' and it was only moderate turbulence which does not require an aircraft inspection. The aircraft was not removed from service, boarding for the next flight started immediately, and inflight took FA2 for a medical assessment.Pilot interpretation of company FOM definition and procedures differed from [Maintenance Control] definition and interpretation of company FOM defined severe turbulence [Maintenance Control] decided NOT to err on the side of safety and do a precautionary severe turbulence inspection of the aircraft due to their interpretation of the FOM severe turbulence definition, solely based on the fact that the autopilot did not disconnect during the event in order to save the integrity of the flight schedule. In the interest of safety, a severe turbulence inspection should have been conducted and a better definition of severe turbulence needs to be updated in company FOM defining what 'momentary loss of aircraft control' means. Safety should always be a priority over schedule integrity.		EMB-175 Flight Crew reported turbulence injured a Flight Attendant. The flight crew reported the event to Maintenance as severe turbulence, but Maintenance overruled them and stated it was moderate turbulence, reportedly to not have to inspect the aircraft and delay the flight.	
1828079	202107	1201-1800	E10.TRACON	CA	330	70		8500		Mixed	Turbulence; Rain; 2		Daylight	10000		TRACON E10	Corporate	Medium Transport, Low Wing, 2 Turbojet Eng		2	Part 91	None	Test Flight / Demonstration		Descent	None	Class E E10																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Corporate	Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Flight Instructor; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 46; Flight Crew Total 5400; Flight Crew Type 470		Communication Breakdown; Confusion; Situational Awareness; Workload	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1828079												Airspace Violation All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural FAR; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence; Inflight Event / Encounter VFR In IMC; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT	Horizontal 2000; Vertical 500		Automation Aircraft Terrain Warning; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Human Factors; Weather	Human Factors	[We were] operating in a military restricted area where range rules [were] to remain in VMC. Due to increasing cloud coverage, we had to knock off our planned flight early and began preparing for return to base (ZZZ). The PIC was managing the descent while I managed the checklists. We leveled at 10,000 ft. MSL to stay below cloud deck. We were flying southbound with mountainous terrain to the west. Cloud coverage was along the entire route ahead of us, and a hot restricted area was to the east (with cloud coverage). As a crew, we discussed the forward visibility which we estimated to be about 30 NM from our present location. It looked like some rain showers ahead down the valley, and cloudy conditions over the mountains (with maybe a little better clearance in the immediate direction). On board, radar was not indicating anything appreciable returns, and weather showed just a few pixels of green.As we approached the weather, the PIC said we should turn right as he thought the weather looked better towards the mountains. I stated that the weather ahead looked better in my opinion, as it looked like we would go under some of the rain showers in the valley. PIC said he thought it was IMC ahead, and started turning right towards the mountains. I reiterated that going down the valley was our best option, especially due to the mountainous terrain to our right. He mentioned he has flown in this area hundreds of times and he knew this would be better. I again reiterated that not only were we turning towards mountains, but there was a National Park over there which required us to stay 3,000 ft. above the terrain, and would limit how low we could go to avoid weather. I estimated that going down the valley could have got us as low as 5,000 ft. MSL safely (about 2,500 ft. AGL). PIC stated that going down the valley would be impossible for us to stay VMC and maintained the heading towards the mountains. I told him that I did not see what he was seeing, and he stated it would work fine.As we approached the first ridge the PIC climbed to 11,000 ft. MSL to stay safely above the terrain (although I estimate we needed to be about 13,000 ft. MSL to avoid National Park overflight). As we continued south, we could see that it was closing in and that the bases were getting lower, so the PIC started stepping down in altitude to approximately 8,500 ft.' MSL. At one point, the PIC was getting nervous, and asked me if we should turn around. As I was super focused on trying to stay ahead of the PIC, I told him whatever you do, do not turn right, as the Terrain display was showing a 10,200 ft. peak while we were descending through about9,500 ft. MSL. The visibility was becoming much reduced and we entered some light rain showers. As we were navigating mountainous terrain in visibility that were maybe 1 NM at times, the GPWS alerted us to 'TERRAIN, TERRAIN' followed immediately by 'PULL UP, PULL UP, PULL UP.' I immediately called climb-climb-climb. The PIC's only response was to pull back on the yoke (as opposed to our training which calls for disconnect AP (Auto Pilot)/AT (Auto Throttles), pitch to PLI (Pitch Limit Indicator) and max power). I put my hand on the throttles and started to push up about the same time the AT's responded to increasing power. As the climb commenced the Terrain Warning silenced, however we did encounter solid IMC. Shortly after we broke through the line of showers, [we] proceeded back to the airfield [in] VFRThere were multiple opportunities to prevent this from happening. The first and foremost that comes to mind is that as soon as we disagreed on the course of action, we should have entered a hold to discuss it. This would have given us time to regroup and may have resulted in a better decision. Additionally (in hindsight), we could have requested an IFR flight plan back to the landing airfield per the range rules. However, we were so focused on remaining VMC that we overlooked that option, probably due to task saturation. Personalities and organizational culture are also paramount to conducting safe flight operations, and these subjects will be presented to leadership as a result of this incident.				Corporate aircraft pilot reported flying VFR and entering IMC over mountainous terrain resulting in GPWS alert and evasive action taken in order to return to VFR flight.	
1829622	202108	1801-2400	PWK.Airport	IL				1800		IMC	Thunderstorm; Windshear; Turbulence		Night			TRACON C90	Air Taxi	Light Transport, Low Wing, 2 Turbojet Eng		2	Part 135		Passenger		Descent		Class E C90																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Taxi	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1829622	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Taxi	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine						1829623	ATC Issue All Types; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Human Factors; Weather	Weather	ATC given heading for vector for weather and entered cloud with severe turbulence with down draft auto pilot disconnected, and power and pitch up with continued down draft about 1,200 ft. altitude lost and severe turbulence until aircraft was out the cloud, report it to ATC.We were vectored by ATC and we did not see the weather on the radar in IMC.		We were getting vectored by ATC to go around weather and on the final vector before the occurrence we got vectored into a cloud that began to produce moderate turbulence. We attempted to get deviations but could not reach the Controller. It was then that we experienced what we described as a severe downward wind shear that made us lose control of the aircraft and the aircraft had an immediate 4000+ ft. per minute drop in altitude. Autopilot disengaged and we found ourselves at 1800 ft. MSL and I began to slow to maneuvering speed to regain control of the aircraft. Once I did I immediately began a climb and we finally had the Controller provide deviation instructions and we exited the cloud and the turbulence subsided.We had nothing on radar and thought the Controller was guiding us away from thunderstorms but he was not seeing it as well.		Flight crew reported a loss of aircraft control in severe turbulence.	
1830724	202108	1201-1800	ZZZ.TRACON	US				5000		VMC	Rain; Thunderstorm; Turbulence		Daylight			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Final Approach	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 85; Flight Crew Total 3687; Flight Crew Type 3687		Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1830724	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 81; Flight Crew Total 3888; Flight Crew Type 140		Communication Breakdown	Party1 ATC; Party2 Flight Crew		1830733	ATC Issue All Types; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT		N	Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Became Reoriented	Environment - Non Weather Related; Human Factors	Human Factors	Flight was deviating off the ZZZZZ arrival due to thunderstorm activity. Subsequently cleared direct ZZZZZ and told to descend to 5,000 feet. While it is possible both the Captain and FO (First Officer) heard the wrong altitude, the FO read back 5,000 feet. Even though the published altitude is 6,000 feet, this did not seem unusual to us as we were flying below storm overhangs and had barely flown any of the arrival up to this point.  After reaching 5,000, the controller asked why we were at 5,000. We informed him that was our cleared altitude. He informed us 6,000 was the minimum vectoring altitude.		On arriving into ZZZ, we were taken off the ZZZZZ4 arrival due to thunderstorms in the terminal area. Upon contact with Terminal Radar Control we were issued radar vectors. The Air Traffic Controller stated that we would rejoin the arrival at ZZZZZ. We were told to fly heading 090 and cleared to descend from 9,000 to 6,000. The clearance was read back and confirmed by both of us. Shortly thereafter the controller gave us a further descent down to 5,000. The clearance was read back and confirmed by both of us. After the clearance was read back, ATC never intervened on the altitude that we read back. ATC then told us to fly direct to ZZZZZ. Whilst we were level at ZZZZZ at 5,000, ATC stated that [the next fix] had an altitude of 6,000 ft. I stated that we were cleared by him down to 5,000. There were no traffic conflicts.		Flight crew reported descending 1000 feet below minimum altitude on approach as cleared by ATC.	
1830939	202108	1201-1800	ZZZ1.ARTCC	US				43000		IMC	Turbulence; Thunderstorm; Hail		Daylight			Center ZZZ1	Fractional	Citation V/Ultra/Encore (C560)		2	Part 135	IFR	Passenger		Cruise	Airway Q40	Class A ZZZ1																								Engine		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Fractional	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Commercial	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 200; Flight Crew Total 2200; Flight Crew Type 149		Workload; Time Pressure; Troubleshooting			1830939												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Aircraft; Company Policy; Human Factors	Aircraft	Moderate turbulence at 43,000 feet, right engine flame out, requested priority handling immediately at ZZZ.				A fractional company pilot reported right engine failure. The pilot descended, restarted the engine, and continued to their destination instead of landing at the nearest suitable airport.	
1831146	202108	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				35000		IMC	Hail; Rain; Thunderstorm; Turbulence		Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Taxi	Beechjet 400		2	Part 135	IFR	Passenger		Cruise	Vectors	Class A ZZZ																								Weather Radar		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Troubleshooting; Situational Awareness; Workload			1831146												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Aircraft; Human Factors; Weather	Weather	When cruising at FL350 and deviating right of course for thunderstorm avoidance on a heading of 075 we began to encounter blow off and overhangs from other cells in the area reducing visibility to IMC. ATC was overwhelmed and could not give us requested higher altitude and due to high traffic in the area we did not initiate a further deviation right of course until we could ask ATC. Our radar was operating and being manipulated by the Captain showing no cells along our route of flight. We did not realize our radar had failed (no cockpit indications) and suddenly we were in a extreme thunderstorm cell. Our altitude deviated considerably 1,500 feet up and 700 feet down from FL350. We encountered light hail extreme precipitation and severe turbulence. Upon exiting the cell we immediately flew towards clear air and made the decision to divert to ZZZ. Suspecting possible negative G limits had been exceeded and concerned about possible aircraft damage. ATC was able to immediately accommodate our diversion and I landed on runway XX at ZZZ without further issue. The radar and possible turbulence damage was written up in the maintenance log and the company notified according to SOPs and Op Specs. Ensure all radars are operational and checked regularly by Maintenance. Also release this incident to the entire pilot group so that they are aware of this possible issue when operating near thunderstorms.				First Officer reported the aircraft weather radar failed in heavy weather and the flight crew elected to divert to a precautionary landing.	
1831728	202108	0601-1200	OAK.Airport	CA			700			IMC	Turbulence; 10		Daylight	700		Tower OAK	Corporate	Light Transport, Low Wing, 2 Turbojet Eng		1	Part 91	IFR	Passenger		Initial Climb	SID CNDEL FOUR	Class C OAK																												Tower OAK	Air Carrier	A321		2		IFR	Passenger		Initial Climb		Class C OAK																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Corporate	Pilot Flying; Single Pilot	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Other Commercial Rotorcraft	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 50; Flight Crew Total 9000; Flight Crew Type 5500		Situational Awareness			1831728												ATC Issue All Types; Deviation - Altitude Crossing Restriction Not Met; Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Environment - Non Weather Related; Human Factors; Procedure	Ambiguous	I was preparing to conduct a Part 91 IFR flight from OAK. My clearance was Runway 30, CNDEL4 Departure EBAYE AVE EHF and to climb via the SID. Upon reaching Runway 30, I was holding on Taxiway Whiskey behind an A321. After the A321 started its takeoff roll, I watched and noted the point at which it lifted off. At the same time I was instructed to 'Line up and wait'. After what I thought to be a very short time, less than a minute from lining up, I was cleared for takeoff with a caution warning for wake turbulence. Upon reaching Vr and prior to the A321's rotation point, I rotated and began my initial climb. I had 1900 ft set in my Altitude Select as I was to cross LECHE at or below 2,000 ft, on a heading of 296 degrees which I had set on my DG's bug. At approximately 600 ft I engaged the autopilot. Almost immediately after entering IMC conditions at approximately 700 ft, I entered very heavy turbulence. I disengaged the autopilot at which time the plane entered an un-commanded roll to the left. I tried to recover but the plane was shaking very violently and kept wanting to violently roll to the left between 35 to 40 degrees of bank angle. The plane was really not responding to my control inputs, at least not according to my flight director. I really had no idea what was happening at the time and was very startled. At first I thought I might have a control surface failure. Then I realized that I must have flown through and was now within the A321 wake vortices.  I had a very hard time controlling the aircraft. I was worried if I could not keep the plane out of an upset condition, I was way too low for a successful recovery. I could also hear that my passengers were very uncomfortable.  At this point, I just reverted to flying 101, aviate, navigate, communicate. I hit my Takeoff/Go-Around switch which also disengaged the yaw damper and put my command bars at a 10 degree nose up and wings level command. At that point I was just trying to concentrate on putting the airplane symbol into the command bars and just keep wings level. All the while keeping my eye on my TCAS for any aircraft near or around me. (I never got one traffic warning or alert during this whole event). At some point a controller came on and began giving me vectors. I had not yet talked to the controllers or advised them of my situation as I had not gotten to the "communicate" part yet. I was also told to squawk my assigned transponder code. I had selected the code prior to taxi but failed to enter it. At approximately 1800 feet I began to try to level the airplane but was having a hard time doing so. I did not want to push down too hard and put the plane in an excessive negative G situation. I think I passed the max altitude by 150 ft but, immediately corrected to 1800 ft. When I was switched to the next frequency I was still attempting to regain my composure and just follow the controller's instructions. The turbulence completely disappeared out of about 2,500 feet. Out of 10,000 feet, I cancelled IFR and continued VFR with flight following. The rest of the flight was uneventful until I was advised by NorCal of a possible pilot deviation and was given a phone number to call upon landing.  How the problem arose: Inadvertent flight into wake turbulence. Contributing Factors: Too early release behind a heavy aircraft. IMC Conditions. Failure to ask for a possible short delay to my takeoff clearance due to wake turbulence. How it was discovered: Difficulty in controlling the aircraft due to violent and uncommanded aircraft attitudes. Corrective Actions: Maintain wings level attitude. Follow ATC Instructions. Perceptions: Fear of possible loss of control in IMC conditions and very low altitude for recovery. Judgements: Just fly the plane. Aviate, Navigate, Communicate. Decisions: Do whatever it takes to keep wings level attitude. Factors affecting the quality of human performance: The startle effect. Actions or Inactions: Have hand written, 'Enter Squawk Code',in my Pre-Taxi Checklist. Just did what I could to fly my plane out of an inadvertent but bad situation.				Light Transport Corporate Pilot reported encountering wake turbulence departing OAK in trail of an A321.	
1832343	202108	0601-1200	ZZZZ.Airport	FO						IMC	Turbulence		Daylight			Center ZZZZ; Tower ZZZZ	Air Carrier	MD-11		4	Part 121	IFR	Cargo / Freight / Delivery		Initial Climb; Climb	Vectors	Class B ZZZZ1																								Normal Brake System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument			Troubleshooting			1832343												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft; Human Factors; Procedure	Aircraft	After the Pilot Flying called for 'Slats retract, after takeoff checklist,' I promptly moved the flap/slat handle to RET and after slats retraction we received the Level 1 Alert HYD 1 OFF. After running the QRH and as a crew we elected to continue to ZZZZ. Once in cruise we discussed potential outcomes of our situation we planned that we may have a total HYD 1 Failure and discussed what we wanted to do if we did. After planning to configure early to see if our problem would manifest into a failure we did indeed have a total HYD 1 FAIL level 2. We [requested priority handling] with ZZZZ Approach asked to continue on our current heading for 5-10 minutes and we would get back with them after we accomplished our checklists. We were given Runway XXR as an option, given that this runway was the longest we elected to utilize this option. We ran the QRH procedure and discussed potential threats and issues. Given that our nose wheel steering would be limited to the left we elected to stop on the runway and request to be towed in via a tug. We informed ZZZZ Approach of this issue and let them know souls on board and approximately how much fuel we had remaining. After being handed off to ZZZZ Tower I once again reminded them we were not going to be able to clear the runway due to our failed condition. I thought this might be important because of the restricted visibility and poor runway conditions so no one would commence a takeoff or an approach with our airplane parked on an active runway. Tower understood clearly our intentions there was no miscommunication. Our approach was normal and normal procedures were utilized in the event. The airplane performed exactly as we anticipated. After engine cool down, assuring the brakes we not too hot and we started the APU we let Tower know our engines were shut down and the aircraft was safe to approach with our company tug. As Company Maintenance personnel entered through the FWD avionics compartment and communicated with Company ground personnel via his handheld radio and would sit in the CA (Captain)'s seat while in control of the aircraft's brakes. I continued to perform Pilot Monitoring duties to obtain a taxi clearance and monitored the aircraft until we set the parking brake at Gate XX. The event overall was a well orchestrated event, [the] Captain showed excellent leadership, CRM and cockpit management skills. [The] Captain reacted calmly and confidently as I backed him up during the process. I posed questions and [a] hypothetical to ensure we had all of our bases covered and [the] Captain was very respective the few I had to offer. [The] Captain demonstrated by example exactly how a captain should react and respond to a potential dangerous situation. I would also like to note that ZZZZ Approach and Tower performed above expectations despite a slight language barrier we all had an understanding of what we needed and they were able to provide that with ease. Lastly ZZZZ Company Operations performed flawlessly we couldn't have asked for anything more. After landing in ZZZZ, Maintenance personnel identified the issue right away it was stated it was an 'auto brake controller', that caused the #1 HYD system to leak all of its fluid and fail. I have not followed up to see if this indeed was the case after maintenance completed its final inspection and repair.				MD-11 First Officer reported hydraulic system 1 failure due to a faulty auto brake controller.	
1832557	202108	0001-0600	ZZZ.ARTCC	US						VMC	Turbulence		Night			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	A300		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								Air Data Computer		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Check Pilot; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Troubleshooting			1832557												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy		N	Automation Aircraft Other Automation; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Became Reoriented; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Approaching ZZZ (50 minutes into flight), Captain's airspeed tape went to zero and altimeter jumped to 250 ft. high at FL340. FD (Flight Director), AT (Autothrottles), and AP (Autopilot) went away accordingly. Captain's airspeed slowly recovered and failed multiple times. Priority handling was asked with no firetruck response needed. Diversion was coordinated with Dispatch to ZZZ. First Officer's data was always correct. Approach and landing were uneventful. ZZZ Approach and Tower were very helpful with groundspeed calls to verify airspeed. The courier compartment was wet when we arrived at the plane and an E&E Inspection was accomplished for water intrusion before departure. [This was probably caused by] ADC failure.				A300 Captain reported diverting due to unreliable airspeed and altitude indications.	
1833443	202106	0601-1200	MRY.Tower	CA	098	6		1700		Mixed	Windshear; Turbulence; 8		Daylight	600		Tower MRY	Personal	Small Aircraft, High Wing, 1 Eng, Fixed Gear		2	Part 91	IFR	Training		Final Approach	Other ILS	Class C MRY																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Personal	Instructor; Pilot Not Flying	Air Traffic Control Fully Certified; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Flight Instructor; Flight Crew Commercial	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 141; Flight Crew Total 415; Flight Crew Type 130		Time Pressure; Distraction			1833443												Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT	Vertical 600	N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Air Traffic Control Issued Advisory / Alert; Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach; Flight Crew FLC complied w / Automation / Advisory; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Diverted	Weather	Weather	Coming in on final approach on the ILS into MRY, we experienced wind shear over the water at about 6 miles from the airport. The rough air pushed us below glide path, receiving a low altitude alert from tower the decision was made to go missed. We continued missed approach and radar vectors to our filed alternate ZZZ.				Flight Instructor reported encountering wind shear on final approach which pushed them below glide path resulting in a Low Altitude Alert from ATC and a missed approach.	
1834493	202108	1201-1800	ZZZZ.ARTCC	FO						IMC	Rain; Turbulence					Center ZZZZ	Air Carrier	MD-11		2	Part 121	IFR	Cargo / Freight / Delivery	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Cruise	Direct	Class A ZZZ																								Engine		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Relief Pilot	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Distraction; Situational Awareness; Troubleshooting			1834493												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence		N	Automation Aircraft Other Automation; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated	Aircraft; Weather	Ambiguous	Cruising at Mach .82/FL310, the aircraft entered an area of heavy precipitation. Engine ignition override was on. Crew observed N2 roll back to approximately 35% and received a Level 2 ENG 2 RPM LO. The throttle did not move and all other engine parameters were normal, EGT, oil pressure/temp. There was no yaw. The crew addressed the QRC, but the engine N2 RPM increased back to normal indications before they could run it. The rollback lasted less than 2 minutes. All indications were normal for remainder of the flight. Unknown cause.				MD-11 Second Officer reported encountering heavy precipitation in cruise flight when engine N2 began to roll back with a caution message, leading to no throttle movement.	
1836299	202109	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US				3100			Windshear; Thunderstorm; Rain; Turbulence		Night			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	A321		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Initial Approach		Class B ZZZ																								Autoflight System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Troubleshooting; Workload; Fatigue			1836299	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Fatigue; Troubleshooting; Workload			1836313	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; Flight Crew FLC complied w / Automation / Advisory; Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Software and Automation; Weather; Human Factors; Aircraft	Aircraft	I was the First Officer and pilot monitoring on our flight from ZZZ1 to ZZZ. This was the last leg of a three-day trip. We taxied out on time and parked to wait out a ground stop. ZZZ was just starting to get the worst of a tropical storm. Convective SIGMETs for embedded thunderstorms covered much of the mid-Atlantic states. The ground stop lifted and we were able to depart 2 hours and 2 minutes after pushback.The first part of the flight went fine, and we were clear of weather until contacting Approach Control. The controller gave us good vectors, but we still had to penetrate some moderate rain showers. Our aircraft was an older 321 with CRT screens and the old radar display, and it was night by this point. This made it difficult to know the intensity until we were very close to the depicted weather. We were able to keep clear of magenta turbulence indications on the display, yet we still encountered moderate to severe turbulence with speed indications +/- 20 kt. below 8,000 ft. Upon turning base, we were in a clear area and could see one more cell crossing the final approach course. Traffic ahead that had just flown through the same cell reported moderate rain and smooth ride. Our ride was worse than that, and after configuring we got an aural predictive windshear warning, 'GO AROUND, WINDSHEAR AHEAD.' We initiated a go-around. The ride was moderate and there were big airspeed fluctuations, but we didn't get a reactive windshear alert. Approach vectored us back for another ILS. The downwind took us around more moderate intensity rain and turbulence. Approach informed us that traffic behind us had landed, and that we were the only one to go around so far. I believe two aircraft landed between our first approach and our second attempt.Our second approach also resulted in a go-around. When we encountered the heaviest part of the rain, airspeed loss was unacceptable and we initiated the go-around. We did not get a predictive windshear alert. I don't recall if we had a reactive windshear warning, but almost instantly we had multiple ECAM warnings and cautions. The autopilot disconnected and Captain assumed manual control - he was pilot-flying throughout the event. The ride felt very similar to how the simulator presents a windshear event. Recovery felt like it took forever. With no autopilot and multiple F/CTRL ECAM messages, we were fully caught up in recovering to a normal state. We climbed up to 3000, which was [the] missed approach altitude. Flight director guidance was fluctuating a lot, and we turned off FD 1 and 2.Re-configuring the aircraft after the approach was delayed, the airspeed trend arrow indicated a very imminent flap over-speed condition. I asked the Captain if he wanted flaps up, and then raised them when he indicated in the affirmative. My perception here was that we were in level flight and accelerating rapidly. In retrospect, I misjudged our energy state and failed to follow the prescribed schedule for flap retraction, retracting them too quickly. As the flaps were retracting, we were somewhere around 3,100 ft. correcting for 3,000, in moderate turbulence. These factors all compounded, resulting in a low-energy state and stall warning indication. I'm not sure what the auto thrust was commanding at this point, or if it had reduced thrust for us to descend back to 3000. I believe I yelled, 'Nose down!' and Captain responded that he was full forward stick. I momentarily pushed my flight stick forward and was corrected by the aural 'dual input' alert. Captain recovered the aircraft. I gave ATC a quick description of our flight control computer problems and they climbed us to 5000 on vectors. We began the process of going through the ECAM malfunctions, with me working the ECAM. The autopilot was inoperative for the rest of the flight. F/CTRL ALTN LAW was next. There is no follow-up for this. I believe the next was F/CTRL ELAC 1 PITCH FAULT and F/CTRL ELAC 2 PITCH FAULT. I know that's the checklist I went to, but after the fact I can't say whether it was the ELAC PITCH FAULT or a generic ELAC FAULT. The pitch fault follow-up prohibits resetting an ELAC in flight, and so we did not attempt a computer reset. The ELAC FAULT reset procedure does allow a reset in flight if you do it one at a time and there are no uncommanded maneuvers happening.After follow-ups, I called Dispatch to see if we could go to ZZZ2 or ZZZ3 or ZZZ4. We had around 12,000 lb. of fuel. ZZZ4 appeared to have storm cells at the field but ZZZ2 and ZZZ3 were clear. I expressed that we were hand-flying with degraded flight controls and needed a clear airport. Dispatch gave us a minimum fuel of 8.0 to go to ZZZ4 if we could not get into ZZZ. Ultimately, ZZZ updated us that winds were shifting straight out of the north, and they could turn the airport around for us. The non-normal section of the [manual] confirmed we had enough runway landing distance. The final for XXR was clear of storms, and we were able to shoot the ILS and land.So many factors were at play here. At the time we entered ZZZ airspace we had been on duty for 10 hours. We were navigating around severe weather in a tropical storm with the old-style radar. It was working well, but overall, it just doesn't provide the clarity that the new radar presents. We were in continuous turbulence of varying intensities, occasionally severe, before our first approach. Our picture of what the weather was doing was not as clear as the situational awareness that can be achieved in cruise flight. We were relying on ATC vectors through areas that other aircraft had just previously flown. Reports from other aircraft indicated acceptable conditions, or conditions that we could plan for by following severe weather avoidance guidance. The predictive windshear system did its job the first time and we had a clean go-around. Other aircraft landed after our missed approach and conditions were ok. Startle factor played a huge role on the second attempt. Windshear plus multiple ECAMs and autopilot failure created a complex and dynamic situation. We were getting bounced all over the place. We were struggling to get the airplane under control. The monitoring challenge was huge because of the turbulence and the degraded flight controls. I was making FCU inputs in between running checklist items. I had sent an ACARS to Dispatch asking where we should go and listing our system failures, and got no response. The crew phone app worked well enough, but I was frustrated that the feedback we got was that it would be preferred that we hold for ZZZ or go to our listed alternate. I'm sure the Dispatcher could hear my frustration. I am glad that we were able to safely continue to ZZZ, but I wanted to get the plane on the ground without dealing with any more threats to the flight. Holding between thunderstorms with no autopilot in alternate law is an unnecessary risk. I'm not sure why the ground stop for ZZZ was lifted. After the flight, we learned that there had been 7 confirmed tornadoes in the area right around ZZZ. These storms were moving very quickly. It looked like it was possible that ZZZ would clear up before we got there, but there was a good chance it would not. Our decision to go was based on having a lot of fuel, and two alternates. Our second alternate was ZZZ3, which remained clear. This meant that if at any point the path ahead looked bad, we could turn around and have an escape plan. The weather moving in from the south across the final ended up being the worst that we would encounter. If we had delayed this flight by another hour, I think we would have been better off.		On first approach, got a windshear warning and went around. On second approach with moderate to almost severe turbulence, we hit a heavy rain shower. From what I remember we were configured for landing when we hit turbulence. Right then from what I can remember, the autopilot kicked off with the nose pitching up and the speed tape coming up. I pushed the power to climb and tried pushing the nose down. At first, nothing was happening. We did get a low-energy warning too. The plane did recover and started to respond to control inputs. On the ECAM we had F/CTRL ALTN LAW [and] ELAC 1&2 PITCH FAULT. I was flying, the FO (First Officer) did the ECAM and follow-ups and communication. No resets in the air so we looked at going somewhere I could shoot a visual approach. ZZZ Approach had us flying a box pattern until we figured out what we wanted to do. ZZZ Approach then gave us the option to land on XXR and visibility was adequate for a visual. We made an uneventful landing in normal law and gusty crosswinds. I'm guessing we hit the rain at the wrong time.ATC did a good job vectoring us between. The aircraft in front of us just had heavy rain and a smooth ride. Cells were building very fast. I'm thinking that one of the probes froze up momentarily, giving us the speed tape fluctuations. One thing that bothered me was that we couldn't get a hold of Dispatch right away, even after [communicating our situation] over ACARS. The FO did a great job with all the ECAM and checklist procedures.		A321 flight crew reported windshear, heavy rain, and severe turbulence on approach along with the loss of autopilot systems that resulted in multiple go-arounds before finally safely landing at the destination airport.	
1836972	202109	1201-1800	SEZ.Airport	AZ		5	400			VMC	Turbulence; 10		Daylight	CLR			Recreational / Hobbyist (UAS)	Micro UAS, Multirotor		2	Recreational Operations / Section 44809 (UAS)	None	Recreational / Hobbyist (UAS)		Cruise; Hovering (UAS)	None													N			Micro	Multi-Rotor	VLOS	Y	Manual Control	No Drone Zone		Purchased	Number of UAS 1	Ground Control Station (UAS)		X	Improperly Operated																																				Outdoor / Field Station (UAS)		Recreational / Hobbyist (UAS)	Remote PIC (UAS); Person Manipulating Controls (UAS)	Flight Crew Private; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days (UAS) 1; Flight Crew Total 1100; Flight Crew Total (UAS) 3; Flight Crew Type (UAS) 3		Situational Awareness; Training / Qualification; Human-Machine Interface; Distraction; Confusion			1836972												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Unauthorized Flight Operations (UAS); Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Ground Event / Encounter Loss Of VLOS (UAS); Inflight Event / Encounter Fly Away (UAS); Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Visual Observer (UAS)	In-flight	Aircraft Lost / Unrecoverable (UAS)	Weather; Aircraft; Human Factors	Human Factors	On a typical week-end trip with friends, we wanted a drone shot in a scenic location, so I searched recommended spots and found that Red Rock was a popular spot, confirmed on the map that it was far enough from the airport and that it was far enough from the wilderness area, so we went there. Into the hike we came across a sign indicating that the wilderness area started there, a bit closer than the map seemed to indicate, so we canceled the plan, and I hiked back to the car to put the drone back in the trunk, and we went for the hike again. After the hike, we had such great views that I figured we could still do a panorama shot safely while staying outside the wilderness area. A friend was the designated watcher, and I quickly prepared the drone for a quick shot while our friends waited. I took off and went to a safe altitude of 400 ft., and started heading towards Red Rock mountain to frame the shot properly. Then the phone screen dimmed suddenly and became harder to read, but I could still clearly see that I was still far from the mountain and the wilderness area, so I headed towards it. What I didn't realize is that the phone had been heat-soaked in the car in the Arizona summer sun all afternoon, and had stopped updating the screen altogether. So while it displayed a picture of the drone safely at a distance, the drone was actually advancing, so I was surprised and confused when the designated watcher called that the drone was out of sight. We talked, and as I went to show him the video feed, the screen went dark. When I turned the phone back on, the connection with the drone was lost.Given last position and direction, it probably crashed near the summit of the Red Rock mountain, hopefully too high to endanger anyone. But it's a touristic wilderness area, so I checked with a ranger who said it was not a problem, that it happens all the time. I'm still worried that the drone likely crashed in a wilderness area, and really hoping it didn't endanger anyone. It's very frustrating as I was trying to do things safely and legally. I should have realized faster that I had lost video feedback and was flying blind. Instead I allowed myself to be distracted by the dimming of the screen. Next time I will pay attention to the heat of course, and should something confusing happen, I will come back and land to sort it out.	Reporter did not provide any additional information.			Recreational UAS pilot had a fly away when the wireless phone controller became overheated and failed.	
1837663	202109	0001-0600	ZZZZ.Airport	FO			0			IMC	Turbulence		Night			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B767-300 and 300 ER			Part 121	IFR	Cargo / Freight / Delivery		Takeoff / Launch	Vectors	Class B ZZZZ																								Main Gear Wheel		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Maintenance		1837663	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Maintenance		1837665	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Ground Event / Encounter Person / Animal / Bird					Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Rejected Takeoff; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Environment - Non Weather Related; Aircraft; Human Factors	Environment - Non Weather Related	On takeoff roll ZZZ-ZZZ1 at (XA05L), we went through a large flock of birds. I observed numerous birds illuminated by the landing lights passing by both sides of the aircraft. We simultaneously heard several thumps as birds impacted the aircraft nose area. Considering the quantity of birds and impacts, I expeditiously decided to reject the takeoff in the interest of safety; concerned about the possibility of bird ingestion by the engines. Windshield wipers were on high in moderate rain with somewhat reduced visibility.  Estimated speed at the time of the event was between 105-120 knots. We applied the rejected takeoff procedure, stopping with approximately 2,500 feet remaining; notified ATC, and initiated the rejected takeoff QRH checklist. Initially we expected more moderate brake temperatures, and did a 180 turn at tower's direction, expecting that we would later be able to taxi clear. We stopped, completed the Recommended Brake Cooling Schedule charts in the QRH Performance Inflight section, and monitored the Brake Temperature Monitoring System (BTMS) display. As the temperatures increased and we saw brake temp values increasing above 4-5, a decision was made to remain in place and request fire trucks, which were dispatched to our location, arriving after several minutes. In hindsight, remaining stopped straight ahead may have been the best option, however the turn did enable the fire trucks to approach us from the front, which aided in coordination, and possibly safety. The BTMS indications peaked at 7 on most brakes, 8 on two, and one eventually reached 9. The fire trucks monitored us for a time, seeing no indication of fire or smoke, but eventually informed us that we had 2 flat tires, one on each truck. At this point I shut down both engines and we ran the after landing checklist. We informed [the company] and the duty officer what had occurred, as well as coordinating through tower control, who was helpful at relaying information. The fire trucks continued to monitor us as the brakes cooled to the normal range and we coordinated with maintenance to be towed back to parking. Once the brakes had cooled, and the tug crew was on scene, the fire trucks were released, and maintenance towed us slowly to parking IAW their procedures. Maintenance inspections later found bloodstains on the leading edge slats #2 and #9. I wish to point out that the First officer performed in an exemplary fashion, providing extremely helpful and timely inputs and suggestions; his excellent CRM skills undoubtedly contributed to the safe outcome for this event. Thank you for your time. Multiple Bird Strikes on Takeoff Roll, leading to a rejected takeoff.Bird control at ZZZ, bird strike events are frequent		At (XA05 L) we were cleared for takeoff from runway XX. It was a rainy night after a typhoon had passed through, and although the wind was calm, we needed our wipers on for the rain. We were accelerating normally, I called 80 knots and the captain acknowledged. A few seconds later when my eyes were scanning the engine instruments I heard several thumps against the fuselage and heard the captain say 'Birds.' He later told me he said this when he saw many medium-sized white streaks pass through our lights. I saw the captain pull the thrust levers to idle and say 'Reject.' I believe we were about 110 knots (V1 of 143 knots was well above our speed on the speed scale) but I did not notice the exact speed. After a normal RTO procedure, the captain called and I completed the QRH procedure for Rejected Takeoff.  Tower instructed us to make a 180 degree turn on the runway, and after the turn we noticed that the BTMS was climbing more than expected.I calculated for a 120 knots reject at our takeoff weight (345,000 lbs) we should see a BTMS Indication of 5. When the BTMS indication on several brakes increased above 5, I called Tower and requested the fire services to come check for fire and smoke from the brakes. After the fire services arrived and told us (via Tower because fire services did not have an aviation frequencies radio) there was no smoke or fire, we saw one wheel (#2) indicate BTMS 9, and two wheels (#3 and #4) indicate BTMS 8, so asked fire services if any tires were flat. They told us that one wheel on the left side was flat, and at that point the captain decided to start the APU and shut off the engines as it was clear that we would not be taxiing back. I tried calling Company ramp/maintenance on the frequency on the chart but we got no answer, so we asked Tower to relay information to our maintenance and request assistance. Fire services then relayed to us that a tire on the right side was also flat. Maintenance arrived around time XA50 and plugged in to the intercom to let us know they would inspect and decide on a course of action. At XB25 with BTMS in the range of 1-2, they started slowly towing us back to the ramp. We exited the runway at around XC05 and set the parking brake on the ramp at XC25 Z.Multiple Bird strikes on takeoff roll causing a rejected takeoffAirport authority should be better at controlling the birds at ZZZ airport		Flight Crew reported multiple bird strikes and rejected the take off causing 2 main wheel fuse plugs to melt and deflate the tires, requiring a tow back to the ramp.	
1837918	202109	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				33000		IMC	Turbulence; Windshear; 0		Daylight			Center ZZZ	Personal	Cessna Citation Mustang (C510)		1	Part 91	IFR	Personal		Descent																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Personal	Pilot Flying; Single Pilot	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Private; Other Rotorcraft Helicopter	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 30; Flight Crew Total 4000; Flight Crew Type 800		Workload; Time Pressure			1837918												Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control	Horizontal 90		Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Weather; Human Factors	Weather	At approximately XA:10, I encountered severe turbulence/wind shear and the autopilot disengaged as I was beginning a descent from 33000 ft. to ZZZZZ intersection on the ZZZZZ1 arrival into ZZZ1. I temporarily lost control [of] the aircraft as it began a hard, left turn of almost 90 degrees before I was able to regain control and return to my assigned route. On board radar showed no precipitation ahead at the time.				C510 pilot reported a temporary loss of aircraft control due to a severe turbulence/wind shear encounter during initial descent.	
1841181	202109	1801-2400	ZOB.ARTCC	OH						Mixed	Turbulence; 10		Daylight				Air Carrier	Balloon		1	Part 91	VFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class E ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Contracted Service	Pilot Flying; Single Pilot	Flight Crew Commercial	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 24; Flight Crew Total 826; Flight Crew Type 826		Time Pressure; Situational Awareness; Distraction; Confusion; Workload			1841181												Ground Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Human Factors; Weather	Weather	Aircraft X VFR Flight Operations Sight Seeing Hot Air Balloon.Approximately 15 minutes into the flight, I descended near the [the] River upstream of the Falls at [the] State Park. A low-level inversion over the river caused the balloon to rise slightly. Shortly after a wind rotor pushed the balloon down over the edge of the falls and back into the cascading water. The mechanical turbulence of this wind was stronger than I have ever experienced. I operated the burners to arrest the descent, gain altitude and ascend out of the gorge. The balloon landed safely 2 miles from the waterfalls. There were no injuries to passengers or pilot and no damage to the aircraft.Since this incident I have spent days reflecting on what occurred, the seriousness of this event, and what I can do differently in the future so that this never happens again. I've reached out to my colleagues for peer pilot review to process this self-critique. I've reviewed the FAA Balloon Flying Handbook. My research and reflection have led me to develop a corrective procedure and make immediate changes to my flight operations. Mechanical wind turbulence- resulting from wind flowing over irregular terrain or obstructions. When the air near the surface of the Earth flows over obstructions, such as irregular terrain, (bluffs, hills, mountains) and buildings, the normal horizontal wind flow is disturbed. As a result, it is transformed into eddies or other irregular air movements. The strength and magnitude of mechanical turbulence depends on: the speed of the wind, the nature of the obstruction, the stability of the air, and the angle at which the wind moves over the obstacle. As the air flows down the leeward side of the mountain, the air follows the contour of the terrain and is increasingly turbulent. This tends to push an aircraft into the side of a mountain. The stronger the wind, the greater the downward pressure and turbulence become. Due to the effect terrain has on the wind in valleys or canyons, downdrafts may be severe.	I flew the balloon too close to an irregular terrain feature, canyon, river and waterfalls where Mechanical Wind Turbulence can be present and at varying levels of severity. I will no longer conduct low level waterfall passages as part of my flight operations. Instead I will approach them at no lower than half the height of the irregular terrain similar to flying over a mountain in a balloon. For example: The Falls in [the] State Park is 100 ft. tall. My flight procedure will be to fly no lower than 50 ft. above the river and falls below. This will insure safe altitude from the effects of mechanical wind turbulence on a hot air balloon.				A Hot Air Balloon pilot reported unexpected wind currents forced the balloon to descend into a waterfall and river but they were able to regain control and climb away to a safe landing area.	
1841860	202109	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US	090	2	100			VMC	Turbulence		Night			Tower ZZZ	Air Taxi	MBB-BK 117 All Series			Part 135	None	Ambulance		Parked	Visual Approach																													Tower ZZZ	Air Taxi	EC135		1	Part 135	None	Ambulance		Final Approach	Visual Approach	Class E ZZZ																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Taxi	Pilot Flying; Single Pilot	Flight Crew Commercial; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 100; Flight Crew Total 3500; Flight Crew Type 3375		Situational Awareness; Communication Breakdown; Time Pressure	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Ground Personnel		1841860												Conflict Ground Conflict, Critical	Horizontal 900; Vertical 75		Person Flight Crew	Pre-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Procedure; Human Factors	Ambiguous	I was the PIC of the BK117, shutdown on top of the ZZZ helipad. I began my overnight shift at XA:00 that evening. After delivering a patient to the hospital, I was awaiting the medical crew's return from delivering the patient to the hospital. Two of the medical crew had returned to the helicopter to configure the medical equipment for the return flight to our home base of ZZZ1. As I was waiting for the crew to finish, I noticed an approaching helicopter from the southeast direction. It has been confirmed this helicopter is operated by Company X. I checked my company pilot phone to see if I had missed a call from our dispatch/communications (comm) center alerting me to an incoming Air Ambulance. This is common practice amongst the dispatch/communication centers of Air Ambulance companies in order to alert pilots of incoming aircraft in order to vacate the helipad and reposition to either an airborne holding area or to a ground holding area at ZZZ2. I did not have a missed call so I called [my company] comm center to inquire about the helicopter approaching and now in a downwind leg (south to north) to the helipad. [They] had no knowledge of the approaching helicopter.This is the first breakdown in the chain of events and the primary contributing factor. The Company X dispatch failed to alert [my] communications center of the inbound aircraft to the ZZZ Helipad.Additional contributing factor: a helipad camera exists at the hospital entrance doors adjacent to the helipad always focused on the helipad. Typically, ZZZ hospital Security personnel monitor the helipad camera when helicopters are inbound and/or shutdown on the helipad. Reportedly, the ZZZ Hospital Security team was not alerted to the inbound H135 Air Ambulance.During my phone call to [my company] comm center, the H135 continued it's approach from downwind to base leg and to final approach. It became quite apparent that the PIC did not see the helicopter shutdown on the pad. As the helicopter continued the approach, I began to quickly move from the helipad to the catwalk connecting the pad to the hospital and yelled at the two medical crew-members to get off the helipad. The H135 continued it's final approach for landing as the medical crew also quickly vacated the helipad. [My company] comm center phone calls are recorded, without exception for this phone call. The urgency in my voice alerting the medical crew can clearly be heard on the recording as I believed our safety was compromised.The H135 began a wave-off maneuver at approximately 300 meters north of the helipad and 50-75 above helipad elevation (100 feet AGL). As the H135 continued it's wave-off, the PIC side-stepped to the east to avoid low overflight of my shutdown helicopter on the helipad. The H135 PIC continued the wave-off over the adjacent hospital building and established an airborne holding area to the southeast.It is unknown if the H135 PIC was aided with Night Vision Goggles (NVGs) at this point in the evening. Official sunset had occurred at approximately XA:15 that evening and end of evening civil or nautical twilight had not yet occurred. I also do not know the typical procedures for this specific PIC or operator concerning NVG practices flying into/out of metropolitan areas. In my experience, I remain aided when landing to metropolitan hospital helipads as an aid to identifying hazards that I may not be able to visually acquire unaided.An additional unknown element is whether or not ZZZ2 ATC tower advised the H135 PIC of the presence of a helicopter shutdown on the ZZZ Hospital helipad. In my experience, the ZZZ2 Tower frequently advises me if a helicopter is on the helipad after my initial Tower check-in. I do know, however, that there was communication between the H135 PIC and the ZZZ2 Tower at some point during the incident. Upon powering on my avionics during my start sequence, I overheard the H135 PIC communicating with the ZZZ2 Tower of my presence on the helipad.Finally, though this appears to be a communication breakdown in normal operating procedures, I believe this incident could have been avoided even with the multiple occurrences of communication breakdown if the helipad had Pilot Controlled Lighting (PCL) available for incoming Air Ambulance Helicopters. Currently, the lighting system is only controlled by a rheostat (timing only, not intensity) from inside the hospital on the helipad floor leading to the helipad.				Air ambulance helicopter pilot reported that prior to engine start and while waiting to depart the hospital helipad, a critical ground conflict occurred when another helicopter attempted to land at the same location. The reporter cited, as contributing, that dispatch had not notified the crew of the inbound helicopter	
1842805	202108	1201-1800			001	38	450			VMC	Turbulence; Windshear; 7			2400			Personal	Cessna 180 Skywagon		1	Part 91	VFR	Personal		Takeoff / Launch		Class E ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Personal	Captain; Single Pilot; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 250; Flight Crew Total 16500; Flight Crew Type 1000		Distraction; Workload			1842805												Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Ground Event / Encounter Ground Strike - Aircraft; Ground Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Aircraft Damaged; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Weather	Weather	While taking of from a 900 foot gravel bar a wind shear/ down draft was encountered. The event forced the aircraft back down and damaged the aircraft.				A pilot departing from a '900 foot gravel bar' reported a wind shear encounter forced the aircraft back down resulting in damage to the aircraft.	
1843128	202109	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US				10000		IMC	Turbulence					TRACON ZZZ1	Air Carrier	A319		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																								Rudder Trim System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 125; Flight Crew Total 226; Flight Crew Type 226		Workload; Situational Awareness; Communication Breakdown; Distraction	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1843128	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 65; Flight Crew Total 93; Flight Crew Type 93		Workload; Situational Awareness; Distraction			1843131	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Weather	Weather	Aircraft X experienced an upset aircraft state as a result of severe turbulence encountered on approach to ZZZ Runway XXR. The aircraft pitched to approximately 20 degrees nose up and 10 to 15 degrees left bank, disengaging the autopilot and causing the autothrust to increase power toward max. The airspeed decayed from 250 KIAS to 220 KIAS as the aircraft climbed from 10,000 ft. to 10,900 ft. before upset recovery procedures returned it to stable flight at 10,000 ft. and 250 KIAS. There were no injuries as passengers and flight attendants were seated early in anticipation of turbulence. There was no aircraft damage. Aircraft X made an uneventful landing on Runway XXR. The severe turbulence encounter was reported to Air Traffic Control and the Company. A full debrief was conducted with Flight Operations.Prior to descending into ZZZ, the First Officer (FO) and I discussed concerns about thunderstorms and convective activity in the terminal area. We had plenty of gas and I noted that ZZZ1 weather was excellent. I briefed the flight attendants to be seated approximately 20 minutes before touchdown. I then confirmed that they were seated. I made a PA to the passengers that I expected very bumpy conditions and I emphasized the need for them to obey the Fasten Seat Belt sign. The FO and I briefed windshear precautions, indications, and procedures while inbound to ZZZ2 for the ZZZZZ [arrival]. We did not re-brief the Upset Recovery procedures as they had been briefed prior to departure. (In retrospect, I think this could be good to review when windshear concerns are expressed as the windshear could result in an upset.) We were initially cleared on the ZZZZZ arrival. We loaded the Runway XXL ILS approach. Shortly into the descent, we had an AUTOFLT RUD TRIM 1 FAULT ECAM. I wrote it up as we were descending. Shortly after that, ATC vectored us off the arrival. ATC did not tell us what we should expect, but I heard several aircraft ahead of us receiving clearances to ZZZZZ1 for what I assumed would be the remainder of the ZZZZZ arrival. Radio communication congestion was significant. I sensed ATC was struggling to manage the number of arrivals from the north. I did not hear anyone receiving holding clearances. Holding might have alleviated the density of traffic and eased the friction for ATC. Eventually, we were told to do a 360 degree turn. Out of the turn, we were cleared to descend to 10,000 ft and given a heading of 190deg. I had heard another aircraft given a vector toward the ZZZZZ2 intersection, so I put it on the fix page for situational awareness. At that time, I was watching the radar which showed a cell next to the ZZZZZ2 intersection and another cell to the southwest. The two aircraft ahead of us were vectored through that gap. A 777 was behind us. At that time, we still did not know what arrival or approach clearance to expect. As we passed through the gap between the two cells, ATC said to advise when we could take a turn direct to ZZZZZ3. We did not have ZZZZZ3 loaded into the FMC. The FO did not know what ZZZZZ3 was. I asked him to load it and I spelled it out. (We still had Runway XXL loaded.) As we tried to get ZZZZZ3 loaded, along with the remainder of the ILS XXR approach, while still in the gap between the two cells, we experienced the severe turbulence and upset. After recovering the aircraft, I gave direction to the FO to load DIRECT ZZZZZ3 and I spelled it again. He did so, and we turned direct to the fix. He then connected the rest of the approach and it was flown uneventfully.As far as the execution of the Upset Recovery procedure, I did not comply with all the procedures. I also did not verbalize the steps and I did not silence the autopilot aural which resulted from the disconnect for quite some time. I had trouble focusing on the instruments and the 'Startle Effect' was significant.		Descending into ZZZ on the ZZZZZ arrival, we were taken off the arrival. I am assuming it was because of the convective weather in the area. We had flown in from the north and were being vectored west away from ZZZ. It seemed ATC was overwhelmed with all of the aircraft and bad weather in the area. Because we knew there was adverse weather around the airport the Captain sat the passengers and flight attendants well in advance. At one point while being vectored west we were told to fly multiple different headings which eventually made us complete a giant 360 degree turn. At the end of the turn I noticed that ATC was vectoring aircraft towards ZZZZZ2 intersection which seemed like ATC had found a gap they were funneling aircraft through. I pulled up ZZZZZ2 intersection on the fix page so we could see we where it was in relation to our position on the nav display. ATC gave us a heading of 190 that was towards ZZZZZ2 intersection. At this point ATC did not tell us which runway or routing to expect. While flying on a heading of 190 and 250 kts. at 10,000 ft. we flew into a cloud and at that point we experienced an upset event of severe turbulence. The aircraft gained about 900 ft., dropped 30 kts., banked 10-15 degrees and disengaged the autopilot. The Captain was flying and immediately returned us to straight and level flight. The Captain reported the incident to ATC and I checked in with the Flight Attendants and no injuries occurred in the cabin. After the event ATC told us to go to ZZZZZ3 intersection and to expect Runway XXR. We landed the airplane and contacted the company about the event.		Flight Crew reported severe turbulence caused a temporary loss of aircraft control.	
1845466	202110	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US	270	5		10500		VMC	Turbulence; 10		Daylight	10000		UNICOM ZZZ	Personal	Bonanza 35		1	Part 91	None	Personal		Climb	None	Class E ZZZ1; Class G ZZZ1																								Fuel Selector		X	Design; Improperly Operated																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Personal	Pilot Flying; Single Pilot	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Flight Instructor	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 240; Flight Crew Total 11498; Flight Crew Type 35		Training / Qualification; Confusion			1845466												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Fuel Issue; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition	Aircraft; Human Factors	Human Factors	On a pleasure flight around the area. I flew from my home base ZZZ1 to the area of ZZZ. During the flight I was burning fuel from the LEFT main tank per the normal fuel management procedure. As all fuel is returned to the LEFT tank on my airplane. As the flight progressed, I burned fuel first from the LEFT tank, then from the RIGHT tank back to the left tank the to the AUX tank. At this point I had encounter some pretty heavy moderate turbulence and as I was attempting to select the fuel selector back to the LEFT tank I inadvertently selected the RIGHT tank from the AUX position. On my aircraft the location of the fuel selector is below my left leg making it very difficult to see. My aircraft also is equipped with only one fuel gauge and a switch to toggle between left and right tank. As I made my way around ZZZ airport and was turning to return to ZZZ1, the engine quit. At this point I turned to return to ZZZ airport and ran through the engine failure procedure. I verified the fuel in the left tank and thinking I had the left tank selected from before, I did not reach down to verify it by hand. Had I done that I would have caught my error. I was near the airport at this point, so I devoted my attention to safely returning to the airport, rather than further troubleshoot my failure. I made a radio broadcast on the UNICOM frequency and landed on Runway XX. The landing was uneventful and I was able to rollout and clear the runway. Ground support personnel met the aircraft and towed me to a parking area by the FBO. As I climbed out of the aircraft I looked down and the Fuel selector and realized my error. A visual inspection of the right fuel tank confirmed it, I had run the tank dry. After refilling the right tank and selecting the left tank the engine fired right up.The cause of the incident was not verifying the correct fuel tank per the fuel management schedule.Contributing factors:Pilot proficiency in aircraft type. Although I fly a lot it is not in this specific aircraft. More proficiency would have made me more mindful of the unique fuel management schedule of this aircraft, and made me more familiar with the fuel selector by feel rather than visually. Which is more difficult based on its location.Another contributing factor is the fuel gauge design of the aircraft. Having two gauges installed would draw attention to an empty tank and trigger a verification of the fuel selector.Environmental factors, bouncing around in the turbulence made the error easier by causing the distraction during the fuel selection process.This incident has driven home the importance of staying proficient in the different aircraft that I intend to fly regularly. It has also driven a commitment to upgrade some systems and displays in my aircraft.				BE-35 pilot reported the engine quit after having selected the wrong fuel tank during scheduled fuel management procedure.  The pilot decided to successfully divert and land at the nearest airport rather than troubleshoot in the air.	
1845822	202110	0601-1200	ZJX.ARTCC	FL						Marginal	Thunderstorm; Turbulence					Center ZJX	Other ALL	Any Unknown or Unlisted Aircraft Manufacturer			Other ALL	IFR	Other ALL		Climb; Cruise; Descent	Oceanic; Vectors	Class A ZJX																																																															Facility ZJX.ARTCC		Government	Instructor; Enroute	Air Traffic Control Fully Certified	Air Traffic Control Time Certified In Pos 1 (yrs) 15		Distraction; Workload; Confusion; Time Pressure			1845822	Facility ZJX.ARTCC		Government	Enroute	Air Traffic Control Fully Certified	Air Traffic Control Time Certified In Pos 1 (mon) 10		Situational Awareness; Time Pressure; Workload; Distraction; Confusion; Communication Breakdown	Party1 ATC; Party2 ATC		1844686	ATC Issue All Types; Airspace Violation All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Separated Traffic; Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance	Weather; Procedure; Human Factors; Company Policy; Airspace Structure	Company Policy	I was working many positions this session: RXX/TXX/DXX/DXY/RXY The volume of traffic became so intense we had to stop taking hand offs from ZTL. In turn they stopped taking hand offs from us. I'm sure there were many aircraft north and southbound that hit other airspace without a proper point out or a hand off. It was so complex that even with as many people as we had over there to handle the situation it could not be accomplished. Command Center denied all of our requests for help to slow down the traffic so we could do our job properly.Give us help from the Command Center when everyone in the country has to go around the weather into one hole between the military exercises and airspace. There is simply too much volume for one or even two controllers to handle. Maybe an ultra-high could have helped with the frequency congestion.		At XA:10am local I came into work and saw a large weather system extending from just east of SZW toward CHS, causing aircraft to go into the Warning Areas over the Atlantic. We were advised the military was recalling airspace (which we have no power to deny) and because of (Military Training Exercise) no aircraft would be permitted to deviate into the airspace once activated. We told our supervisors and Traffic Management Unit if this were the case, all southbound aircraft that were not able to fly the Atlantic routes would have to be routed something similar to CAE..DBN..VNA..SZW. This would cause all southbound traffic to be routed through two sectors XX/XY which do not have an ultra high and are FL240-600. When what we predicted started to unfold, we were told Command Center thought what we needed was too restrictive. XX/XY were already red sectors, it what they didn't know is roughly 85% of our traffic were on bad routes and were going to be added to the already unsafe numbers at XX/XY. This resulted in chain reaction no notice holds. After complaining for hours, we were told ZTL would not issue reroutes. This was by and far one of the most unsafe situations I had witnessed. Additionally, I overheard that Command Center was pressuring us to come out of ground stops while sectors were holding aircraft.Command Center constantly limits our ability to manage traffic and results in extremely unsafe situations. I have been told several Traffic Management Coordinators that have went to Command Center from ZJX go to terminal areas, thus wasting the knowledge they possess about our complex military/funnel/weather structure. It was clear that yesterday safety was not a priority in the National Airspace System and the Command Center introduced risk into the system by trying to overload the system and pressuring it to break.		Two Jacksonville Center Controllers reported FAA Command Center would not comply with their request for traffic flow restrictions for known weather, causing operational challenges.	
1847276	202110		ZZZ.Airport	US							Turbulence; 10			12000			Personal	Small Aircraft, Low Wing, 1 Eng, Retractable Gear		2	Part 91	None	Personal		Landing	Visual Approach	Class G ZZZ																								Landing Gear		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Personal	Instructor	Flight Crew Flight Instructor; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 25; Flight Crew Total 2360; Flight Crew Type 75		Training / Qualification; Situational Awareness; Communication Breakdown; Human-Machine Interface	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1847276												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Ground Event / Encounter Ground Strike - Aircraft			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Aircraft Damaged; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft; Human Factors	Human Factors	I was contacted by a pilot [who] needed a Flight Review by the end of [the month]. He owns 2 aircraft. We were going to fly his Aircraft X. I arrived around XAam and we conducted a ground discussion. We agreed on what type of air work he wanted to do. The plan was for slow flight, ground reference and short, soft and crosswind operations. His medical is current, and his flight review is valid until the end of [the month]. The aircraft was fresh from annual and was properly signed off in maintenance log. [Pilot] was alert and coherent. Admittedly, I was semi-awestruck by his status and flying experience. I told him I was not going to charge him for the flight review as I thought it was an honor for me. Take off was normal, although it was gusty with thunderstorms forecast for late afternoon. I was a little uncomfortable with the aircraft age (1950's) although it is immaculate, well maintained and had many recent avionics upgrades. I lack experience and familiarity with this aircraft. It has lap belts only and a throw-over yoke which he familiarized me with. I internally questioned if I would have the presence of mind to bring the yoke to my side safely in an emergency. Also the layout of the panel and associated controls was unfamiliar to me. Additionally, this was my first time at this landing strip, a grass strip with wires and trees in proximity. As we flew we noticed the stall warning horn was not functioning. He said he had the stall horn and gear 'serviced' at the last annual and was disappointed that it was not working. He did not elaborate and I have limited knowledge of the finer points of this aircraft systems. At no time did I handle controls, manipulate any systems or communicate on the radio. I also was having intermittent issues with my headset connection during the flight. It seemed like a poor connection. As we approached the field to land the wind was a gusty left crosswind at about 15 kts. There is a power line on the approach end of the runway with one wire section underground closest to the approach path. An adjacent property had a large [gathering of people]. I distinctly recall having to move my left leg so the pilot could lower the gear. I was focused on the power line and the crosswind and don't recall if there were 3 green lights on gear position. It was gusty and I knew that with the throw over yoke, I was just a passenger and I don't like that feeling as I am a control freak. The plane seemed close to the ground and then the prop kicked up dirt, the pilot yelled 'the gear didn't go down '. We slid on the grass strip and came to rest upright. He secured the power and we exited the plane. Neither of us were injured and nothing on the ground was contacted. No leaks from the aircraft were noted. I looked in the cockpit and noted the gear selector was in the down position. The prop was bent on both tips. The pilot's neighbors showed up quickly and a piece of heavy equipment was used with straps to lift the plane up. The main gear was extended and the nose doors were bent, but manually pulled open and the nose gear was extended and locked. I am not an A&P but I did not even see much scratching of the under belly of the aircraft. Other than the dents on nose wheel gear doors, the rolled prop tips, and some antennas dislodged from the belly, I did not see and he did not mention any other damage. The plane was towed back to the hangar with a golf cart. The pilot was upset and not in a good mood after the incident. I felt like crap about the entire event. I don't see me flying again in a general aircraft where I don't have controls available to me all the time. It would not have prevented this most likely, but I would not be worried about what happen if I needed to take controls.				Flight Instructor reported a gear-up landing while conducting a flight review in an unfamiliar aircraft at an unfamiliar location.	
1847286	202110	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			0			VMC	Turbulence; 10		Daylight	10000		Tower ZZZ	FBO	Light Sport Aircraft		1	Part 91	VFR	Training		Landing	Direct																																																																Aircraft X	Flight Deck	FBO	Pilot Flying; Single Pilot	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Private	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 19.4; Flight Crew Total 159.4; Flight Crew Type 159.4		Situational Awareness			1847286												Ground Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Aircraft Damaged	Aircraft; Human Factors; Weather	Human Factors	The PIAT is a small lightweight aircraft. When landing at Runway XX at ZZZ the front tire blew. The aircraft had landed on the runway and looked as if everything was normal but then the aircraft became airborne again. This could've been due to a gust of wind that lifted the aircraft. It could've been the fact that Runway XX starts off as a flat runway and then slopes downward. Either way if you are in an aircraft that likes to glide recognize the risk for becoming airborne again. It didn't take much to blow the front tire, I was airborne for maybe 1/2 a second or less and then made contact with the runway again. The PIAT aircraft does not have individual brakes on the main tires (unlike a Cessna 172), it has one brake lever that operates the brakes in unison. If for some reason your nose wheel blows you should anticipate uncontrollable turn. I used full right rudder and applied full brakes to avoid going off the runway, but we still drifted left. At the same time of sliding down the runway I shut off mags to stop the propeller from spinning (minimizing risk and moving parts). I did this because I knew that the nose was low to the ground thus making the ground clearance on the propeller low. This all happened within 1/2 a second, meaning time to go-around was not an option. If you are in an aircraft that is a natural glider be careful on landings not to become airborne again. If you do become airborne again and can recognize it fast going around would be your best choice.				PIAT (Light Sport Aircraft) pilot reported the aircraft became airborne for 1/2 a second after touchdown due to possible gusts resulting in a blown front tire, loss of aircraft control, and a near runway excursion.	
1847887	202109	1201-1800	ZZZ.TRACON	US							Turbulence		Daylight			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	EMB ERJ 170/175 ER/LR		2	Part 121	IFR			Initial Approach																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument			Situational Awareness			1847887												Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach			Automation Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Flight Crew Returned To Clearance; Flight Crew Became Reoriented	Human Factors	Human Factors	Per usual cleared for the Instrument Approach and flying the visual profile as normal. Prior to XXXXX ATC requested we make a short approach. As we made the turn towards the runway ATC mentioned a low altitude alert and verified we had the current altimeter. The PF (Pilot Flying) arrested the descent and leveled off momentarily. After realizing our altimeter was showing the current setting we opted to continue the approach and landing as we both believed it would be accomplished safely. The momentary level off had placed us slightly high over the landmark. The PF corrected the descent profile and we landed without incident.If unsure about a procedure that ATC is requesting to mention 'unable'. I think this would prevent the second guess of if we had done something wrong.				EMB-170 First Officer reported during a short approach to landing that ATC issued a low altitude alert, resulting in a momentary level off and being slightly high on approach. The pilot flying corrected and continued to a landing.	
1850099	202110	0601-1200	CHA.TRACON	TN				3600		IMC	Turbulence; 6		Daylight	3100		TRACON CHA	Air Taxi	Light Transport		2	Part 135	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach	Vectors	Class C CHA																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Corporate	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 30; Flight Crew Total 3300; Flight Crew Type 6		Communication Breakdown; Situational Awareness; Training / Qualification	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1850099	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Corporate	Check Pilot; Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 150; Flight Crew Total 10500; Flight Crew Type 3500		Distraction; Situational Awareness; Training / Qualification; Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1850132	Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT			Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Air Traffic Control Issued Advisory / Alert; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Human Factors	Human Factors	During descent to CHA from 11,000, expecting the visual approach Runway 20. ATIS ceilings were SCT 3,100 and Broken 4,500. ATC said descend maintain 3,600, left heading 110, altimeter 29.90 advise airport in sight. I read back altimeter 29.90, left 110, altitudes 3,600. When selecting our given instructions, I was going to adjust our altimeter first. Captain told me to select altitude to 3,100 first. I selected and announced '3,100 Altitude Set'. Captain acknowledged and we proceeded our descent. Upon 3,300 feet, ATC announce terrain alert climb 3,600. Captain disengaged autopilot and climb. I read back instructions and selected 3,600 altitude. We proceeded to climb back to 3,600 feet.After the event. The Captain called ATC to confirm we were given instructions of 3,100. ATC replied saying he gave us 3,600 feet. During the event, the Captain and I minds were set ATC gave us instructions to descend 3,100.		On our descent into Chattanooga, the flight crew was issued heading of 110 to enter a right base for a visual to Runway 20 and an altitude of 3,600 feet. During that descent the First Officer had some difficulty reading back the instructions and ultimately setting the appropriate altitude. I had set the assigned heading and after waiting for him to set the altitude I instructed the First Officer to set 3,100 feet in the altitude selector which he did. Just prior to that time I had been thinking of what altitude I was anticipating obtaining visual contact with the airport and must have transposed what was the lowest cloud layer at CHA of 3,100 into our assigned altitude. Once we descended below 3,600 feet ATC issued a low altitude alert and instructed us to climb back to 3,600 feet which we did immediately. We were then vectored for the ILS approach to 20. I requested the approach control phone number which I called once we were on the ground to follow up.  ATC verified we accurately read back 3,600.  I thanked them and advised I would file a report.As an instructor pilot, it can be difficult at times to decide to whether to let your trainee attempt to complete a task on their own for the purposes of gaining experience or to intervene and assure the appropriate outcome.  In this instance I tried to let my trainee First Officer attempt the read back the clearance and set the altitude as assigned. The reason I did not intervene was that he had been having some difficulty with these tasks and I wanted him to get as much as experience as possible. I believe that while I waited for him to accomplish these tasks I had become distracted by the delay and unfortunately instructed him incorrectly to set the wrong altitude. A significant contributing factor in this sequence of events is the lack of experience that the First Officer has flying in the IFR environment. I had to spend an extra amount of time monitoring and correcting him during this flight and our previous flights in his communications and IFR procedures as most of his previous experience was as a pilot for [another] business for the last three years in mostly VFR conditions.  That being said, in the future regardless of my desire to give my trainees the added benefit of more experience, I will have to be more diligent in intervening when they are not complying with their tasks in a timely manner and I will also have to better assess the ability or lack of ability that my trainees have and limit when and where I allow them to gain additional experience.		Light Transport aircraft flight crew reported a CFTT event during approach to CHA airport.  The crew selected 3,100 rather than the cleared altitude of 3,600 feet.  ATC announced the terrain alert and the crew took evasive action by climbing.	
